&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Particulars of the nominations received today will be uploaded to the election website (<a href="https://www.elections.gov.hk/ecss2026/eng/index.html" target="_blank">www.elections.gov.hk</a>).]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[Hong Kong Customs seizes suspected counterfeit goods worth over $7.6 million in "Time Shield" operation (with photos)]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/14/P2026101400679.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/14/P2026101400679.htm</link><pubDate>Wed, 14 Oct 2026 19:08:00 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Hong Kong Customs conducted a two-week enforcement operation codenamed &quot;Time Shield&quot; from September 28 to yesterday (October 13) to combat counterfeit watches and related products via cross-boundary transshipment cargo. During the operation, Customs detected 16 related cases and seized around 7 000 items of suspected counterfeit goods, including watches, clothing, mobile phones etc with an estimated market value of over $7.6 million. One person was arrested.<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Through intelligence analysis and detailed investigations, Customs detected related cases at various local logistics companies and express parcel centres.&nbsp;After follow-up investigations, Customs believed that all of the suspected counterfeit goods would have been exported to overseas destinations. Customs officers therefore organised a controlled delivery operation in respect of one batch of those seized items. On October 7, Customs officers arrested a 65-year-old man in an industrial unit in Kwai Chung, and seized about 600 items of suspected counterfeit goods including watches, mobile phones etc with an estimated market value of about $400,000.&nbsp;<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Investigations of the above-mentioned cases are ongoing. The arrested person has been released on bail pending further investigation.<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Customs appeals to consumers to purchase goods at reputable shops or websites to avoid buying counterfeit or infringing goods. Practitioners in the logistics industry should also comply with the requirements of the Trade Descriptions Ordinance (TDO) and to check with the trademark owners or authorised agents if the authenticity of a product is in doubt.<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Customs will continue to step up inspections and conduct intelligence-led enforcement to vigorously combat different types of counterfeit and infringing goods activities.<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Under the TDO, any person who imports or exports or sells or possesses for sale any goods to which a forged trademark is applied commits an offence. The maximum penalty upon conviction is a fine of $500,000 and imprisonment for five years.<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Members of the public may report any suspected counterfeiting activities to Customs&#39; 24-hour hotline 182 8080 or its dedicated crime-reporting email account&nbsp;(<a href="mailto:crimereport@customs.gov.hk" target="_blank">crimereport@customs.gov.hk</a>) or online form (<a href="https://eform.cefs.gov.hk/form/ced002/en/" target="_blank">eform.cefs.gov.hk/form/ced002</a>).<div id="photos"><a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/14/P2026101400679_photo_1341136.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/14/P2026101400679_photo_1341136.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;<a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/14/P2026101400679_photo_1341137.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/14/P2026101400679_photo_1341137.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;<a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/14/P2026101400679_photo_1341138.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/14/P2026101400679_photo_1341138.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;</div>]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[Brussels ETO continues support for Hong Kong films at renowned Sitges International Fantastic Film Festival of Catalonia (with photos)]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/14/P2026101300590.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/14/P2026101300590.htm</link><pubDate>Wed, 14 Oct 2026 18:55:00 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Together with the Cultural and Creative Industries Development Agency under the Culture, Sports and Tourism Bureau of the Hong Kong Special Administrative Region (HKSAR) Government, the Hong Kong Economic and Trade Office in Brussels (Brussels ETO) is continuing the established partnership with the Sitges International Fantastic Film Festival of Catalonia and is supporting the screening of Hong Kong films at the 2026 edition being held from October 8 to 18 (Sitges time) in Sitges, Spain.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; This year, the Festival features five Hong Kong films, namely &quot;The Black Magic with Buddha&quot;, &quot;Cold War 1994&quot;, &quot;Love Massacre&quot;, &quot;Master of the Flying Guillotine&quot; and &quot;The Mage&quot;.<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Brussels ETO hosted a reception on October 12, bringing together some 100 guests from the European film, cultural, media and business sectors. The event was graced by the presence of the producer and cast member of the film &quot;The Mage&quot;, including renowned Hong Kong artist and lead actress of the film Josie Ho, as well as Michael Werner, a veteran of the Hong Kong film industry and an accomplished film producer.<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Delivering a welcoming speech at the reception, Deputy Representative of Brussels ETO Mr Kasper Ng, thanked the organiser for once again having Hong Kong cinema be part of the Festival, and highlighted the HKSAR Government&#39;s commitment to supporting the development of the local film industry as well as the promotion of Hong Kong cinema to global audiences.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; &quot;We are dedicated to helping Hong Kong filmmakers reach audiences around the world. By showcasing both classics and new productions at prestigious European film festivals, such as here in Sitges, we celebrate Hong Kong&#39;s unique charm as an East-meets-West centre for international cultural exchanges, while unlocking new commercial opportunities for our creative industries,&quot; he said.<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; The development of Hong Kong&#39;s film industry is also supported under the First Five-Year Plan for Economic and Social Development of the Hong Kong Special Administrative Region (2026-2030) and the Chief Executive&#39;s 2026 Policy Address, which were recently published. Measures include attracting more private investment and facilitating Mainland and overseas film crews filming in Hong Kong.<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Founded in 1968, the Sitges International Fantastic Film Festival of Catalonia is the world&#39;s leading fantasy film festival. It screens films across the fantastic genre, including action and adventure movies and black comedies, with a special emphasis on thrillers. The last edition attracted an audience of more than 130 000 from around the world.<div id="photos"><a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/14/P2026101300590_photo_1341125.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/14/P2026101300590_photo_1341125.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;<a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/14/P2026101300590_photo_1341126.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/14/P2026101300590_photo_1341126.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;</div>]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[LCQ7: Concern about adolescents' addiction to the Internet]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/14/P2026101400568.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/14/P2026101400568.htm</link><pubDate>Wed, 14 Oct 2026 18:52:00 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;Following is a question by the Hon Judy Chan and a written reply by the Secretary for Health, Professor Lo Chung-mau, in the Legislative Council today (October 14):<br />
<br />
Question:<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;There are views pointing out that with the popularity of social media and online platforms, the psychological and mental health issues of children and adolescents in Hong Kong arising from cyberbullying and excessive addiction to the Internet have drawn increasing concern. In this connection, will the Government inform this Council:<br />
<br />
(1) whether it knows the respective numbers of new cases recorded, cumulative number of persons waiting and average waiting times for the child and adolescent psychiatric services under the Hospital Authority (HA) in each of the past five years; among these cases, of the numbers of cases involving depression, anxiety disorders or self-harm risks caused by cyberbullying or Internet addiction, as well as the year-on-year increases; how the Government assesses the seriousness of the aforementioned situation;<br />
<br />
(2) as the Student Health Service Annual Health Report for 2024/25 School Year published by the Department of Health in March this year pointed out that in the 2024-2025 school year, 2.1 per cent of students reported having been bullied online in the past 12 months, how the Government assesses the correlation or causal relationship between the use of social media and the negative impacts on the emotional health of children and adolescents;<br />
<br />
(3) given that the World Health Organization has regarded cyberbullying as an important social problem, whether the Government has set up dedicated multidisciplinary medical teams (including psychiatrists, clinical psychologists, social workers and school counselling personnel) to provide support for children and adolescents with Internet addiction or trauma from cyberbullying; if so, of the respective numbers of cases referred, assessed and treated under this mechanism, the service coverage rates and the average time taken for handling cases in each of the past five years; if not, the reasons for that;<br />
<br />
(4) whether the Government or HA has provided frontline healthcare personnel with specialised clinical guidelines, assessment tools and continuing professional training for identifying and diagnosing social anxiety and mental distress among adolescents caused by Internet addiction; if so, of the scope and frequency of such training; if not, the reasons for that;<br />
<br />
(5) whether, in promoting public mental health education over the past five years, the Government has formulated dedicated public education and publicity strategies targeting &quot;prevention of Internet addiction among children and adolescents&quot; and &quot;coping with trauma from cyberbullying&quot;; if so, of the contents and partners of the relevant publicity projects, and the number of times they were organised; if not, the reasons for that; and<br />
<br />
(6) to safeguard the well-being of children and adolescents, whether the Government will formulate a long-term cross-departmental plan to prevent the mental health of adolescents from being affected by their addiction to the use of the Internet; if so, of the details; if not, the reasons for that?<br />
<br />
Reply:<br />
<br />
President,<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;In consultation with the Commerce and Economic Development Bureau, Education Bureau (EDB), the Innovation, Technology and Industry Bureau, the Labour and Welfare Bureau (LWB), the Security Bureau, the Department of Justice (DoJ), the Department of Health (DH) and the Hospital Authority (HA), my reply to the various parts of the question raised by the Hon Judy Chan is as follows:<br />
<br />
(2) The health impacts of screen and social media use depend on a combination of factors, including user characteristics, duration of use, type of content, and usage context. Nowadays, young people use digital screens and social media for learning, communication and socialising. Some academic studies have indicated that moderate use of social media can help foster social connections and well-being amongst young people. If social media is used appropriately, it may offer opportunities to enhance digital literacy and promote well-rounded development. However, some local and studies from other regions have also highlighted that the inappropriate use of the internet and social media may expose young people to risks such as privacy intrusion, cybersecurity, online scam and crime, child sexual exploitation, exposure to misinformation or disinformation, and the influence of radical or hate speech. The impact on young people&#39;s development has gone beyond the realm of mere public health or personal well-being.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;Given the complex and multifaceted nature of this issue, safeguarding the online safety of children and young people requires a shared commitment and collaborative efforts across different professional disciplines. Government bureaux and departments, non-governmental organisations (NGOs) and community partners have consistently worked together, adopting a multipronged approach to safeguard the younger generation from potential harm arising from the use of the Internet and social media, whilst taking into account various policy considerations within the ever-evolving digital landscape.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;The Government has studied the use of social media by children and young people, and the associated impacts through statistical surveys and by referring to relevant reports from NGOs. According to the Thematic Household Survey conducted by the Census and Statistics Department, adolescents aged 10 to 14 spent an average of 13.8 hours per week on online social activities in 2024, representing an increase from 8.6 hours per week in 2014. A similar increase was observed in the 15 to 24 age group, with weekly usage rising from 14.0 hours in 2014 to 20.6 hours in 2024. The DH released the &quot;Student Health Service (SHS) Annual Health Report for 2024/25 School Year&quot; in March this year. The health assessment questionnaire (Note 1) revealed that 2.1 per cent of students reported experiencing cyberbullying within the past 12 months during the 2024/25 academic year, representing a slight decrease from 3.0 per cent recorded in the 2023/24 academic year. A local survey conducted in 2020 found that students who used social media for three hours or more per day exhibited significantly higher levels of depression, anxiety, and stress compared to those who used social media for less than three hours per day.<br />
&nbsp;<br />
(3) and (4) In response to the above situation, to protect children and young people from potential harm arising from using the internet and social media, various Government bureaux and departments, NGOs and community partners have taken a series of measures to strengthen relevant services and referrals, as well as public education (as detailed in Part (5) of the reply below). Our strategy is to identify and intervene in potential cases at an early stage through cross-sectoral collaboration, thereby minimising the likelihood of them developing into more complex social, family, health and safety issues. The individual cases mentioned in the question, where mental health problems require medical intervention, are already serious; treatment or rehabilitation is more difficult, and the social and personal costs are higher. Therefore, adopting a strategy of early identification and intervention is of paramount importance.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;The EDB has all along been providing comprehensive student counselling and support services in schools through the Whole School Approach and multidisciplinary collaboration. Schools are staffed with professionals from different disciplines, including student counselling personnel, school social workers and school-based educational psychologists. Teachers work closely with the school&#39;s specialist team to identify at an early stage and support students in need (including those who are addicted to the Internet or affected by cyberbullying). Besides, the EDB provides the &quot;Information Literacy for Hong Kong Students&quot; Learning Framework and relevant learning and teaching resources for schools. Schools are also encouraged to appropriately incorporate programmes with themes such as &quot;Internet addiction&quot; and &quot;prevention of internet pitfalls&quot; or organise relevant activities. Meanwhile, the EDB also published the Curriculum Framework on Parent Education where promoting the healthy, happy, and balanced development of children or adolescents is one of the key areas. Parents are also encouraged to establish reasonable rules regarding social media and screen time taking into account their children&#39;s ages and developmental needs, and to work in conjunction with school policies to jointly mitigate related risks.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;The Social Welfare Department (SWD) has been providing young people with a range of preventive, developmental and remedial services to help them build up positive values and prevent them from having delinquent behaviours including Internet addiction, during their developmental stage. The services concerned include the &quot;two school social workers for each school&quot; measure implemented in all secondary schools over the territory, and subvented 139 Integrated Children and Youth Services Centres to offer appropriate support and counselling to students or adolescents in need, such as guiding them to use the Internet properly. In addition, in December 2018, the SWD established five Cyber Youth Support Teams to proactively search for and engage with young people through online platforms they frequently use, and to provide timely intervention, support and counselling services to those in need, thereby reducing the likelihood of them becoming cyberbullies or victims of cyberbullying.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;Regarding the regulation of obscene or indecent content on the Internet, given the vast volume and rapidly changing nature of online information, the Office for Film, Newspaper and Article Administration (OFNAA) has been working with the industry to jointly regulate and handle complaints regarding the online publication of obscene or indecent materials, and also proactively monitors content on online platforms. If content deemed obscene or indecent is discovered, the OFNAA will require the relevant platform to add a warning message or to delete, block, or completely obscure the concerned content. In addition, the OFNAA actively promotes wide range of public education and awareness campaigns, collaborating with various stakeholders to enhance public awareness, particularly among children and adolescents, of the Control of Obscene and Indecent Articles Ordinance, encouraging them to stay away from harmful information, and foster a proper understanding of healthy Internet use.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;The DH issued updated health recommendations for children and adolescents in 2018 based on the Report of Advisory Group on Health Effects of Use of Internet and Electronic Screen Products. The SHS Centre of the DH has also been providing free annual health assessments for primary and secondary school students all along, including growth monitoring, screening for vision and hearing, and health assessment questionnaire. If students are found to have health, psychological, mental or behavioural issues, such as excessive screen use, healthcare professionals will provide health counselling and relevant information (such as support services and an overview of information regarding online behaviour). They will also refer cases where mental health concerns are suspected and psychological or medical follow-up is required to clinical psychologists at the SHS, specialist clinics under the HA, or other organisations for further follow-up. In the 2024/25 academic year, approximately 1.0 per cent (Note 2) of students who utilised the SHS were referred to clinical psychologists at the SHS, specialist clinics under the HA, or other organisations for further follow-up due to psychological and behavioural issues.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;The multidisciplinary professional teams of the HA&#39;s child and adolescent psychiatric service, involving doctors, clinical psychologists, nurses, speech therapists, occupational therapists and medical social workers, provide early identification, assessment and treatment services for children and adolescents suspected of having mental health problems requiring medical intervention.&nbsp; The doctors establish diagnoses through clinical assessments and based on international diagnostic criteria.&nbsp; The multidisciplinary professional teams also provide patients with comprehensive and continuous psychiatric assessments through various methods, including integrated clinical interviews, evidence-based assessment tools, and observations.&nbsp; Based on the patient&#39;s clinical condition, complexity of the illness, needs and associated risks, the professional teams set individualised goals and draw up recovery plans, and provide appropriate psychiatric services and support, encompassing inpatient, specialist outpatient (SOP), day rehabilitation training, and community support services, with a view to enhancing patients&#39; speech and communication, social skills, emotion management, problem-solving, learning and life skills. The medical teams also provide the parents and carers of the concerned children and adolescents with knowledge about the diseases in order to enhance their understanding of the symptoms and treatment needs of the patients.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;There has long been a well-established referral mechanism between government departments and NGOs in various sectors. The EDB provides schools with additional resources and professional support; schools, in turn, have dedicated professional teams to provide the necessary support and referral services to students in need (including those addicted to the Internet or affected by cyberbullying). Over the past five academic years, schools have handled an average of approximately 440 cases per year involving school bullying (including cyberbullying).<br />
<br />
(1) Under the abovementioned referral mechanism, individuals whose conditions are severe enough to cause mental health problems requiring medical intervention will receive services at the HA. The HA psychiatric specialist outpatient clinics (SOPCs) implemented the new case triage system to ensure that patients with urgent conditions requiring early diagnosis and treatment receive priority follow-up and treatment. New referrals are typically screened by nurses first and reviewed by the relevant specialists before cases are being classified as Priority 1 (urgent), Priority 2 (semi-urgent), and Routine (stable) cases. In general, psychiatric SOPCs will classify patients with urgent medical needs as Priority 1 (urgent) cases for follow-up. Patients may return to their respective psychiatric SOPCs to receive assessment again if their mental conditions have changed during the waiting time, so that an assessment could be made as to whether there is a need to advance their consultation, or they may consider seeking treatment from the accident and emergency services.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;From 2021-22 to 2025-26, the number of new cases and their respective median waiting time in the HA for child and adolescent psychiatric SOP service are tabulated below (Note 3 and 4):<br />
&nbsp;
<table border="1" style="width:566px">
	<tbody>
		<tr>
			<td rowspan="2" style="height:17px; width:75px">Year</td>
			<td colspan="2" style="height:17px; width:156px">Priority 1</td>
			<td colspan="2" style="height:17px; width:159px">Priority 2</td>
			<td colspan="2" style="height:17px; width:167px">Routine</td>
		</tr>
		<tr>
			<td style="height:33px; width:74px">Number of new cases&nbsp;</td>
			<td style="height:33px; width:80px">Median waiting time (week(s))</td>
			<td style="height:33px; width:74px">Number of new cases&nbsp;</td>
			<td style="height:33px; width:83px">Median waiting time (week(s))</td>
			<td style="height:33px; width:74px">Number of new cases&nbsp;</td>
			<td style="height:33px; width:91px">Median waiting time (week(s))</td>
		</tr>
		<tr>
			<td style="height:17px; width:75px">2021-22</td>
			<td style="height:17px; width:74px">313</td>
			<td style="height:17px; width:80px">1</td>
			<td style="height:17px; width:74px">952</td>
			<td style="height:17px; width:83px">3</td>
			<td style="height:17px; width:74px">12 245</td>
			<td style="height:17px; width:91px">79</td>
		</tr>
		<tr>
			<td style="height:17px; width:75px">2022-23</td>
			<td style="height:17px; width:74px">206</td>
			<td style="height:17px; width:80px">1</td>
			<td style="height:17px; width:74px">959</td>
			<td style="height:17px; width:83px">3</td>
			<td style="height:17px; width:74px">13 089</td>
			<td style="height:17px; width:91px">73</td>
		</tr>
		<tr>
			<td style="height:17px; width:75px">2023-24</td>
			<td style="height:17px; width:74px">190</td>
			<td style="height:17px; width:80px">1</td>
			<td style="height:17px; width:74px">1 107</td>
			<td style="height:17px; width:83px">2</td>
			<td style="height:17px; width:74px">13 078</td>
			<td style="height:17px; width:91px">77</td>
		</tr>
		<tr>
			<td style="height:17px; width:75px">2024-25</td>
			<td style="height:17px; width:74px">173</td>
			<td style="height:17px; width:80px">&lt;1</td>
			<td style="height:17px; width:74px">1 082</td>
			<td style="height:17px; width:83px">2</td>
			<td style="height:17px; width:74px">13 607</td>
			<td style="height:17px; width:91px">77</td>
		</tr>
		<tr>
			<td style="height:17px; width:75px">2025-26</td>
			<td style="height:17px; width:74px">130</td>
			<td style="height:17px; width:80px">&lt;1</td>
			<td style="height:17px; width:74px">1 033</td>
			<td style="height:17px; width:83px">2</td>
			<td style="height:17px; width:74px">13 624</td>
			<td style="height:17px; width:91px">70</td>
		</tr>
	</tbody>
</table>
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;Based on the reply above, the factors and impacts of social media are manifold. The development of depression, anxiety, or self-harm behaviours among children and adolescents can be attributed to multiple factors, making it difficult to identify a single cause. Cyberbullying or excessive Internet use may be among the contributing factors. The HA does not maintain statistics on the number of psychiatric patients suffering from conditions such as depression or anxiety that are induced by cyberbullying or Internet addiction.<br />
<br />
(5) In addition to parts (3) and (4) of the reply regarding identification, intervention, treatment, and rehabilitation services, the Government attaches great importance to promoting public mental health education, examples of public education and outreach efforts regarding the prevention of Internet addiction among children and adolescents and the coping with cyberbullying trauma are as follows:<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;The EDB, the Committee on Home-School Co-operation and the Hong Kong Education City jointly organise a series of parent seminars every year, such as parenting for the e-Generation, youth online culture, online risks and tips for handling Internet addiction of children. The EDB also disseminates important messages in a timely manner to support the physical and psychological development of students, contents include helping children use electronic devices properly, preventing children from falling into online pitfalls and handling Internet addiction of children through the Smart Parent Net one-stop parent education website (<a href="https://www.parent.edu.hk/en/smart-parent-net" target="_blank">parent.edu.hk</a>) and its social media platforms for parents&#39; reference. The EDB has also commissioned NGOs to provide telephone hotlines to offer individual support to teachers, students and parents in need.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;The SWD has launched the Pilot Programme on Training for Mental Health Promotion Ambassador in October 2026 and aims to recruit 5 000 ambassadors from all ages and backgrounds. After receiving 50 hours of training (including training in digital mastery), practical activities and internship, the ambassadors will help promote healthy lifestyles, foster resilience and establish care support networks in the community.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;The GovHK features a dedicated webpage on Points to Note for Internet Social Networking, which outlines the risks associated with using social networking platforms and the relevant precautionary measures. The webpage also provides advice for parents on helping their children develop healthy Internet usage habits, including working out a reasonable timetable for online activities with their children to prevent addictive Internet use. If parents discover that their children encounter cyberbullying, they are advised to encourage their children to seek help, and contact their children&#39;s school and consider reporting the matter to the Police for assistance.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;The Digital Policy Office (DPO) also provides information on information security and safe use of the Internet through its one-stop information security portal, InfoSec (<a href="https://www.infosec.gov.hk" target="_blank">www.infosec.gov.hk</a>), which provides reference materials for adolescents and students, as well as parents and teachers, covering cyber ethics, and the safe use of social networking platforms and instant messaging applications, to help them understand online risks and relevant precautionary measures.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;The DH is committed to promoting healthy lifestyle and mental health. It provides practical health information to children, adolescents, and parents through various media and channels, including websites, short videos and infographics, in an interesting way. These include the &quot;Emotional Health Tips&quot; and the YouthCan website specifically designed for the youth, covering topics such as &quot;Cyber Health&quot;, &quot;Stop Bullying&quot;, and &quot;Addictive Behaviours&quot;. The DH will continue promotion of healthy lifestyle, mental health and healthy use of the Internet and electronic screen products among students and parents. Health tips and advice are disseminated through different media and channels. They are available at the SHS website.&nbsp;<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;In September 2023 and March 2026, &quot;Shall We Talk&quot; launched bilingual articles and infographics covering topics such as &quot;Cyberbullying and Prevention of Adolescent Suicide&quot;, &quot;Social Media and Happiness&quot; and &quot;Making Good Use of Social Media to Safeguard Our Mental Health&quot;. Combined with traditional media (newspapers and radio) and social media, these educational materials aimed to promote relevant mental health messages to the public. As changes in the social environment and daily habits have led to increased screen time and Internet usage among children, &quot;Shall We Talk&quot; launched a primary school drama tour running from April to December 2026 across Hong Kong. The tour promotes the healthy and proper use of screens and the Internet, while encouraging children to share their concerns or feelings with trusted people and seek help when necessary. As of September 2026, the programme has reached 26 primary schools.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;The Hong Kong Police Force (HKPF) has been committed to addressing the risks associated with the use of electronic screens and social media, with a view to better protecting children from cyber harm. The HKPF implements targeted preventive and educational initiatives in a proactive and collaborative approach. The HKPF has set up relevant online platforms, including the &quot;One-stop Child Protection Online Platform&quot; (<a href="https://www.childprotection.gov.hk/#/" target="_blank">ChildProtection.gov.hk</a>), which provides comprehensive online resources for children, youth, parents and teachers to help them identify cyber pitfalls and related crimes, and enhance their cybersecurity awareness and self-protection ability. Apart from online platforms, the HKPF also provides physical education resources, such as the publication &quot;Decoding Traps: A Guide for Teachers and Parents&quot; and the &quot;Letters to Parents&quot; series. Through real life cases, these materials offer parents and teachers practical guidelines and toolkits for addressing cyber risks faced by children and youth. Meanwhile, the HKPF also actively organises school talks and multidisciplinary seminars to strengthen collaboration among families, schools and NGOs, to jointly build a safe digital environment for children and youth.<br />
<br />
(6) The Government is deeply concerned about the potential impact of electronic screen and social media use on the physical and mental development of children and adolescents. In response to concerns raised by the Government and the community regarding the health effects of electronic screen use and social media among children and young people, the interdepartmental Advisory Group on Health Effects of Screen and Social Media Use for Children and Adolescents (Advisory Group) was established in October 2025. Its members include representatives from the DH, the EDB, the SWD, the DPO, the Primary Healthcare Commission, as well as the Hong Kong Academy of Medicine and NGOs. The Advisory Group is tasked with reviewing the latest medical and scientific evidence, relevant developments and experiences in other regions, and synthesising the views of experts and stakeholders, with the aim of releasing updated health recommendations in 2026. As announced in the 2026 Policy Address, a dedicated task force on minors&#39; use of social media will be established under the Interdepartmental Working Group to Review Legislation to Support Wider Application of AI spearheaded by the DoJ. It will co-ordinate with relevant bureaux/departments to review the risks associated with the use of social media by minors (including interaction with AI), and assess the need for more targeted solutions, including legislative and administrative measures.<br />
<br />
Note 1: Students in primary 4 and 6, secondary 2, 4 and 6 complete the health assessment questionnaire themselves.<br />
Note 2: The current data from the SHS Centre of the DH only covers the number of students referred due to psychological and behavioural issues; it does not provide a breakdown by specific reasons for referral (such as Internet addiction or trauma resulting from cyberbullying).<br />
Note 3: With effect from October 1, 2022, the waiting time for new case booking is calculated from the date the booking application is submitted to the scheduled appointment date at SOPC or integrated clinic (whichever is earlier).<br />
Note 4: In view of the COVID-19 epidemic outbreak in Hong Kong in early 2020, the HA has adjusted its services to cope with the outbreak. Therefore, the above situation should also be taken into account when comparing the HA&#39;s service volume across the relevant years. With the subsiding of Hong Kong&#39;s COVID-19 epidemic situation and cessation of anti-epidemic measures in early 2023, the HA has been gradually resuming provision of all of its public healthcare services to tie in with the Government&#39;s normalcy measures.]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[CFS finds excessive Bacillus cereus in sample of bun with fresh meat and preserved vegetable]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/14/P2026101400675.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/14/P2026101400675.htm</link><pubDate>Wed, 14 Oct 2026 18:38:36 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The Centre for Food Safety (CFS) of the Food and Environmental Hygiene Department today (October 14) announced that a sample of a bun with fresh meat and preserved vegetable was found to contain an excessive amount of Bacillus cereus. The CFS is following up on the incident.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;A spokesman for the CFS said, &quot;The CFS collected the above-mentioned sample from a food factory in Kwun Tong for testing. The test result showed that the sample contained Bacillus cereus at a level of 250 000 per gram. According to the Microbiological Guidelines for Food, if ready-to-eat food contains Bacillus cereus at a level of more than 100 000 per gram, it is considered unsatisfactory.&quot;<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The CFS instantly deployed staff to the food factory concerned for an investigation, and has informed it of the irregularity. The food factory has stopped selling and discarded the affected product according to the CFS&#39;s instruction. The CFS has also provided education on food safety and hygiene to the staff of the food factory, and requested that they carry out a thorough cleaning and disinfection.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&quot;Bacillus cereus is commonly found in the environment. Unhygienic conditions in food processing and storage may give rise to its growth. Consuming food contaminated with excessive Bacillus cereus or its heat-stable toxins may cause gastrointestinal upset such as vomiting and diarrhoea,&quot; the spokesman said.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;According to section 54 of the Public Health and Municipal Services Ordinance (Cap. 132), all food available for sale in Hong Kong, locally produced or imported, should be fit for human consumption. An offender is subject to a maximum fine of $50,000 and imprisonment for six months upon conviction.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The CFS will continue to follow up on the incident and take appropriate action. The investigation is ongoing.]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[Scam alert related to banks]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/14/P2026101400495.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/14/P2026101400495.htm</link><pubDate>Wed, 14 Oct 2026 18:10:00 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[The following is issued on behalf of the Hong Kong Monetary Authority:<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The Hong Kong Monetary Authority (HKMA) wishes to alert members of the public to the press releases issued by the banks listed below relating to fraudulent websites, internet banking login screens, phishing emails or other scams, which have been reported to the HKMA. Hyperlinks to the press releases are available on <a href="https://www.hkma.gov.hk/eng/smart-consumers/beware-of-fraudsters/#fraudulent-bank-websites-phishing-emails-and-similar-scams" target="_blank">the HKMA website</a>.<br />
&nbsp;
<table border="1" style="width:564px">
	<tbody>
		<tr>
			<td style="width:277px">Bank</td>
			<td style="width:287px">Type of Scam</td>
		</tr>
		<tr>
			<td style="width:277px">The Bank of East Asia, Limited</td>
			<td style="width:287px">Fraudulent website and internet banking login screen</td>
		</tr>
		<tr>
			<td style="width:277px">Shanghai Commercial Bank Limited</td>
			<td style="width:287px">Fraudulent website and internet banking login screen</td>
		</tr>
		<tr>
			<td style="width:277px">Chong Hing Bank Limited</td>
			<td style="width:287px">Fraudulent website and internet banking login screen</td>
		</tr>
		<tr>
			<td style="width:277px">Chiyu Banking Corporation Limited</td>
			<td style="width:287px">Fraudulent website and internet banking login screen</td>
		</tr>
		<tr>
			<td style="width:277px">The Hongkong and Shanghai Banking Corporation Limited</td>
			<td style="width:287px">Fraudulent website</td>
		</tr>
		<tr>
			<td style="width:277px">Livi Bank Limited</td>
			<td style="width:287px">Phishing instant messages</td>
		</tr>
	</tbody>
</table>
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The HKMA wishes to remind the public that banks will not send SMS or emails with embedded hyperlinks which direct them to the banks&#39; websites to carry out transactions. They will not ask customers for sensitive information, such as login passwords or One-Time Password, by phone, email or SMS (including via embedded hyperlinks).<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Anyone who has provided his or her personal information, or who has conducted any financial transactions, through or in response to the scams concerned, should contact the relevant bank with the information provided in the corresponding press release, and report the matter to the Crime Wing Information Centre of the Hong Kong Police Force at 2860 5012.]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[Toll rate for Central Kowloon Bypass to take effect from December 6]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/14/P2026101400518.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/14/P2026101400518.htm</link><pubDate>Wed, 14 Oct 2026 18:00:00 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The Government announced today (October 14) that the Central Kowloon Bypass will be fully commissioned on December 6, 2026 (Sunday), and an $8 toll will take effect from the same date. The Legislative Council (LegCo) approved in 2025 the $8 toll arrangement for all vehicles using the Central Kowloon Bypass. In this connection, the Secretary for Transport and Logistics has made the Road Tunnels (Government) (Amendment) (No. 2) Regulation 2025 (Commencement) Notice (the Commencement Notice) to specify that the relevant toll provisions will come into operation from December 6, 2026. The Commencement Notice will be published in the Gazette on October 16 and tabled at the LegCo for negative vetting on October 21.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The Central Kowloon Bypass comprises the already commissioned Central Kowloon Bypass (Yau Ma Tei Section Tunnel), and the Central Kowloon Bypass (Kowloon Bay Section Tunnel) which is scheduled for commissioning on December 6, 2026. Together with the Tseung Kwan O - Lam Tin Tunnel, it forms Route 6, linking Tseung Kwan O, Kowloon East and Kowloon West. Upon the commissioning of Route 6, the journey time between Tseung Kwan O and Yau Ma Tei during peak hours is expected to be substantially reduced from about 65 minutes to about 12 minutes, bringing considerable convenience to the public.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;A spokesperson for the Transport and Logistics Bureau said, &quot;Having duly considered public affordability, the toll for the Central Kowloon Bypass has been set at $8. It is expected to recover part of the operating costs while achieving the intended traffic diversion effect, thereby alleviating traffic on major roads in Kowloon. The Government has been actively launching publicity campaigns to help the public to fully grasp the toll arrangement, with a view to ensuring the smooth implementation of the new toll arrangement.&quot;]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[FEHD launches second phase of "Operation Skynet" today to inspect public markets after achieving significant results in first phase (with photos)]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/14/P2026101400627.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/14/P2026101400627.htm</link><pubDate>Wed, 14 Oct 2026 17:55:00 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The Food and Environmental Hygiene Department (FEHD) announced today (October 14) that the first phase of &quot;Operation Skynet&quot;, a two-week special inspection and enforcement operation launched on September 30, targeting food premises such as supermarkets and restaurants, has concluded. The department also announced the immediate launch of its second phase targeting public markets to continue its stringent crackdown on pest and hygiene problems, with a view to ensuring food safety and improving environmental hygiene.<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The first phase of &quot;Operation Skynet&quot; covered supermarkets, restaurants and other food premises across all districts. During the operation, FEHD officers focused on reviewing the environmental hygiene and refuse management of these premises to ensure proper waste disposal, checking goods shelves, refrigerators and food storage areas to ensure cleanliness and hygiene, and thoroughly inspecting for signs of pest and rodent activity to identify root causes and potential risks.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;During the operation, FEHD officers conducted a total of 1 177 inspections against supermarkets, issued 65 statutory Notices of Elimination of Vermin, 25 Notices of Requirement to Cleanse and Repair Food Premises and two warnings, and initiated 14 prosecutions; conducted a total of 432 inspections against restaurants, issued 35 statutory Notices of Elimination of Vermin, 41 Notices of Requirement to Cleanse and Repair Food Premises and six warnings, and initiated 65 prosecutions; and conducted 321 inspections against other food premises, issued 20 statutory Notices of Elimination of Vermin and 17 Notices of Requirement to Cleanse and Repair Food Premises, and initiated 24 prosecutions. The department will continue to follow up on these findings, and to step up follow-up inspections and take further enforcement action where necessary.<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;A spokesman for the FEHD said that any premises (including supermarkets) selling pre-made and prepackaged cooked foods, sushi and sashimi must obtain the relevant licence or restricted food permit issued by the FEHD and comply with the applicable licensing conditions. The department has been flexibly deploying resources in light of the actual circumstances, and adopting a risk-based strategy for inspections and enforcement actions to safeguard public health.<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The spokesman stressed that maintaining environmental hygiene in the community is everyone&#39;s responsibility. Pest and rodent control as well as food safety rely on the concerted efforts of the Government, the trade and the public. The FEHD will continue to closely monitor the hygiene conditions of various food premises through a multipronged approach, combining risk-based inspections with publicity, education and community collaboration. Should irregularities be found, the department will take immediate enforcement action to diligently perform its gatekeeping role in protecting food safety and safeguarding environmental hygiene for the public.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The second phase of &quot;Operation Skynet&quot;, a special inspection and enforcement operation targeting public markets across all districts, will last for two weeks. The department will step up inspections and enforcement, strictly requiring stall tenants to take proper food hygiene management and rodent control measures. Stringent enforcement action will be taken in accordance with the law if stalls are found in poor hygienic conditions or in case any tenant fails to comply with the requirements. The department will take into account information including public complaints, inspection results, number of rodents caught, rodent signs and actual risks to adjust the intensity of inspections and rodent control efforts in response to the actual circumstances of various public markets.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Meanwhile, the FEHD is conducting an enforcement operation codenamed &quot;Operation Clear Edge&quot; across its cooked food centres and public markets to strictly combat and crack down on illegal smoking through blitz inspections. Since the commencement of the operation from September 28, FEHD officers have issued nearly 50 fixed penalty notices for smoking offences as of October 12. The second phase of &quot;Operation Skynet&quot; and &quot;Operation Clear Edge&quot; will be carried out concurrently to ensure the environmental hygiene of public markets.<div id="photos"><a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/14/P2026101400627_photo_1341135.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/14/P2026101400627_photo_1341135.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;<a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/14/P2026101400627_photo_1341132.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/14/P2026101400627_photo_1341132.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;<a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/14/P2026101400627_photo_1341131.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/14/P2026101400627_photo_1341131.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;<a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/14/P2026101400627_photo_1341130.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/14/P2026101400627_photo_1341130.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;<a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/14/P2026101400627_photo_1341129.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/14/P2026101400627_photo_1341129.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;</div>]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[Tender of 3-year HKD HKSAR Institutional Government Bonds through re-opening to be held on October 21 ]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/14/P2026101400544.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/14/P2026101400544.htm</link><pubDate>Wed, 14 Oct 2026 17:46:54 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[The following is issued on behalf of the Hong Kong Monetary Authority:<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;​The Hong Kong Monetary Authority (HKMA), as representative of the Hong Kong Special Administrative Region Government (HKSAR Government), announced today (October 14) that a tender of 3-year HKD institutional Government Bonds (Bonds) through the re-opening of existing 5-year Government Bond issue 05GB2912001 under the Infrastructure Bond Programme will be held on October 21, 2026 (Wednesday), for settlement on October 22, 2026 (Thursday).<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;An additional amount of HK$0.75 billion of the outstanding 5-year Bonds (issue no. 05GB2912001) will be on offer. The Bonds will mature on December 5, 2029 and will carry interest at the rate of 3.23 per cent per annum payable semi-annually in arrear. The Indicative Pricings of the Bonds on October 14, 2026 are 98.74 with an annualised yield of 3.690 per cent.<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Tender is open only to Primary Dealers appointed under the Infrastructure Bond Programme. Anyone wishing to apply for the Bonds on offer can do so through any of the Primary Dealers on the latest published list, which can be obtained from the Hong Kong Government Bonds website at <a href="https://www.hkgb.gov.hk/en/index.html" target="_blank">www.hkgb.gov.hk</a>. Each tender must be for an amount of HK$50,000 or integral multiples thereof.<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Tender results will be published on the HKMA&rsquo;s website, the Hong Kong Government Bonds website, Bloomberg (GBHK &lt;GO&gt;) and Refinitiv (IBPGSBPINDEX). The publication time is expected to be no later than 3pm on the tender day.<br />
<br />
HKSAR Institutional Government Bonds Tender Information<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Tender information of 3-year HKD HKSAR Institutional Government Bonds:<br />
&nbsp;
<table border="0">
	<tbody>
		<tr>
			<td style="width:154px">Issue Number</td>
			<td style="width:19px">&nbsp;</td>
			<td style="width:19px">:</td>
			<td style="height:20px; width:20px">05GB2912001</td>
		</tr>
		<tr>
			<td style="height:20px; width:20px">Stock Code</td>
			<td style="height:20px; width:20px">&nbsp;</td>
			<td style="height:20px; width:20px">:</td>
			<td style="height:20px; width:20px">4284 (HKGB 3.23 2912)</td>
		</tr>
		<tr>
			<td style="width:154px">Tender Date and Time</td>
			<td style="width:19px">&nbsp;</td>
			<td style="width:19px">:</td>
			<td style="height:40px; width:20px">October 21, 2026 (Wednesday)<br />
			9.30am to 10.30am</td>
		</tr>
		<tr>
			<td style="width:154px">Issue and Settlement Date</td>
			<td style="width:19px">&nbsp;</td>
			<td style="width:19px">:</td>
			<td style="width:362px">October 22, 2026 (Thursday)</td>
		</tr>
		<tr>
			<td style="width:154px">Amount on Offer</td>
			<td style="width:19px">&nbsp;</td>
			<td style="width:19px">:</td>
			<td style="height:20px; width:20px">HK$0.75 billion</td>
		</tr>
		<tr>
			<td style="height:20px; width:20px">Maturity</td>
			<td style="height:20px; width:20px">&nbsp;</td>
			<td style="height:20px; width:20px">:</td>
			<td style="height:20px; width:20px">3 years</td>
		</tr>
		<tr>
			<td style="height:20px; width:20px">Remaining maturity</td>
			<td style="height:20px; width:20px">&nbsp;</td>
			<td style="height:20px; width:20px">:</td>
			<td style="height:20px; width:20px">Approximately 3.12 years</td>
		</tr>
		<tr>
			<td style="height:20px; width:20px">Maturity Date</td>
			<td style="height:20px; width:20px">&nbsp;</td>
			<td style="height:20px; width:20px">:</td>
			<td style="height:20px; width:20px">December 5, 2029&nbsp;(Wednesday)</td>
		</tr>
		<tr>
			<td style="height:20px; width:20px">Interest Rate</td>
			<td style="height:20px; width:20px">&nbsp;</td>
			<td style="height:20px; width:20px">:</td>
			<td style="height:20px; width:20px">3.23 per cent p.a. payable semi-annually in arrear</td>
		</tr>
		<tr>
			<td style="width:154px">Interest Payment Dates</td>
			<td style="width:19px">&nbsp;</td>
			<td style="width:19px">:</td>
			<td style="height:140px; width:20px">June 5 and December 5 in each year, commencing on the Issue Date up to and including the Maturity Date, subject to adjustment in accordance with the terms of the Institutional Issuances Information Memorandum of the Infrastructure Bond Programme and Government Sustainable Bond Programme (Information Memorandum) published on the Hong Kong Government Bonds website.</td>
		</tr>
		<tr>
			<td style="width:154px">Method of Tender</td>
			<td style="width:19px">&nbsp;</td>
			<td style="width:19px">:</td>
			<td style="width:362px">Competitive tender</td>
		</tr>
		<tr>
			<td style="width:154px">Tender Amount</td>
			<td style="width:19px">&nbsp;</td>
			<td style="height:220px; width:20px">:</td>
			<td style="height:220px; width:20px">Each competitive tender must be for an amount of HK$50,000 or integral multiples thereof. Any tender applications for the Bonds must be submitted through a Primary Dealer on the latest published list.<br />
			The accrued interest to be paid by successful bidders on the issue date (October 22, 2026) for the tender amount is HK$615.03 per minimum denomination of HK$50,000.<br />
			(The accrued interest to be paid for tender amount exceeding HK$50,000 may not be exactly equal to the figures calculated from the accrued interest per minimum denomination of HK$50,000 due to rounding).</td>
		</tr>
		<tr>
			<td style="width:154px">Other Details</td>
			<td style="width:19px">&nbsp;</td>
			<td style="width:19px">:</td>
			<td style="height:60px; width:20px">Please see the Information Memorandum available on the Hong Kong Government Bonds website or approach Primary Dealers.</td>
		</tr>
		<tr>
			<td style="width:154px">Expected commencement date of dealing on<br />
			the Stock Exchange<br />
			of Hong Kong Limited</td>
			<td style="width:19px">&nbsp;</td>
			<td style="width:19px">:</td>
			<td style="width:362px">The tender amount is fully fungible with the existing 05GB2912001 (Stock code: 4284) listed on the Stock Exchange of Hong Kong.</td>
		</tr>
		<tr>
			<td style="width:154px">Use of Proceeds</td>
			<td style="width:19px">&nbsp;</td>
			<td style="width:19px">:</td>
			<td style="height:100px; width:20px">The Bonds will be issued under the institutional part of the Infrastructure Bond Programme. Proceeds will be invested in infrastructure projects in accordance with the Infrastructure Bond Framework published on the Hong Kong Government Bonds website.</td>
		</tr>
	</tbody>
</table>
]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[Tender of 10-year HKD HKSAR Institutional Government Bonds through re-opening to be held on October 21]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/14/P2026101400574.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/14/P2026101400574.htm</link><pubDate>Wed, 14 Oct 2026 17:46:00 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[The following is issued on behalf of the Hong Kong Monetary Authority:<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The Hong Kong Monetary Authority (HKMA), as representative of the Hong Kong Special Administrative Region Government (HKSAR Government), announced today (October 14) that a tender of 10-year HKD institutional Government Bonds (Bonds) through the re-opening of existing 10-year Government Bond issue 10GB3507001 under the Infrastructure Bond Programme will be held on October 21, 2026 (Wednesday), for settlement on October 22, 2026 (Thursday).<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;An additional amount of HK$1.25 billion of the outstanding 10-year Bonds (issue no. 10GB3507001) will be on offer. The Bonds will mature on July 24, 2035 and will carry interest at the rate of 3.17&nbsp;per cent per annum payable semi-annually in arrear. The Indicative Pricings of the Bonds on October 14, 2026 are 94.28 with an annualised yield of 3.986 per cent.<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Tender is open only to Primary Dealers appointed under the Infrastructure Bond Programme. Anyone wishing to apply for the Bonds on offer can do so through any of the Primary Dealers on the latest published list, which can be obtained from the Hong Kong Government Bonds website at <a href="https://www.hkgb.gov.hk" target="_blank">www.hkgb.gov.hk</a>. Each tender must be for an amount of HK$50,000 or integral multiples thereof.<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Tender results will be published on the HKMA&#39;s website, the Hong Kong Government Bonds website, Bloomberg&nbsp;(GBHK &lt;GO&gt;)&nbsp;and Refinitiv (IBPGSBPINDEX). The publication time is expected to be no later than 3pm on the tender day.<br />
<br />
HKSAR Institutional Government Bonds Tender Information<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Tender information of 10-year HKD HKSAR Institutional Government Bonds:<br />
&nbsp;
<table border="0">
	<tbody>
		<tr>
			<td style="width:157px">Issue Number</td>
			<td style="width:20px">:</td>
			<td style="height:20px; width:20px">10GB3507001</td>
		</tr>
		<tr>
			<td style="height:20px; width:20px">Stock Code</td>
			<td style="height:20px; width:20px">:</td>
			<td style="height:20px; width:20px">4294 (HKGB 3.17 3507)</td>
		</tr>
		<tr>
			<td style="height:20px; width:20px">Tender Date and Time</td>
			<td style="height:20px; width:20px">:</td>
			<td style="height:20px; width:20px">October 21, 2026&nbsp;(Wednesday)<br />
			9.30 am to 10.30 am</td>
		</tr>
		<tr>
			<td style="height:20px; width:20px">Issue and Settlement Date</td>
			<td style="height:20px; width:20px">:</td>
			<td style="height:20px; width:20px">October 22, 2026&nbsp;(Thursday)</td>
		</tr>
