			<td style="width:20px">:</td>
			<td style="height:100px; width:20px">The Bonds will be issued under the institutional part of the Infrastructure Bond Programme.&nbsp; Proceeds will be invested in infrastructure projects in accordance with the Infrastructure Bond Framework published on the Hong Kong Government Bonds website.</td>
		</tr>
	</tbody>
</table>
]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[Hong Kong Customs seizes dried shark fins of suspected scheduled endangered species (with photo)]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/14/P2026101400623.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/14/P2026101400623.htm</link><pubDate>Wed, 14 Oct 2026 17:45:00 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Hong Kong Customs yesterday (October 13) seized 18 kilograms of suspected scheduled dried shark fins of endangered species, with an estimated market value of about $45,000, at the Lo Wu Control Point.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Customs officers intercepted an incoming 68-year-old Mainland female passenger at the Arrival Hall of the above-mentioned control point yesterday. Upon examination, the batch of suspected scheduled dried shark fins was seized from her personal baggage. Officers of the Agriculture, Fisheries and Conservation Department (AFCD) attended the scene for inspection and preliminarily identified the batch of dried shark fins as of endangered species listed in the Appendices to the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora and regulated under the Protection of Endangered Species of Animals and Plants Ordinance in Hong Kong. The case was handed over to the AFCD for follow-up investigation.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Customs reminds the public not to carry controlled items into and out of Hong Kong.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Under the Protection of Endangered Species of Animals and Plants Ordinance, any person importing, exporting or possessing specimens of endangered species not in accordance with the Ordinance commits an offence and will be liable to a maximum fine of $10 million and imprisonment for 10 years upon conviction with the specimens forfeited.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Members of the public may report any suspected smuggling activities to Customs&#39; 24-hour hotline 182 8080 or its dedicated crime-reporting email account (<a href="mailto:crimereport@customs.gov.hk" target="_blank">crimereport@customs.gov.hk</a>) or online form (<a href="https://eform.cefs.gov.hk/form/ced002/en/" target="_blank">eform.cefs.gov.hk/form/ced002</a>).<div id="photos"><a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/14/P2026101400623_photo_1341124.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/14/P2026101400623_photo_1341124.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;</div>]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA["iAM Smart" introduces AI Assistant feature]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/14/P2026101400480.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/14/P2026101400480.htm</link><pubDate>Wed, 14 Oct 2026 17:00:00 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;The Digital Policy Office (DPO) announced today (October 14) that the &quot;iAM Smart&quot; mobile application is introducing an AI Assistant feature to answer enquiries about government public services.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;The AI Assistant&#39;s service covers enquiries related to government public services. Members of the public only need to type in their enquiries, and the AI Assistant will leverage AI technology to retrieve information instantly from official sources, and organise it into coherent and concise content for reference by members of the public. When members of the public enquire about an online application procedure, the AI Assistant will list out eligibility criteria, step-by-step instructions and a relevant service link, enabling one-click access to the online service. Where applicable, it will also integrate with the &quot;e-ME&quot; form filling function of &quot;iAM Smart&quot; to assist users in completing online forms with ease.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;The latest version of the &quot;iAM Smart&quot; mobile application (version 5.0), with the AI Assistant feature, will be released to users gradually starting from today to late October via official application stores. Users can install the latest version of the mobile application and simply tap the icon of the mascot &quot;Swifty&quot; (see attachment) on the homepage to activate the AI Assistant.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;The DPO will continuously optimise the AI Assistant feature. It is anticipated that an enhanced version of AI Assistant with personalised features will be launched by the end of 2026, further enhancing the user experience.]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[FS to attend Annual Meetings of IMF and World Bank Group in Thailand]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/14/P2026101400449.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/14/P2026101400449.htm</link><pubDate>Wed, 14 Oct 2026 17:00:00 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;The Financial Secretary, Mr Paul Chan, will depart for Bangkok, Thailand, tomorrow morning (October 15) to attend the Annual Meetings of the International Monetary Fund (IMF) and the World Bank Group (WBG) as a member of the Chinese delegation.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;This year&#39;s Annual Meetings will bring together officials responsible for fiscal, financial and economic affairs, central bank representatives, business and non-governmental sector leaders, and academics from around the world to discuss issues including the global economic outlook, financial stability, poverty reduction, inclusive growth and job creation. The meetings will also focus on mobilising private capital to support infrastructure investment, employment and sustainable growth across economies, as well as harnessing artificial intelligence to promote development while managing the associated risks.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;Mr Chan will attend the plenary session of the Annual Meetings on October 16. During the Annual Meetings, he will also attend a roundtable co-organised by the IMF and the Hong Kong Monetary Authority to explore investment and financing opportunities in the Hong Kong market for economies in the Middle East and Central Asia. He will outline how Hong Kong can leverage the strengths of its financial markets and professional services to meet these economies&#39; investment and financing needs, and foster cross-border financial co-operation.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;In addition, Mr Chan will take part in dialogue sessions organised respectively by Bloomberg and the Atlantic Council to exchange views on Hong Kong&#39;s economy, offshore Renminbi business, technological and financial innovation, and the global economic and trade landscape. He will highlight Hong Kong&#39;s strengths and resilience as an international financial centre, as well as its role in connecting the Mainland and global capital markets.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;During the Annual Meetings, Mr Chan will also meet with financial officials from various economies and senior executives of the IMF, the WBG and international financial firms to exchange views on the global and regional economic and financial situations. He will also introduce the latest developments and opportunities in Hong Kong&#39;s financial services sector to strengthen ties and foster further co-operation.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;Mr Chan will depart from Bangkok for Hong Kong on the morning of October 17 and arrive in Hong Kong in the afternoon. During his absence, the Deputy Financial Secretary, Mr Michael Wong, will act as Financial Secretary.]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[HKMH to showcase education legacy of Hong Kong's Ta Teh Institute (with photos)]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/14/P2026101400252.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/14/P2026101400252.htm</link><pubDate>Wed, 14 Oct 2026 16:37:00 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;The Leisure and Cultural Services Department (LCSD) has collaborated with the Guangzhou Municipal Culture, Radio, Television and Tourism Bureau (Guangzhou Municipal Cultural Heritage Administration) to present the &quot;National Development and Achievements Series: Cultivation and Renewal - The Education Legacy of Hong Kong&#39;s Ta Teh Institute&quot; exhibition at the Hong Kong Museum of History (HKMH) from today (October 14) to February 15, 2027. The exhibition presents a fuller picture of the Institute&#39;s educational legacy and its contribution in cultivating an impressive cohort of talented individuals who went on to play leading roles in the development of the country.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;Hong Kong&#39;s Ta Teh Institute was founded under the leadership of the Guangdong District Committee of the Communist Party of China, with co-operation between the Communist Party of China and the patriotic democrats. It was established in Tuen Mun, Hong Kong, as a full-time liberal arts university and operated between 1946 and 1949. The Institute offered a high-calibre professional and cultural curriculum, and brought together many renowned academics and cultural figures, including historians Hou Wailu and Jian Bozan, political scientist Deng Chumin, literary theorist Huang Yaomian, economists Shen Zhiyuan and Qian Jiaju, as well as literary figures such as Guo Moruo, Mao Dun and Cao Yu, philosopher Hu Sheng, and more.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;This year marks the 80th anniversary of the establishment of the Ta Teh Institute. The exhibition specially brings together more than 60 items (sets) of artefacts related to the Institute and its alumni, as well as around 125 historical photographs and supplementary exhibits, of which over half are displayed for the first time in Hong Kong. Selected exhibits include meeting minutes of the 8th Meeting of the Institute Affairs Committee, student cards, lecture outlines delivered by renowned professors, a medal of an athletic meet, a political work engineer uniform, and a commemorative badge for the liberation of Central-South China from alumni, and other exhibits. The exhibition recreates the most iconic features of the campus, including the arch, school buildings, dormitory and classroom, taking visitors on a journey through the campus life, inspiring teacher-student stories and vibrant learning atmosphere of this legendary Institute.<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;During the exhibition, the HKMH will organise a series of engaging and diverse experiential activities, including lectures, interactive workshops, and screenings with talks, allowing audiences to understand more about the educational ideals upheld by the Ta Teh Institute and its unique place in the history of education in Hong Kong.<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;This exhibition is one of the exhibitions of the National Development and Achievements Series, focusing on the nation&#39;s cultivation of talent. Admission is free. The exhibition is jointly presented by the LCSD and the Guangzhou Municipal Culture, Radio, Television and Tourism Bureau (Guangzhou Municipal Cultural Heritage Administration), jointly organised by the HKMH and the Memorial Museum of Comrade Mao Zedong Sponsoring the Peasant Movement Institute, and supported by the Hong Kong Chronicles Institute. Details of the exhibition are available at <a href="https://hk.history.museum/en/web/mh/exhibition/Ta-Teh.html" target="_blank">hk.history.museum/en/web/mh/exhibition/Ta-Teh.html</a>.<div id="photos"><a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/14/P2026101400252_photo_1340991.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/14/P2026101400252_photo_1340991.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;<a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/14/P2026101400252_photo_1340992.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/14/P2026101400252_photo_1340992.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;<a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/14/P2026101400252_photo_1340993.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/14/P2026101400252_photo_1340993.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;<a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/14/P2026101400252_photo_1340994.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/14/P2026101400252_photo_1340994.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;<a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/14/P2026101400252_photo_1340995.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/14/P2026101400252_photo_1340995.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;<a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/14/P2026101400252_photo_1340996.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/14/P2026101400252_photo_1340996.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;<a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/14/P2026101400252_photo_1340997.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/14/P2026101400252_photo_1340997.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;<a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/14/P2026101400252_photo_1340998.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/14/P2026101400252_photo_1340998.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;<a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/14/P2026101400252_photo_1340999.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/14/P2026101400252_photo_1340999.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;</div>]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[LCQ 6: Confluence and Elevation - School Excellence Project  ]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/14/P2026101400523.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/14/P2026101400523.htm</link><pubDate>Wed, 14 Oct 2026 16:35:00 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Following is a question by the Hon Tang Fei and a reply by the Secretary for Education, Dr Choi Yuk-lin, in the Legislative Council today (October 14):<br />
&nbsp;<br />
Question:<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Given the changes in the school-age population, the Chief Executive has proposed the &quot;Confluence and Elevation - School Excellence Project&quot;&nbsp;in the 2026 Policy Address to encourage mergers of suitable publicly-funded primary and secondary schools, consolidating resources and raising the quality of education. Regarding the specific arrangements of the Project, will the Government inform this Council:<br />
&nbsp;<br />
(1) when the specific implementation timetable, eligibility criteria and vetting and approval criteria of the Project will be announced; how the authorities will assess the scope of operation and resource integration of schools after mergers, so as to ensure that the Project achieves the policy objective of raising the quality of education;<br />
&nbsp;<br />
(2) what measures the authorities have in place to support schools in addressing the issues of manpower deployment for teachers and utilisation of school premises arising from mergers, including whether transitional arrangements for teachers will be provided, and whether the consolidation of school premises and operational resources will be co-ordinated, thereby assisting school sponsoring bodies in implementing mergers smoothly and raising the quality of education; and<br />
&nbsp;<br />
(3) whether the Project will allow mergers of schools under different funding modes; if so, how the authorities will formulate proper transition arrangements in respect of tuition fees, student admission mechanism, utilisation of school premises and resource deployment when handling mergers involving aided and Direct Subsidy Scheme schools, so as to assist the schools concerned in achieving a smooth transition?<br />
&nbsp;<br />
Reply:<br />
<br />
President,<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The National 15th Five-Year Plan expressly advocates expanding excellence and enhancing quality in basic education. It also emphasises the importance of stepping up the dynamic adjustment of resources for basic education and their cross-level reallocation to address surpluses and shortfalls, whilst enhancing the quality of teaching and learning. To align with the National 15th Five-Year Plan, Hong Kong&#39;s First Five-Year Plan sets out a development direction for continuous enhancement of the quality of basic education in the face of the challenges brought about by a structural decline in school-age population. As announced in the 2026 Policy Address, the Education Bureau (EDB) will launch the &quot;Confluence and Elevation - School Excellence Project&quot;&nbsp;for publicly-funded schools within the 2026/27 school year, which aims to optimise the allocation of education resources and develop quality education in response to parents&#39; aspirations for high-quality schools.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;In fact, schools are places for nurturing the whole-person development of students. Maintaining an appropriate size of the overall student population and the number of classes are beneficial to creating a conducive teaching and learning environment and providing opportunities for group learning to facilitate students&#39; all-round development. To this end, the Project aims to encourage schools to strive for expanding excellence and enhancing quality, consolidate their strengths for better synergy and expand their operation scale, thereby achieving the synergy effect of &quot;one plus one is greater than two&quot; in quality enhancement. The EDB will introduce more facilitation measures and remove policy barriers for publicly-funded schools approved to participate in the Project, so as to support them in seeking confluence and elevation.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Our reply to the question raised by the Hon Tang Fei is as follows:<br />
<br />
(1) and (2) It is expected that schools participating in the Project will undergo a comprehensive upgrade of their physical infrastructure, such as school premises and facilities, and allocate abundant manpower and resources to provide students with a higher quality of education. At present, the EDB is collecting views on the support and facilitation measures under the Project from representatives of the school sector, with a view to finalising the implementation details, such as whether there is a need to set an application threshold based on school scale (e.g. a requirement on the number of approved classes), the feasibility of merging schools across different finance types, whether arrangements will be in place for the flexible use of school premises upon merger, as well as the provision of additional resources and manpower during the transitional period. The EDB will work swiftly to sort out and refine the details, with a target of launching the Project within the 2026/27 school year for application by eligible schools. We will prudently consider each application to ensure that the approved cases are in line with the policy objectives and principles.<br />
<br />
(3) Based on past experience, there has been a merger between a Direct Subsidy Scheme (DSS) secondary school and an aided secondary school, after which the merged school has continued to operate as an aided school. Currently, the EDB is studying the feasible options for joint operation of schools of different finance types to ensure that the numbers of both aided and DSS schools will not increase following the mergers, so as to stabilise the education ecosystem. As for government schools, only mergers among government schools are possible given their unique staff establishment and finance type.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;We have to emphasise that, regardless of whether the schools participating in the Project are under the same school sponsoring bodies (SSBs), they must give due consideration to the district and school circumstances as well as students&#39; interests, and maintain communication with stakeholders prior to making a decision to join the Project. For merger proposals involving schools of different finance types, the schools must thoroughly consult various stakeholders (including school staff, parents and students) about relevant arrangements, such as the appointment terms for staff, tuition fees, school premises and curriculum arrangements. Merger proposals should be taken forward subject to effective communication with stakeholders.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The EDB established the Task Force on Supporting the Sustainable Development of Public Sector Schools in March 2026, which is led by a directorate officer of the EDB and comprises officers from the School Development Sections of the Regional Education Offices of the EDB, experienced retired principals and stakeholder representatives. The Task Force provides professional advice for the school sector on planning the way forward and assists schools in achieving a smooth transition and continuous development. Moreover, members of the Task Force play a facilitating and supporting role in the process. Apart from helping SSBs reach a consensus with stakeholders after thorough communication, the members also offer suitable advice based on schools&#39; actual needs and support them in consolidating resources as well as taking forward the implementation and related arrangements for the merger proposals in light of local circumstances.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;President, the EDB&#39;s aim in addressing the structural decline in school-age population is crystal clear. We will accord top priority to the interests of students while ensuring healthy and sustainable development of our education system. Amid the structural decline in school-age population, apart from our efforts to strive for a &quot;soft landing&quot;, the EDB must seek to advance the development of quality education in Hong Kong to turn a possible crisis into opportunities. Therefore, we encourage SSBs to plan ahead for long-term school development and support their proactive formulation of plans to consolidate resources and enhance the quality of education.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The EDB will maintain communication with the sector and continue to review the prevailing policies and options with respect to class structures and school development with a focus on enhancing the overall education quality, so as to provide students with more opportunities to receive quality education in schools of higher quality, larger scale and higher efficiency.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Thank you, President.]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[Hong Kong Customs steps up enforcement operations to combat smuggling activities to Macao by river trade vessels (with photos)]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/14/P2026101400504.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/14/P2026101400504.htm</link><pubDate>Wed, 14 Oct 2026 16:35:00 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;​Hong Kong Customs has conducted special operations since late September to strengthen enforcement against sea smuggling activities to Macao by river trade vessels.<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;During the operations, Hong Kong&nbsp;Customs detected three smuggling cases and seized a large batch of smuggled goods, including about 790 000 suspected controlled pharmaceutical products, about 150 000 suspected controlled injections, and about 142 kilograms of cigars and tobacco, with an estimated market value of over $77 million.<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Investigations of the above-mentioned cases are ongoing. The likelihood of arrests is not ruled out.<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;During the period, Hong Kong Customs also provided intelligence to Macao Customs and conducted joint enforcement operations. During the operations, Macao Customs detected two cases involving river trade vessels smuggling goods from Hong Kong to Macao in early October, seizing a large batch of smuggled medicines, health supplements and cosmetics, etc, with an estimated market value of about MOP$2.7 million (about HK$2.62 million).<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Being a government department primarily responsible for tackling smuggling activities, Hong Kong&nbsp;Customs has long been combating various smuggling activities on all fronts. Hong Kong&nbsp;Customs will keep up its enforcement action and continue to resolutely combat sea smuggling activities through proactive risk management and intelligence-based enforcement strategies, and carry out targeted anti-smuggling operations at suitable times to crack down on relevant crimes.<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Smuggling is a serious offence. Under the Import and Export Ordinance, any person found guilty of importing or exporting unmanifested cargo is liable upon conviction to a maximum fine of $2 million and imprisonment for seven years. Any person who exports pharmaceutical products and medicines without a valid export licence commits an offence. The maximum penalty upon conviction is a fine of $500,000 and imprisonment for two years.<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Members of the public may report any suspected smuggling activities to Customs&#39; 24-hour hotline 182 8080 or its dedicated crime-reporting email account (<a href="mailto:crimereport@customs.gov.hk" target="_blank">crimereport@customs.gov.hk</a>) or online form (<a href="https://eform.cefs.gov.hk/form/ced002/en/" target="_blank">eform.cefs.gov.hk/form/ced002</a>).<div id="photos"><a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/14/P2026101400504_photo_1341118.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/14/P2026101400504_photo_1341118.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;<a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/14/P2026101400504_photo_1341119.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/14/P2026101400504_photo_1341119.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;<a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/14/P2026101400504_photo_1341120.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/14/P2026101400504_photo_1341120.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;</div>]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[Analytical Accounts of Exchange Fund]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/14/P2026101400251.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/14/P2026101400251.htm</link><pubDate>Wed, 14 Oct 2026 16:30:00 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[The following is issued on behalf of the Hong Kong Monetary Authority:<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;​The Hong Kong Monetary Authority (HKMA) released today (October 14) the key analytical accounts of the Exchange Fund at the end of September 2026.<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Foreign assets, representing the external assets of the Exchange Fund, decreased during the month by HK$76.7 billion to HK$3,576.0 billion.<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The Monetary Base, comprising Certificates of Indebtedness, Government‑issued currency notes and coins in circulation, the balance of the banking system and Exchange Fund Bills and Notes issued, amounted to HK$2,090.2 billion.<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Claims on the private sector in Hong Kong amounted to HK$473.0&nbsp;billion.<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Foreign liabilities amounted to HK$135.3 billion.<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The analytical accounts of the Exchange Fund are released in accordance with the International Monetary Fund&#39;s Special Data Dissemination Standard (SDDS) and are referred to as the Analytical Accounts of the Central Bank under SDDS (Annex).<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The release calendar of the Analytical Accounts of the Exchange Fund for 2027 is announced below:<br />
&nbsp;
<table border="0">
	<tbody>
		<tr>
			<td style="width:192px">Reference Month</td>
			<td style="width:198px">Release Date</td>
		</tr>
