]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[LCQ13: Expediting digitalisation of trade documents]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/14/P2026101400512.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/14/P2026101400512.htm</link><pubDate>Wed, 14 Oct 2026 16:18:00 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Following is a question by Dr the Hon Thomas So and a written reply by the Secretary for Commerce and Economic Development, Mr Algernon Yau, in the Legislative Council today (October 14):<br />
&nbsp;<br />
Question:<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; There are views pointing out that with the Government&#39;s active support, the development of the first two phases of the Trade Single Window (TSW) and the related services have yielded positive results, but progress in the digitalisation of &quot;Business-to-Business&quot; (B2B) trade documents, which account for the vast majority of all trade documents, remains relatively slow. While the Government already issued a consultation paper between late 2025 and early 2026 on legislative amendments to facilitate the digitalisation of B2B trade documents, a legal basis and official verification standards are still lacking. In this connection, will the Government inform this Council:<br />
&nbsp;<br />
(1) given that the Government has previously indicated that it does not recommend the introduction of a mandatory accreditation or regulatory regime at this stage for establishing the reliability of electronic trade documents owing to the current lack of a leading technological solution in the market, but there are views suggesting that Hong Kong has a responsibility to lead and promote the formulation of the Guangdong-Hong Kong-Macao Greater Bay Area (GBA) Standards for electronic trade documents, whether the Government will promote technological development for the digitalisation of B2B trade documents in such directions as establishing an official regulatory and accreditation framework in the future;<br />
&nbsp;<br />
(2) as there are views pointing out that &quot;ValidAP&quot;, the Government&#39;s platform for the cross-boundary validation of electronic trade documents, has achieved preliminary results in promoting commercial data verification, and the Government is also pressing ahead with Phase 3 of the TSW at full steam, whether the Government will consider adopting existing cross-&zwj;boundary data verification technology as the basis and taking the lead in developing a comprehensive electronic accreditation and mutual data recognition system covering B2B commercial certificates and transaction records such as electronic bills of lading (eBLs) and commercial invoices; and<br />
&nbsp;<br />
(3) as the Project Ensemble launched by the Hong Kong Monetary Authority (HKMA) already covers use cases such as trade finance and eBLs, and the HKMA has signed the Memorandum of Understanding between Shanghai and Hong Kong on Digitised Cargo Trade and Finance Cooperation with the Shanghai Data Bureau and the National Technology Innovation Center for Blockchain, whether the Government will further consolidate existing resources and establish an interdepartmental cross-boundary digital trade sandbox jointly with the Commerce and Economic Development Bureau, the Customs and Excise Department and the Innovation and Technology Commission to enable the logistics, trading, banking and insurance sectors to conduct trials on a common platform for unifying standards on electronic trade documents and data, and connect it to the Project Ensemble and the initiative on Shanghai-Hong Kong co-operation on eBLs, with a view to expediting the formation of digital trade rules recognised in the GBA and even the international community?&nbsp;<br />
<br />
Reply:<br />
&nbsp;<br />
President,<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; In consultation with the Customs and Excise Department, and the Hong Kong Monetary Authority (HKMA), the consolidated reply to the question raised by Dr the Hon Thomas So is as follows:<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; The Government has been committed to promoting the development of digital trade in order to maintain and enhance Hong Kong&#39;s position as an international trade centre. With reference to the Model Law on Electronic Transferable Records (MLETR) advocated by the United Nations Commission on International Trade Law, the Government is advancing legislative amendments to facilitate the digitalisation of &quot;Business-to-Business&quot; (B2B) trade documents, and will introduce an amendment bill to the Electronic Transactions Ordinance (Cap. 553) into the Legislative Council within this year. In taking forward the work relating to the digitalisation of trade documents, the Government has been, and will continue to be, actively drawing reference from the practices of other jurisdictions.<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; The MLETR adopts a functional approach and provides a legal framework to ensure that electronic transferable records (ETRs) possess the necessary characteristics to achieve their legal effect and functions, while upholding the principle of technological neutrality by not prescribing the use of any particular technology or platform. The advantage of this approach is that it affords flexibility to accommodate rapidly evolving technologies and business models, and facilitates cross-border interoperability.<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Specifically, the MLETR provides that a reliable method must be used to establish that an ETR may be regarded as equivalent to a paper-based document. In defining &quot;reliable method&quot;, the provisions of the MLETR set out a range of factors for consideration, including the security of hardware and software, the ability to prevent unauthorised access, and applicable industry standards. To enhance legal certainty, the MLETR also advocates a &quot;proven-in-fact&quot; approach, under which a method need not be assessed against the criteria of the factor-based evaluation if it has in fact been proven capable of achieving its intended function.<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Currently, the more common international practice is for each jurisdiction to provide a legal framework for ETRs that is consistent with international standards (including the MLETR), while, in accordance with the principles of technology-and-medium neutrality, allowing enterprises to use and develop different technological solutions, thereby driving the market to develop technologies and systems that meet practical needs.<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; The Government has been promoting trade digitalisation through improving the legal framework, upgrading trade infrastructure and facilitating cross-boundary data interoperability. The Trade Single Window, as Hong Kong&#39;s digital trade infrastructure, enables the industry to submit trade documents to the Government on a one-stop basis. ValidAP, on the other hand, provides cross-boundary electronic trade document authentication services to verify, among other things, whether an electronic document has been issued by the relevant authority. The two platforms each have their own policy objectives and service scopes, and differ from the proposed end-to-end electronic authentication and mutual recognition system covering B2B transactions.<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Given that relevant technological solutions are already available in the market, and that technologies and business models continue to evolve, the Government&#39;s current focus is to establish a clear legal framework for the recognition of electronic trade documents and promoting cross-boundary interoperability, thereby creating favourable conditions for the industry to adopt and develop different solutions. It might not be the most cost-effective use of public resources if the Government were to develop or construct similar systems on its own. Regarding the introduction of a mandatory accreditation regime, full consideration must also be given to its impact on enterprise choice and market innovation. At present, other jurisdictions generally have not introduced mandatory accreditation regimes. We will uphold the principle of technological neutrality, allowing enterprises to select suitable solutions according to their business needs, and will continue to monitor international developments and the associated benefits.<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; The Government understands that promoting cross-boundary interoperability with the Guangdong-Hong Kong-Macao Greater Bay Area and the Chinese Mainland as a whole is of great importance to Hong Kong&#39;s commercial and trade development. In recent years, the Chinese Mainland has actively promoted the trials and the application of electronic trade documents, including amending the Maritime Code of the People&#39;s Republic of China (Maritime Code) in October 2025 to provide legal status for electronic transport records such as electronic bills of lading. The amended Maritime Code, which was also formulated with reference to the MLETR and relevant international conventions, has been implemented since May 2026. Upon completion of the relevant legislative amendments, Hong Kong will be able to enhance the cross-boundary acceptability of such documents with the Chinese Mainland.<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; On the other hand, the HKMA is advancing trade finance digitalisation through the multi-year Project CargoX. With the participation of 21 banks, the CargoX Pilot Programme was launched in May 2026 to facilitate the iterative validation of cargo and trade data initiatives. In 2026 and 2027, the Pilot Programme focuses on four key areas, including integrating with key government cargo and trade data platforms (such as the Transport and Logistics Bureau&#39;s Port Community System and the Commerce and Economic Development Bureau&#39;s Trade Single Window), combining small and medium-sized enterprises&#39; trade flow and cash flow data for banks&#39; multi-dimensional credit assessment, adopting Digital Corporate Identity to enable trusted data sharing, and enhancing connectivity with key trade corridors, such as the Chinese Mainland and the Association of Southeast Asian Nations.<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; In this connection, the HKMA has collaborated with the Shanghai Data Bureau (SDB) to successfully establish a link-up between the HKMA&#39;s Commercial Data Interchange and a digital trade platform developed by the SDB. Known as &quot;CargoX Connect&quot;, this collaboration has enabled five pilot banks to access and utilise trade declaration data from the Yangtze River Delta region to facilitate various trade finance processes, such as trade verification and due diligence on trade counterparts, resulting in faster loan drawdowns for Hong Kong businesses engaging in both onshore and offshore trade. To date, this pilot project has enabled the approval of 10 trade finance loans amounting to $180 million. The HKMA intends to extend this successful model to other provinces and cities in the Chinese Mainland as the next phase of development. The Government will continue to monitor standard developments both internationally and in the Chinese Mainland to provide a foundation for cross-boundary interoperability.]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[DH urges members of public to maintain hygiene amid increasing local activity of hand, foot and mouth disease]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/14/P2026101400516.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/14/P2026101400516.htm</link><pubDate>Wed, 14 Oct 2026 16:12:00 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; The Centre for Health Protection (CHP) of the Department of Health (DH) today (October 14) reminded the public to maintain strict personal and environmental hygiene, given the significant increase in local hand, foot and mouth disease (HFMD) activity recently, to prevent infection. The CHP will issue letters to schools today, updating them on the latest situation regarding HFMD. Schools and institutions should report any outbreak to the CHP immediately.<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The CHP&#39;s latest surveillance data showed a gradual increase in the number of HFMD outbreaks in institutions and schools, from eight (affecting 27 persons) per week in mid-September (September 13 to 19), to 12 outbreaks (affecting 41 persons), 11 outbreaks (affecting 26 persons), and 20 outbreaks (affecting 48 persons) per week in the past three weeks (i.e. September 20 to 26, September 27 to October 3, and October 4 to 10 respectively). During the first three days of this week (October 11 to 13), nine outbreaks affecting 24 persons have been reported. Ninety per cent of the outbreaks occurred in Child Care Centres/Kindergartens and primary schools.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Additionally, the CHP&#39;s sentinel surveillance based at <a href="https://www.chp.gov.hk/en/statistics/data/10/26/44/151.html" target="_blank">Child Care Centres/Kindergartens</a> indicated a significant increase in HFMD activity over the past week.&nbsp;<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;HFMD is a common disease in children caused by enteroviruses (EVs) such as Coxsackie virus and EV71. HFMD occurs throughout the year in Hong Kong, with the usual peak occurring from May to July and October to December. Institutions have higher chances to encounter outbreaks as HFMD can spread more easily among young children with more close contact. As there has been an increase in HFMD activity, the CHP reminded parents that they should stay alert to the health condition of their young children.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Most HFMD patients have mild symptoms, and the disease usually begins with fever, poor appetite, tiredness and sore throat. Painful sores may develop in the mouth one to two days after the onset of fever and then often become ulcers. Most patients recover on their own in seven to 10 days.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The CHP reminded schools that they are required to follow the&nbsp;<a href="https://www.chp.gov.hk/files/pdf/guidelines_on_prevention_of_communicable_diseases_in_schools_kindergartens_kindergartens_cum_child_care-centres_child_are_centres.pdf" target="_blank">Guidelines on Prevention of Communicable Diseases in Schools/Kindergartens/Kindergartens-cum-Child Care Centres/Child Care Centres</a>&nbsp;on preventive and control measures as well as management of outbreaks, which should be reported to the CHP as soon as possible for follow-up. Management of venues with play facilities should refer to the CHP&#39;s&nbsp;<a href="https://www.chp.gov.hk/files/pdf/health_advice_play_facilities.pdf" target="_blank">Public Health Advice for Play Facilities</a>&nbsp;on appropriate infection control in activities involving young children under&nbsp;6 during the peak season of HFMD.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Moreover, alcohol-based handrub should not substitute hand hygiene with liquid soap and water, as alcohol does not effectively kill some viruses causing HFMD, for example, EV71.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;To prevent HFMD, members of the public (especially those in the management of schools and institutions) should take heed of the following preventive measures:<br />
&nbsp;
<ul>
	<li>Maintain good air circulation;</li>
	<li>Keep hands clean and wash hands properly. Wash hands before meals, after going to the toilet, when they are soiled by respiratory secretions, such as after sneezing, or after handling diapers or other stool-soiled materials;</li>
	<li>Cover the nose and mouth while sneezing or coughing and dispose of nasal and oral discharges properly;</li>
	<li>Regularly clean and disinfect frequently touched surfaces such as furniture, toys and commonly shared items with 1:99 diluted household bleach (mixing one part of household bleach containing 5.25 per cent sodium hypochlorite with 99 parts of water), leave for 15 to 30 minutes, and then rinse with water and keep dry;</li>
	<li>Use absorbent disposable towels to wipe away obvious contaminants such as respiratory secretions, vomitus or excreta, and then disinfect the surface and neighbouring areas with 1:49 diluted household bleach (mixing one part of bleach containing 5.25 per cent sodium hypochlorite with 49 parts of water), leave for 15 to 30 minutes and then rinse with water and keep dry;</li>
	<li>Avoid going to overcrowded places;</li>
	<li>Children who are ill should be kept out of school until their fever and rashes have subsided and all vesicles have dried and crusted; and</li>
	<li>Parents should maintain close communication with schools to let them know the latest situation of sick children.</li>
</ul>
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The CHP&#39;s weekly publication, <a href="https://www.chp.gov.hk/en/resources/29/502.html" target="_blank">EV SCAN</a>, is issued every Friday to report the latest local situation of HFMD. Members of the public may also visit the CHP&#39;s&nbsp;<a href="https://www.chp.gov.hk/en/view_content/16354.html" target="_blank">page</a>&nbsp;for more information on&nbsp;HFMD and EV71 infection.]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[Orders on Comprehensive Avoidance of Double Taxation Agreements with six countries to be gazetted]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/14/P2026101400352.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/14/P2026101400352.htm</link><pubDate>Wed, 14 Oct 2026 16:00:00 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;​The Chief Executive in Council made six Orders under the Inland Revenue Ordinance (Cap. 112) on October 6, 2026, to implement respectively the Comprehensive Avoidance of Double Taxation Agreements (CDTAs) that Hong Kong signed with Barbados, Jordan, the Kyrgyz Republic, Maldives, Norway and Rwanda. The Orders will be gazetted on October 16.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&quot;The CDTAs ensure that residents of Hong Kong and the relevant tax jurisdictions will not be subject to double taxation on the same income, and enable investors to assess more effectively the potential tax burden arising from their cross-border economic activities, thereby promoting bilateral trade and investment,&quot; a spokesman for the Financial Services and the Treasury Bureau said.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The Orders will be tabled at the Legislative Council on October 21 for negative vetting. The six CDTAs will enter into force after both Hong Kong and the relevant tax jurisdictions have completed their respective ratification procedures. Highlights of the CDTAs are set out at Annex.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Hong Kong signed the CDTAs with Maldives, Jordan, Rwanda and Norway in May, September, October and December 2025 respectively, and with the Kyrgyz Republic and Barbados in March 2026. Hong Kong has signed CDTAs with 60 jurisdictions, 15 of which were signed by the current-term Government.]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[Effective Exchange Rate Index]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/14/P2026101400210.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/14/P2026101400210.htm</link><pubDate>Wed, 14 Oct 2026 16:00:00 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The effective exchange rate index for the Hong Kong dollar on Wednesday, October 14, 2026 is 100.4 (same as yesterday&#39;s index).]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[Two persons sentenced to seven and five months' imprisonment respectively for illegally importing alternative smoking products]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/14/P2026101400520.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/14/P2026101400520.htm</link><pubDate>Wed, 14 Oct 2026 15:55:49 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;A woman and a man were sentenced today (October 14) at the West Kowloon Magistrates&#39; Courts to seven and five months&#39; imprisonment respectively for illegally importing 40 000 and 33 000 alternative smoking products. The Department of Health (DH) welcomed the verdict and reminded members of the public and visitors not to bring alternative smoking products such as e-cigarettes, heated tobacco products or herbal cigarettes into Hong Kong, and not to use any alternative smoking products.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;The Tobacco and Alcohol Control Office (TACO) of the DH was notified by Hong Kong Customs yesterday (October 13) that 40 000 and 33 000 alternative smoking products had been intercepted in the luggage of the aforesaid individuals arriving from Japan respectively. TACO immediately arrested and prosecuted them.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;Since the amendments to the&nbsp;Tobacco Control Legislation (Amendment) Ordinance 2025, which conferred arrest powers on TACO inspectors,&nbsp;took effect on September 19, 2025, TACO has prosecuted 32 cases involving the importation of large quantities of alternative smoking products. Thirty-five persons have been convicted and sentenced to prison terms ranging from four weeks to eight months.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;According to the Import and Export Ordinance (Cap. 60), a person who imports alternative smoking products, including electronic smoking products, heated tobacco products and herbal cigarettes, commits an offence and is&nbsp;liable on summary conviction to a fine of $500,000 and imprisonment for two years; or liable on conviction on indictment to a fine of $2 million and imprisonment for seven years.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;Under the Smoking (Public Health) Ordinance (Cap. 371), no person may promote, manufacture, sell, or possess for commercial purposes alternative smoking products. An offender is liable to a fine of $50,000 and imprisonment for six months.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;TACO will continue to closely monitor and enforce the law to combat related offences.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;In addition, the DH also cautions the public that alternative smoking products are addictive and are not effective smoking cessation or harm reduction tools. E-cigarettes may increase the risk of cancer, respiratory diseases, and cardiovascular diseases. Smokers are urged to quit smoking as early as possible for their own health and that of others. For assistance, please call the DH&#39;s Integrated Smoking Cessation Hotline on 1833 183, or visit&nbsp;<a href="https://www.livetobaccofree.hk/en/index.html" target="_blank">www.livetobaccofree.hk</a>&nbsp;for information on quitting.]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[Inspection of aquatic products imported from Japan]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/14/P2026101400535.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/14/P2026101400535.htm</link><pubDate>Wed, 14 Oct 2026 15:48:00 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;In response to the Japanese Government&#39;s plan to discharge nuclear-contaminated water at the Fukushima Nuclear Power Station, the Director of Food and Environmental Hygiene issued a Food Safety Order which prohibits all aquatic products, sea salt and seaweeds originating from the 10 metropolis/prefectures, namely Tokyo, Fukushima, Ibaraki, Miyagi, Chiba, Gunma, Tochigi, Niigata, Nagano and Saitama, from being imported into and supplied in Hong Kong.<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;For other Japanese aquatic products, sea salt and seaweeds that are not prohibited from being imported into Hong Kong, the Centre for Food Safety (CFS) of the Food and Environmental Hygiene Department is conducting comprehensive radiological tests to verify that the radiation levels of these products do not exceed the guideline levels before they are allowed to be supplied in the market.<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;From noon on October 13 to noon today (October 14), the CFS conducted tests on the radiological levels of 211 food samples imported from Japan, which were of the &quot;aquatic and related products, seaweeds and sea salt&quot; category. No sample was found to have exceeded the safety limit. Details can be found on the CFS&#39;s thematic website titled &quot;Control Measures on Foods Imported from Japan&quot; (<a href="https://www.cfs.gov.hk/english/programme/programme_rafs/programme_rafs_fc_01_30_Nuclear_Event_and_Food_Safety.html" target="_blank">www.cfs.gov.hk/english/programme/programme_rafs/programme_rafs_fc_01_30_Nuclear_Event_and_Food_Safety.html</a>).<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;In parallel, the Agriculture, Fisheries and Conservation Department (AFCD) has also tested 50 samples of local catch for radiological levels. All the samples passed the tests. Details can be found on the AFCD&#39;s website (<a href="https://www.afcd.gov.hk/english/fisheries/Radiological_testing/Radiological_Test.html" target="_blank">www.afcd.gov.hk/english/fisheries/Radiological_testing/Radiological_Test.html</a>).<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;The Hong Kong Observatory (HKO) has also enhanced the environmental monitoring of the local waters. No anomaly has been detected so far. For details, please refer to the HKO&#39;s website (<a href="https://www.hko.gov.hk/en/radiation/monitoring/seawater.html" target="_blank">www.hko.gov.hk/en/radiation/monitoring/seawater.html</a>).<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;From August 24, 2023, to noon today, the CFS and the AFCD have conducted tests on the radiological levels of 267 637 samples of food imported from Japan (including 166 598 samples of aquatic and related products, seaweeds and sea salt) and 57 010 samples of local catch respectively. All the samples passed the tests.]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[LCQ18: Support measures for carers]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/14/P2026101400289.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/14/P2026101400289.htm</link><pubDate>Wed, 14 Oct 2026 15:40:00 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;​Following is a question by the Hon Elaine Chik and a written reply by the Secretary for Labour and Welfare, Mr Chris Sun, in the Legislative Council today (October 14):<br />
<br />
Question:<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;In the 2026 Policy Address, the Government announced that the amount of allowance under the Scheme on Living Allowance for Carers of Elderly Persons from Low-income Families and the Scheme on Living Allowance for Low-income Carers of Persons with Disabilities (Allowance Schemes) would be increased by 10 per cent to $3,300 per month. However, there are views that despite the large number of unpaid carers in Hong Kong, the current number of beneficiaries under the Allowance Schemes is rather small, and that the existing community support for carers is seriously inadequate, thereby placing many families in which elderly persons are caring for other elderly persons or persons with disabilities under considerable pressure. In this connection, will the Government inform this Council:<br />
<br />
(1) of the actual number of beneficiaries and the total amount of allowances disbursed under the two aforesaid Allowance Schemes in each of the past three financial years, and the respective percentages of the numbers of such beneficiaries in the total number of unpaid carers and the total number of families of persons with disabilities in Hong Kong;<br />
<br />
(2) whether, when determining and adjusting the allowance amount, the authorities have obtained data on the average actual monthly expenditure incurred by long-term carers on items such as medical consumables, transport for accompanying care recipients to medical appointments and day respite services; if so, of the details; if not, the objective criteria adopted by the authorities to assess whether the monthly allowance level of $3,300 is adequate to meet family needs;<br />
<br />
(3) of the number of applications rejected under the two aforesaid Allowance Schemes in each of the past three financial years, with a breakdown by major reasons for rejection, including household income exceeding the prescribed limit, receipt of other public benefits, failure to meet the requirement of providing at least 80 hours of care per month, and the care recipient not being on the Central Waiting List for Subsidised Long Term Care Services;<br />
<br />
(4) whether the authorities have compiled statistics on the number of carers in Hong Kong who are ineligible to apply for carer allowance by virtue of their current receipt of Comprehensive Social Security Assistance (CSSA), Old Age Living Allowance or Disability Allowance (other allowances); given that subsistence allowances for the grassroots and support for carers are different in nature, whether the authorities will conduct a comprehensive review and remove the restriction that precludes recipients of CSSA or other allowances from applying for carer allowance; if not, of the reasons for that;<br />
<br />
(5) whether, during the transitional period prior to the formal commissioning of the inter-departmental &quot;welfare information sharing e-platform&quot;, the authorities will establish an inter-departmental mechanism and specific indicators to proactively identify and reach out to hidden high-risk families in which elderly persons are caring for other elderly persons or for persons with disabilities, so as to address the current passive situation of inadequate publicity for the Allowance Schemes and sole reliance on self-initiated applications by members of the public; if not, what other measures the authorities will adopt to enhance publicity; and<br />
<br />
(6) whether the authorities will set up &quot;one-stop carer support centres&quot; in the 18 districts across the territory, study the implementation of a &quot;Carer Pass&quot; to provide public healthcare services and transport concessions, and collaborate with community groups to jointly establish a community safety net; if not, of the reasons for that?<br />
<br />
Reply:<br />
<br />
President,<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;The current-term Government has spared no effort in introducing a range of welfare services and financial assistance to support carers of elderly persons and carers of persons with disabilities, including the enhancement measures announced in the 2026 Policy Address. The Government will continue to expand support channels and increase service capacity, and apply smart technology and big data, so as to put in place a more comprehensive and sustainable suite of carer support measures, and enhance the ability to identify high-risk cases and intervene in crises.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;Our reply to the various parts of the question is as follows:<br />
<br />
(1) and (3) The Scheme on Living Allowance for Carers of Elderly Persons from Low-income Families (the Allowance Scheme for Carers of the Elderly) and the Scheme on Living Allowance for Low-income Carers of Persons with Disabilities (the Allowance Scheme for Carers of Persons with Disabilities) aim to provide a cash allowance to eligible carers of elderly persons or carers of persons with disabilities, so as to help supplement their living expenses. To strengthen support for carers, the 2026 Policy Address announced that the amount of the two allowances would be increased by 10 per cent to $3,300 per month. An eligible applicant who takes care of more than one elderly person or person with disabilities may receive up to $6,600 per month.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;The two carer allowance schemes were incorporated into the Government&#39;s regular assistance programmes in October 2023. In the past three financial years, the expenses were $110 million, $321 million and $396 million respectively. The annual number of beneficiaries and the breakdown of ineligible cases in the respective years are set out in Table 1 and Table 2. The Government does not maintain the total number of unpaid carers in Hong Kong, and therefore cannot calculate the percentage of which the beneficiaries of the two allowance schemes account for.<br />
<br />
Table 1: Number of beneficiaries under the two carer allowance schemes<br />
&nbsp;
<table border="1" style="width:625px">
	<tbody>
		<tr>
			<td colspan="1" rowspan="2">&nbsp;</td>
			<td colspan="3" rowspan="1">Allowance Scheme for<br />
			Carers of the Elderly</td>
			<td colspan="3" rowspan="1">Allowance Scheme for<br />
			Carers of Persons with Disabilities</td>
		</tr>
		<tr>
			<td>2023-24</td>
			<td>2024-25</td>
			<td>2025-26</td>
			<td>2023-24</td>
			<td>2024-25</td>
			<td>2025-26</td>
		</tr>
		<tr>
			<td>No. of beneficiaries</td>
			<td>4 194 (Note)</td>
			<td>5 507</td>
			<td>7 094</td>
			<td>3 213 (Note)</td>
			<td>3 751</td>
			<td>3 983</td>
		</tr>
	</tbody>
</table>
<br />
Note: Counted from the regularisation of the schemes in October 2023. All cases receiving an allowance under the pilot schemes before regularisation were required to submit fresh applications after October 2023. This number includes relevant eligible applications.<br />
<br />
Table 2: Number of ineligible cases and reasons for ineligibility under the two carer allowance schemes<br />
&nbsp;
<table border="1" style="width:625px">
	<tbody>
		<tr>
			<td colspan="1" rowspan="2">Reason for ineligibility</td>
			<td colspan="3" rowspan="1">Allowance Scheme for<br />
			Carers of the Elderly</td>
			<td colspan="3" rowspan="1">Allowance Scheme for<br />
			Carers of Persons with Disabilities</td>
		</tr>
		<tr>
			<td>2023-24 (Note 1)</td>
			<td>2024-25</td>
			<td>2025-26</td>
			<td>2023-24 (Note 1)</td>
			<td>2024-25</td>
			<td>2025-26</td>
		</tr>
		<tr>
			<td>(i) Care recipient not waitlisted for the relevant services (Note 2)</td>
			<td>2</td>
			<td>35</td>
			<td>270</td>
			<td>55</td>
			<td>82</td>
			<td>249</td>
		</tr>
		<tr>
			<td>(ii) Care recipient already receiving residential care services</td>
			<td>28</td>
			<td>26</td>
			<td>19</td>
			<td>32</td>
			<td>21</td>
			<td>25</td>
		</tr>
		<tr>
			<td>(iii) Applicant&#39;s household income exceeding the limit</td>
			<td>43</td>
			<td>19</td>
			<td>24</td>
			<td>67</td>
			<td>16</td>
			<td>9</td>
		</tr>
		<tr>
			<td>(iv)&nbsp;Application withdrawn, or insufficient information provided for vetting</td>
			<td>51</td>
			<td>41</td>
			<td>60</td>
			<td>48</td>
			<td>20</td>
			<td>31</td>
		</tr>
		<tr>
			<td>(v)&nbsp;Applicant receiving the Old Age Living Allowance (OALA) or Comprehensive Social Security Assistance (CSSA)</td>
			<td>262</td>
			<td>120</td>
			<td>96</td>
			<td>115</td>
			<td>24</td>
			<td>21</td>
		</tr>
		<tr>
			<td>(vi)&nbsp;Applicant receiving the Disability Allowance (DA) (Note 3)</td>
			<td>47</td>
			<td>17</td>
			<td>26</td>
			<td>34</td>
			<td>14</td>
			<td>17</td>
		</tr>
		<tr>
			<td>(vii) Applicant unable to undertake the caring responsibility, except for reason in item (vi) (Note 3)</td>
			<td>17</td>
			<td>9</td>
			<td>24</td>
			<td>9</td>
			<td>7</td>
			<td>3</td>
		</tr>
		<tr>
			<td>(viii)&nbsp;Others (e.g. the applicant is not a Hong Kong resident, the care recipient has passed away, or the applicant and the care recipient are the same person, etc.)</td>
			<td>77</td>
			<td>31</td>
			<td>79</td>
			<td>13</td>
			<td>9</td>
			<td>6</td>
		</tr>
		<tr>
			<td>Total</td>
			<td>527</td>
			<td>298</td>
			<td>598</td>
			<td>373</td>
			<td>193</td>
			<td>361</td>
		</tr>
	</tbody>
</table>
<br />
Note 1: Counted from the regularisation of the schemes in October 2023.<br />
<br />
Note 2: Under the Allowance Scheme for Carers of the Elderly, at the time of application the elderly person being cared for must have been assessed under the Social Welfare Department (SWD)&#39;s Standardised Care Need Assessment Mechanism for Elderly Services (SCNAMES) and recommended as suitable for community care services and/or residential care services, and must still be waitlisted on the Central Waiting List (CWL) for subsidised long-term care services (that is, residential care services and/or community care services). Under the Allowance Scheme for Carers of Persons with Disabilities, at the time of application the person with disabilities being cared for must be waitlisted for any specified rehabilitation services of the SWD, the boarding placement of special schools under the Education Bureau (EDB), or the infirmary service under the Hospital Authority (HA).<br />
<br />
Note 3: Under the two allowance schemes, an applicant must be capable of taking up the caregiving role. Persons aged under 15, DA recipients, persons assessed and recommended under SCNAMES as suitable for community care services and/or residential care services, and persons waitlisted on the CWL for subsidised long-term care services or for any specified rehabilitation services of the SWD, the boarding placement of special schools under the EDB or the infirmary service under HA, will not be deemed as fit and capable carers, and are not eligible for the allowance under the schemes.<br />
<br />
(2) The Government will take into account a basket of factors, including inflation, the needs of recipients and the affordability of public finance, when reviewing the two allowance schemes and adjusting the amount of allowance as appropriate.<br />
<br />
(4) To ensure proper use of public funds and avoid duplication of resources, recipients of CSSA or OALA cannot concurrently receive carer allowances. The number of cases where applicants are ineligible for the two carer allowance schemes due to the above arrangement is set out in item (v) of Table 2. With regard to DA, Note 3 to Table 2 has stated that applicants for the two carer allowances must be capable of taking up caregiving responsibilities. DA recipients are not deemed as capable of taking up caregiving responsibilities, and are therefore ineligible for application. Relevant figures are set out in item (vi) of Table 2.<br />
<br />
(5) and (6) The SWD has, based on the information in the CWL, proactively issued invitation letters to eligible carers to apply for the allowance at the pilot stage before regularisation of the two allowance schemes and after regularisation. In addition, the SWD has also introduced online application forms to facilitate public access to relevant information and submission of applications at any time.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;To enhance the ability to identify high-risk carers and intervene in a crisis, the Government has actively applied smart technology and big data in recent years. The Labour and Welfare Bureau and the SWD established the Carer Support Data Platform in July 2025 and launched two schemes. Scheme I consolidates data of the SWD and the Hong Kong Housing Authority (HKHA) to identify high-risk households living in public housing, comprising singleton elderly persons and doubleton elderly persons, carers of elderly persons and carers of persons with disabilities who lack community support, and engages the District Services and Community Care Teams (Care Teams) to conduct home visits and provide support. These visits were extended in March 2026 to cover high-risk households living in &quot;three-nil&quot; buildings. As at the end of September 2026, Care Teams had contacted more than 36 300 high-risk households and made more than 4 200 referrals for welfare services. Scheme II links up data of the SWD, HA, the HKHA and the Hong Kong Housing Society, and has put in place a notification mechanism. When the SWD is notified that a carer has been admitted to a hospital, it will intervene proactively through the Designated Hotline for Carer Support 182 183 (Hotline) and provide emergency support to the care recipient. As at the end of September 2026, the Hotline had contacted more than 10 500 carer cases, of which five were confirmed to require immediate emergency support, including meal support, home-based respite and escort services. For the remaining cases, the Hotline provided the contact details of social welfare units so that support for care recipients could be arranged when needed. The 2026 Policy Address announced that the Government would enhance the automated operation of the Data Platform and expand its coverage.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;At district level, apart from mobilising Care Teams to proactively visit households in need, the Government has all along been providing various kinds of support for carers through District Elderly Community Centres, Neighbourhood Elderly Centres, District Support Centres for Persons with Disabilities (DSCs), and Parents/Relatives Resource Centres, which cover all 18 districts across the territory. Whether they are frontline staff of service units, the Hotline or members of the Care Teams, when providing or referring support services to carers, they will introduce the two allowance schemes to carers based on their needs, and make every effort to assist carers in making application. The 2026 Policy Address announced that the Government would optimise the system of the Hotline and increase the manpower of DSCs, so as to strengthen early identification of and support for high-risk carers.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;There are various initiatives among the carer support measures implemented by the Government that exemplify collaboration among the Government, the business sector and the community. These include the Information Gateway for Carers established by the SWD in collaboration with the Hong Kong Jockey Club Charities Trust, and the Pilot Scheme on Installing Intelligent Accident Detection Systems for High-risk Households, which is sponsored by the HKEX Foundation, and implemented and evaluated through collaboration between the Government, various service providers, non-governmental organisations and a university.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;The Government will continue to listen to the views of stakeholders, and work hand in hand with all sectors of the community to continuously enhance the carer support measures.]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[LCQ21: Deepening patriotic education]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/14/P2026101400340.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/14/P2026101400340.htm</link><pubDate>Wed, 14 Oct 2026 15:31:45 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Following is a question by the Hon Lee Chun-keung and a written reply by the Secretary for Home and Youth Affairs, Miss Alice Mak, in the Legislative Council today (October 14):<br />
<br />
Question:&nbsp;<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;In the 2026 Policy Address (Policy Address), the Chief Executive proposes to deepen patriotic education for young people by making good use of the Mainland&#39;s resources to deliver multi-faceted and content-rich exchange and internship activities as well as designating annual themes, which will enable young people to witness first-hand the country&#39;s development and achievements, deepening their understanding of the nation&#39;s history and culture as well as its enduring bond and kinship with Hong Kong. There are concerns over the implementation details and long-term effectiveness of the relevant programmes. In this connection, will the Government inform this Council:<br />
<br />
(1) of the detailed plans and timetables for implementation of the proposal in the Policy Address to list the Yan&#39;an Revolutionary Memorial Hall in Shaanxi Province as the second patriotic education base for the youth of the Hong Kong Special Administrative Region (HKSAR) in the Mainland and to designate annual themes, such as the country&#39;s aerospace achievements and the supply of Dongjiang water to Hong Kong for exchange and internship activities;<br />
<br />
(2) of the specific criteria and strategy to enhance the quality of youth exchange and study tours to the Mainland and prevent the itineraries from becoming merely a formality; for example, whether it will introduce high-quality thematic project learning and immersive experiences into youth exchange tours and establish quantifiable indicators for performance assessment to ensure that the exchange activities can truly deepen young people&#39;s recognition of the nation&#39;s history, development and achievements; if so, of the details; if not, the reasons for that; and<br />
<br />
(3) while national education and the Values Education Curriculum Framework have now been formally implemented in all primary and secondary schools across the territory, whether the Government will, together with the Working Group on Patriotic Education, make use of new media platforms, such as short videos and interactive games or partner with youth hostels and local clansmen associations to establish dedicated promotional networks for tertiary students who have graduated or left school, or for young people who are in employment or not in education, so as to prevent a gap in national education; if so, of the details; if not, the reasons for that?<br />
<br />
Reply:<br />
<br />
President,<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Having consulted the Constitutional and Mainland Affairs Bureau and the Education Bureau (EDB), the reply in response to the questions raised by the Hon Lee Chun-keung is as follows:<br />
<br />
(1) The 2026 Policy Address stipulates that the Government will deepen patriotic education for young people by making good use of the Mainland&#39;s resources to deliver multi faceted and content rich exchange and internship activities as well as designating annual themes, such as the country&#39;s aerospace achievements and the supply of Dongjiang water to Hong Kong. This will enable young people to witness first hand the country&#39;s development and achievements, deepening their understanding of the nation&#39;s history and culture as well as its enduring bond and kinship with Hong Kong, thereby strengthening young people&#39;s sense of national identity, national pride and cultural confidence.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;This year marks the 90th anniversary of the victory of the Long March. Yan&#39;an is a sacred place of the Chinese revolution and the cradle of New China. With the support of the Central Government, the HKSAR Government will list the Yan&#39;an Revolutionary Memorial Hall in Shaanxi Province as the second patriotic education base for the youth of the HKSAR in the Mainland, and encourage students and young people to visit the place for study. The HKSAR Government is now liaising with the relevant Mainland authorities on the timing and other arrangements for the establishment of this base.&nbsp;<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The EDB has been arranging for students to visit the Mainland for learning through the Mainland Exchange Programmes for Primary and Secondary School Students (MEPs) and the Senior Secondary Citizenship and Social Development (CS) Mainland Study Tours. The Yan&#39;an Revolutionary Memorial Hall in Shaanxi has been incorporated into the routes of the MEPs and CS Mainland Study Tours, with a view to enabling students to learn, through visits, about the journey of the Red Army under the leadership of the Communist Party of China (CPC) in the Long March, its establishment of a foothold in Yan&#39;an, and the history of Yan&#39;an once serving as the seat of the CPC Central Committee. In addition, other relevant visiting spots, such as Zhuhai Aerospace Land in Guangdong, the source of Dongjiang water at Sanbai Mountain in Jiangxi, and the Memorial Hall of the Victims in Nanjing Massacre by Japanese Invaders, have also been incorporated into the routes of the MEPs and CS Mainland Study Tours. The EDB will continue to review and optimise the arrangements for Mainland exchange activities for students, maintain close communication and liaison with relevant Mainland units, and disseminate successful experiences for enhancing the effectiveness of the study tours.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;At present, the Home and Youth Affairs Bureau (HYAB) provides funding for non-governmental organisations (NGOs) to organise youth exchange projects in the Mainland through the HYAB Funding Scheme for Youth Exchange in the Mainland (FSYEM). The FSYEM aims to enhance young people&#39;s understanding of the country&#39;s culture, history, way of life and latest developments, and strengthening exchanges with their Mainland peers. Applications for the 2027-28 FSYEM will open in mid-November this year. The HYAB will actively encourage NGOs to arrange for youth participants to visit the patriotic education bases for Hong Kong and Macao youth in their exchange projects, and relevant applications will be given priority consideration. Furthermore, to deepen patriotic education for young people, the HYAB will designate annual themes for 2027-28, which include: (i) the 30th anniversary of Hong Kong&#39;s return to the Motherland; (ii) the 100th anniversary of the founding of Chinese People&#39;s Liberation Army; (iii) the country&#39;s aerospace achievements; and (iv) supply of Dongjiang water to Hong Kong.<br />
<br />
(2) The EDB has been collecting views from different stakeholders, taking various factors into consideration, and maintaining close exchanges with relevant Mainland authorities to adjust and optimise the arrangements for Mainland exchange activities for students in each school year. Like a &quot;walking classroom&quot;, the exchange programmes enable students to gain first-hand understanding of national affairs and the latest development of our country. Apart from emphasising close connection with curriculum elements, the EDB has also enriched relevant visiting spots and learning objectives in response to the latest national developments and major historical events. For instance, inclusion of the Museum of the War of the Chinese People&#39;s Resistance Against Japanese Aggression in Beijing and the Yan&#39;an Revolutionary Memorial Hall in Shaanxi, both being patriotic education bases for Hong Kong and Macao youth, and designated itineraries for the theme of the 80th Anniversary of victory in the War of Resistance. The cross-province route in Jiangxi and Guangdong, on the theme of &quot;Remembering the Source When Drinking Water&quot;, combines the history of the Long March and revolutionary deeds, allowing students to appreciate, through thematic project-based learning and immersive experience, the profound affection of our country towards Hong Kong. The EDB will continue to gauge the learning effectiveness of Mainland exchange activities for students through participant questionnaires, school visits, inspections, collection of student works (including photos, video clips and student reflections), and interviews with teachers and students, using quantifiable performance indicators. The EDB also regularly organises experience-sharing sessions to promote professional exchanges among teachers, and showcase the outcomes of students&#39; experiential learning through diversified means, such as the CS Mainland Study Tour Project Learning Report Award Scheme.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The HYAB provides young people with Mainland exchange opportunities outside of those that complement the school curriculum, allowing them to choose exchange activities with different themes based on their interests and gain more in-depth experience of the latest developments in various aspects in the Mainland. Funded exchange projects under the FSYEM should feature specific themes, such as understanding the National 15th Five-Year Plan, the development of the Guangdong-Hong Kong-Macao Greater Bay Area, the Belt and Road initiative, as well as national history, economy, science, culture, etc. In the past two years, the HYAB has also strongly encouraged funded NGOs to organise exchange projects centered on themes such as the 80th Anniversary of victory in the War of Resistance, the 60th Anniversary of Dongjiang Water Supply to Hong Kong, the 90th Anniversary of the victory of the Long March, and the 160th Anniversary of Dr Sun Yat-sen&#39;s birthday. Through thematic exchanges and immersive experiences, these projects enable young people to gain a deeper understanding of the country&#39;s historical development and achievements, thereby further enhancing their sense of national identity and patriotism. Upon completion of the exchange projects, participants are invited to fill in evaluation questionnaires, from which the HYAB can gauge their feedback and monitor project effectiveness and quality. Representatives of the HYAB and the Youth Development Commission also attend local activities (such as sharing sessions) organised by the funded NGOs to gather participants&#39; views on the projects and monitor their effectiveness and quality.<br />
<br />
(3) In 2024, the Government established the Working Group on Patriotic Education (the Working Group) under the Constitution and Basic Law Promotion Steering Committee chaired by the Chief Secretary for Administration. The Working Group is responsible for co-ordinating the work of government departments and NGOs in taking forward patriotic education in Hong Kong. Four sub-groups of the Working Group focus on school education, community engagement, cultural and historical promotion, and media publicity. The Working Group has been emphasising on conducting patriotic education through a soft and heart-moving, as well as innovative and diversified approach for comprehensive patriotic education in various aspects for all walks of life. The Working Group will continue its work under this approach, including to further adopt new media platforms, to take forward patriotic education in Hong Kong.&nbsp;<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;For recent graduates, working youth and out-of-school youth, the HYAB has been working closely with the Committee on the Promotion of Civic Education to promote civic and national education (including patriotic education) to general public outside schools and within the community through different channels and diverse means. These efforts include disseminating information via the Committee&#39;s website and social media pages; organising the Basic Law Quiz Competition and Basic Law Seminars, etc; and sponsoring eligible organisations (including various youth organisations and district clansmen associations) to host activities in promoting national and patriotic education under the Community Participation Scheme. Also, through the Youth Hostel Scheme, the HYAB and the operating organisations provide various activities on national education to the youth tenants, such as visits and guided tours to the Chinese People&#39;s Liberation Army Hong Kong Garrison Exhibition Center, the National Security Exhibition Gallery and Xuelong 2; sharing sessions on the spirit of the &quot;two sessions&quot;; and exchange tours to the Mainland, in order to deepen their understanding of the country. In terms of the adaption of new media, to commemorate the 80th anniversary of victory in the War of Resistance, the Information Services Department (ISD), in collaboration with Radio Television Hong Kong (RTHK), have produced a 5-episode mini-drama titled &quot;Victory in War of Resistance&quot; and have the series available on the official websites and social media platforms of the ISD and RTHK, to enhance public awareness of the War of Resistance on the Chinese Mainland and in Hong Kong, to allow citizens to remember history, honour martyrs, and cherish and safeguard peace.]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[HKMA welcomes launch of Chongqing-Hong Kong Cross-Boundary Data Validation Platform]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/14/P2026101300478.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/14/P2026101300478.htm</link><pubDate>Wed, 14 Oct 2026 15:30:00 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[The following is issued on behalf of the Hong Kong Monetary Authority:<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The Chongqing and Hong Kong authorities announced today (October 14) the launch of the Chongqing-Hong Kong Cross-boundary Data Validation Platform (CQ-HK DVP).&nbsp;&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;As set out in the Chief Executive&#39;s 2026 Policy Address, this new cross-boundary data validation mechanism jointly established with Chongqing is one of the measures for encouraging the orderly flow of financial elements between the Chinese Mainland and Hong Kong. The Hong Kong Monetary Authority (HKMA) facilitated the establishment of the CQ-HK DVP (Note 1) together with authorities including the People&#39;s Bank of China Chongqing Municipal Branch, the Office of the Financial Commission of the Chongqing Municipal Committee, the Office of the Cyberspace Affairs Commission of the Chongqing Municipal Committee, and the Hong Kong and Macao Affairs Office of the Chongqing Municipal People&#39;s Government.<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Leveraging blockchain technology and data coding (i.e. hash values), the CQ-HK DVP enables the verification of data presented by customers without involving the cross-boundary transfer or storage of the original documents. The relevant innovative technologies and mechanism have been successfully applied and proven to be stable and effective via the Shenzhen-Hong Kong Cross-boundary Data Validation Platform (Note 2).<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The CQ-HK DVP will mainly support cross-boundary financial use cases at the outset. Banks in Chongqing and Hong Kong are actively advancing use cases involving the validation of both Northbound and Southbound data flows, which will support customers in the two places to engage in diverse banking services, including account opening and loan applications. &nbsp;&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;The Chief Executive of the HKMA, Mr Eddie Yue, said, &quot;Under the &#39;Fintech 2030&#39; strategy, the HKMA is committed to creating next-generation data infrastructure. The CQ-HK DVP will further foster secure and efficient cross-boundary data flow between Chongqing and Hong Kong. We will maintain close liaison with the relevant Mainland authorities, the banking industry, frontier technology companies and other stakeholders to continuously enhance the platform. This, in turn, will benefit residents and corporates from both places<a> </a>and support the development of the real economy.&quot;<br />
<br />
Note 1: The platform is operated by Chongqing Credit Co., Ltd and WeBank Technology Services Limited in Chongqing and Hong Kong respectively.<br />
<br />
Note 2: The Shenzhen-Hong Kong Cross-boundary Data Validation Platform (SZ-HK DVP) was <a href="https://www.hkma.gov.hk/eng/news-and-media/press-releases/2024/05/20240506-4/" target="_blank">pilot launched in May 2024</a> and linked up with the HKMA&#39;s Commercial Data Interchange in April 2025. To date, the usage of the SZ-HK DVP has surpassed 40 000 times, and it has successfully provided assistance to over 10 banks across more than 10 business scenarios, including the validation work required for bank account opening, remittances, and loans. Both Northbound and Southbound data flows have been covered.&nbsp;]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[Government invites interested parties to submit expressions of interest for development of onshore power supply facility at Kai Tak Cruise Terminal ]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/14/P2026101300358.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/14/P2026101300358.htm</link><pubDate>Wed, 14 Oct 2026 15:30:00 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;The Environment and Ecology Bureau (EEB), with the Tourism Commission (TC), today (October 14) invites interested parties to submit expressions of interest (EOIs) for the development of an onshore power supply (OPS) facility at the Kai Tak Cruise Terminal (KTCT).&nbsp;<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;The use of green energy is a major trend in the international shipping industry. The International Maritime Organization (IMO) has set a target to peak greenhouse gas (GHG) emissions from international shipping as soon as possible and to reach net-zero GHG emissions by or around 2050, taking into account different national circumstances. Hong Kong, China, as an associate member of the IMO, has long been supporting the related emission reduction target. Installing OPS facilities at cruise terminals allows cruise ships to connect to the onshore power grid while at berth, helping to reduce GHG emissions and fuel consumption from the ship&#39;s auxiliary engines, and abating emissions of air pollutants including nitrogen oxides.&nbsp;<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The KTCT had reserved space for setting up OPS facilities during its construction. In view of the growing adoption of OPS in the international cruise industry in recent years, the EEB, in collaboration with the TC, commissioned a consultancy study on the installation of OPS facilities at the KTCT. The study includes reviewing the trend of developing OPS facilities in major ports around the world, analysing the installation of onboard systems for onshore power connection and their use by cruise ships in various places, and examining the technical requirements and development costs for installing such facilities at the KTCT. The study reveals that installing OPS facilities at the KTCT is technically feasible, yet its long-term economic benefits and costs would require further examination. Therefore, the Government is conducting an EOI exercise to gauge the private sector&#39;s interest in investing in and operating the facilities, and to assess their commercial viability, with a view to formulating the development arrangements.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;A spokesperson for the EEB said, &quot;Developing OPS facilities will help advance Hong Kong&#39;s transition towards green shipping. We encourage interested parties to submit EOIs to provide views and proposals on the development of OPS facilities at the KTCT, including delivery model, OPS facility design, operational plan and cost effectiveness analysis. The EOI exercise is not a tender exercise and does not form part of any procurement process.&quot;<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;The deadline for submission of EOIs is noon on December 14, 2026 (Monday). Details of the EOI invitation can be found on the webpage of the EEB (<a href="https://www.eeb.gov.hk/en/business_job/business_opp/eoi/ops-ktct.html" target="_blank">www.eeb.gov.hk/en/business_job/business_opp/eoi/ops-ktct.html</a>).]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[Fanling Swimming Pool temporarily closed]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/14/P2026101400500.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/14/P2026101400500.htm</link><pubDate>Wed, 14 Oct 2026 15:15:00 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[Attention TV/radio announcers:<br />
<br />
Please broadcast the following as soon as possible and repeat it at regular intervals:<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Here is an item of interest to swimmers.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The Leisure and Cultural Services Department announced today (October 14) that due to suspension of water supply resulting from water pipe burst in the vicinity, Fanling Swimming Pool in North District has been temporarily closed until further notice.]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[HKICL alerts public of fraudulent website]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/14/P2026101400482.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/14/P2026101400482.htm</link><pubDate>Wed, 14 Oct 2026 14:55:00 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[The following is issued on behalf of the Hong Kong Monetary Authority:<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Hong Kong Interbank Clearing Limited (HKICL) has recently noted a fraudulent website at fasterpaymentsystemfps.xyz purported to be from HKICL. The fraudulent website imitates as &quot;Buyer Online Protection&quot; to provide services including 1) refund to buyer, 2) unauthorised online transaction reporting, and 3) online transaction support, for online transaction over FPS payment. It intends to trick user into giving away personal document number and ID photo together with phone number for real name verification in order to get cash reward. In addition, it also tricks user into providing bank name, bank account number and bank account holder&#39;s name to top up and withdraw money from its platform&#39;s virtual wallet. Further, it directs user to a fraudster impersonating as customer service personnel.<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Please be reminded that the fraudulent website may have multiple combinations at the end of the link, e.g.:
<ul>
	<li><a target="_blank">fasterpaymentsystemfps.xyz/page/place_an_order/?uuid=refund </a></li>
</ul>
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;​HKICL advises that this fraudulent website has no affiliation with HKICL, or any business of HKICL. HKICL will not directly provide FPS service to individual members of the public or contact individual members of the public proactively under usual circumstance. The genuine official website addresses of HKICL are <a href="https://www.hkicl.com.hk/" target="_blank">www.hkicl.com.hk</a> and <a href="https://fps.hkicl.com.hk/" target="_blank">fps.hkicl.com.hk</a>.<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;HKICL urges the public to beware of suspicious calls, websites or other communications, and to remain vigilant in protecting personal information. If members of the public receive any suspicious communication purportedly from HKICL, please call HKICL&#39;s general line at 2533 1111 to verify. Members of the public who suspect that they&nbsp;have become the victim of fraudulent acts should report promptly to the Police.]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[LCQ17: Strengthening dog management regime and enforcement operations]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/14/P2026101400275.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/14/P2026101400275.htm</link><pubDate>Wed, 14 Oct 2026 14:55:00 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;Following is a question by Professor&nbsp;the&nbsp;Hon Priscilla Leung and a written reply by the Secretary for Environment and Ecology, Mr Tse Chin-wan, in the Legislative Council today (October 14):<br />
&nbsp;<br />
Question:<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;In light of the recent spate of dog attacks in Hong Kong that have caused injury or loss of life to humans and other dogs, public concern has been raised over the identification and control of dangerous dogs, the responsibility of dog owners to keep their dogs under proper control, and the management of stray dogs. In this connection, will the Government inform this Council:<br />
<br />
(1) given that Schedule 1 to the Dangerous Dogs Regulation (Cap. 167D) currently specifies four categories of &quot;fighting dogs&quot;, and that a dog may only be classified as a &quot;known dangerous dog&quot; on the basis of its previous attacks on humans or domestic animals, whether the Government will, as part of the ongoing review, study (i) expanding the statutory categories of &quot;fighting dogs&quot;; and (ii) reviewing the existing classification and control system for &quot;known dangerous dogs&quot;, including examining the inclusion of factors such as a dog&#39;s previous attack records, behavioural history and other risk indicators, with a view to identifying at an earlier stage dogs that may pose a higher risk to public safety; if so, of the details; if not, the reasons for that;<br />
<br />
(2) whether it will review Cap. 167D and the Rabies Ordinance (Cap. 421) to strengthen the provisions and penalties relating to the abandonment of dogs, the keeping of dogs at large, and the failure to keep dogs under proper control, including examining an increase in the maximum fines and terms of imprisonment, as well as enhancing the legal liabilities of dog owners who repeatedly contravene the relevant requirements or whose dogs are involved in incidents resulting in serious injury or loss of life; if so, of the details; if not, the reasons for that;<br />
<br />
(3) given that the Agriculture, Fisheries and Conservation Department (AFCD) has formulated the code of practice for keeping dogs on construction sites, and that dogs may also be present at certain car parks, open working areas, logistics sites, container yards and brownfield operational areas, whether the Government will study extending to such premises the dog control requirements currently applicable to construction sites; if so, of the details; if not, the reasons for that;<br />
<br />
(4) over the past five years, of the respective annual numbers of stray dogs captured by the AFCD, and among those dogs, how many were reclaimed by their original owners, transferred to animal welfare organisations, adopted by members of the public, or euthanised on humane grounds; and whether the Government will study the expansion of animal accommodation facilities and increase the number of places available through collaboration with animal welfare organisations for boarding, fostering and rehoming, with a view to arranging adoption for as many adoptable dogs as possible; if so, of the details; if not, the reasons for that; and<br />
<br />
(5) in view of the recent spate of incidents involving dog attacks resulting in injury or loss of life to humans and animals, whether the Government will review its existing stray dog inspection strategy, and proactively step up inspections in areas where dog attack incidents have occurred or where stray dogs are known to be prevalent; if so, of the details; if not, the reasons for that; whether it will review how the practice of registering dog microchips on behalf of others should be regulated, with a view to strengthening the management of dogs and clearly identifying the persons responsible for them; if so, of the details; if not, the reasons for that?<br />
<br />
Reply:<br />
<br />
President,<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The Government attaches great importance to the recent dog attacks on people and animals. The Agriculture, Fisheries and Conservation Department (AFCD) and the Police are conducting a comprehensive investigation into the causes of the incidents and have taken appropriate enforcement action and control measures. It is the unequivocal statutory obligation of dog keepers to keep their pets under proper control. To enhance the protection of public safety, the Government is conducting a comprehensive review of the existing legislation on dog control, will consult the Legislative Council Panel on the proposed amendments by the end of this year and strive to complete the legislative amendment exercise within next year.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Having consulted the Development Bureau, a reply to the question raised by Professor the&nbsp;Hon Priscilla Leung is as follows:<br />
<br />
(1) The Dangerous Dogs Regulation (Cap. 167D) (the Regulation) stipulates that Pit Bull Terrier, Japanese Tosa, Dogo Argentino and Fila Braziliero, as well as any crossbreed of these four types of dogs, are &quot;fighting dogs&quot;. In addition, a magistrate may classify a dog as a &quot;known dangerous dog&quot; if it, without provocation, bites or attacks a person or a kept animal, causing death or serious injury, or if it repeatedly attacks people or causes people to fear that they may be attacked.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;As to whether the above definitions should be updated, the Government is listening to the views of different stakeholders and will also make reference to the regulatory experience of other countries and regions, with a view to formulating an appropriate proposal based on the risk-based principle.<br />
<br />
(2) The current Rabies Ordinance (Cap. 421) and the Regulation contain clear requirements on the proper control of dogs, and also specify the penalties for offences such as abandonment of animals.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;In view of the general public view that the existing penalties for abandoning animals and failing to keep dogs under proper control are not sufficiently deterrent, the Government will study increasing the penalties for such offences.<br />
<br />
(3) Whether in public places or workplaces (including construction sites, car parks, open-air workshops, logistics sites, container yards and brownfield sites), dog keepers are required to keep their dogs under proper control, and to ensure that their dogs are licensed and vaccinated against rabies regularly. The AFCD will make reference to the existing &quot;Code of Practice for Keeping Dogs on Construction Sites&quot; to draw up educational materials suitable for rural environments, explaining to site operators and dog keepers their responsibility to keep dogs under proper control, with a view to reducing potential risks to public safety.<br />
<br />
(4) After capturing unattended dogs, the AFCD will try to locate the keepers and contact them to reclaim the dogs. For unclaimed dogs that are in good health with a gentle temperament, the AFCD will transfer them to animal welfare organisations&nbsp;(AWOs) for rehoming by members of the public. Dogs that are aggressive or otherwise unsuitable for adoption may eventually be euthanised. The figures on unattended dogs handled by the AFCD in the past five years are set out at Annex.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The Government has been actively assisting AWOs in expanding their animal accommodation and adoption facilities. The AFCD operates an annual Subvention for Animal Welfare Organisation Subvention to support animal welfare organisations in organising activities relating to animal management and welfare, including adoption services, education and publicity programmes, and preventive veterinary treatments (including neutering).<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;AWOs seeking land for the operation of animal rehoming centres may consider applying for the lease of vacant government sites (including vacant school premises) published by the Lands Department (LandsD) on the &quot;GeoInfo Map&quot; website and made available for application by non-governmental organisations (NGOs) by way of short-term tenancies for community, institutional or non-profit making purposes. NGOs which have obtained policy support from the relevant bureaux and have secured in-principle approval from the LandsD for the grant of short-term tenancies may apply to the Development Bureau (DEVB) for funding under the funding scheme to support the use of vacant government sites by non-government organisations (the Funding Scheme) to support one-off, basic and essential restoration works required for taking forward worthy projects for the community. To date, among the projects funded under the Funding Scheme that have been completed and have commenced operation, three are animal rehoming centres. The applicant organisations are all partnering AWOs of the AFCD. These facilities could accommodate a total of approximately 450 animals (including stray dogs). In addition, one animal rehoming centre project is currently undergoing detailed design and site investigation works, with a target completion date of end-2027. For the aforesaid four animal rehoming centre projects, funding approvals under the Funding Scheme amount to approximately $135.6 million in total to date. The Government will enhance support along the aforesaid directions as necessary.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The Government will continue to strengthen collaboration with its partner AWOs in providing accommodation and adoption services, and to promote the message of &quot;adopting rather than buying&quot; to members of the public who intend to keep dogs, so that more unclaimed dogs can find new homes.<br />
<br />
(5) The AFCD has analysed recent cases of dog attacks on people and animals based on the risk-based principle. It will accord priority to stepping up patrols and capturing unattended dogs at high-risk locations where incidents have occurred or where there are larger numbers of stray dogs, so as to eliminate potential safety risks in the community. If dog keepers are found not to have properly controlled their dogs, the AFCD will take stringent enforcement action.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The existing legislation does not provide for any arrangement of &quot;registering dog microchip on another person&#39;s behalf&quot;. As a registered dog owner, a person must assume all the responsibilities of a dog keeper prescribed by law, and properly care for and control the dog registered in his or her name. Abandoning a dog or allowing it to wander without control in a public place is an offence. The AFCD is studying the updating of the dog licence application form, using clearer wording to remind keepers of their responsibility for proper control.]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[Red flags hoisted at Big Wave Bay Beach and Cheung Chau Tung Wan Beach]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/14/P2026101400473.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/14/P2026101400473.htm</link><pubDate>Wed, 14 Oct 2026 14:47:00 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[Attention TV/radio announcers:<br />
<br />
Please broadcast the following as soon as possible:<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;Here is an item of interest to swimmers.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;The Leisure and Cultural Services Department announced today (October 14) that due to big waves, red flags have been hoisted at Big Wave Bay Beach in Southern District, Hong Kong Island, and Cheung Chau Tung Wan Beach in Islands District. Beachgoers are advised not to swim at these beaches.]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[LCQ14: Illegal parking and illegal activities in Wan Chai District]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/14/P2026101400357.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/14/P2026101400357.htm</link><pubDate>Wed, 14 Oct 2026 14:45:00 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Following is a question by the Hon Adrian Ho and a written reply by the Secretary for Security, Mr Tang Ping-keung, in the Legislative Council today (October 14):<br />
<br />
Question:<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;It has been reported that in recent months, vehicles have been parked for long periods at night on relatively narrow road sections in Wan Chai District (such as in the vicinity of Wan Chai Road). This has affected the passage of large vehicles, caused obstruction to local road traffic, and brought much inconvenience and nuisance to residents in the district. In this connection, will the Government inform this Council:<br />
<br />
(1) of the respective numbers of (i) complaints received by law enforcement agencies over the past three years involving illegal parking of vehicles in respect of the aforesaid road sections in the district and (ii) prosecutions instituted;<br />
<br />
(2) as there are views relaying that persons involved in the aforesaid illegal vehicle parking are suspected of engaging in illegal acts (such as allegedly transporting women by prostitution syndicates to and from hotels or buildings in the district), whether law enforcement agencies have conducted in-depth investigations into the issue to ascertain if syndicate-controlled illegal acts are involved; if so, of the progress of the relevant investigations and the prosecution status respectively; if not, the reasons for not conducting in-depth investigation into the issue; and<br />
<br />
(3) to effectively combat various illegal acts in Wan Chai District and maintain law and order in the district, whether law enforcement agencies have plans to establish a notification mechanism with stakeholders in the district, so as to enhance co-operation in combating the use of premises such as bars, residential buildings and hotels in the district for organised illegal activities; if so, of the details; if not, the reasons for that?<br />
<br />
Reply:<br />
<br />
President,<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The Hong Kong Police Force (HKPF) has been closely monitoring the law and order situation in Wan Chai District and has adopted a multi-pronged strategy, including intelligence-led enforcement operations conducted in response to stakeholder reports and public complaints, with a view to vigorously combating various types of organised crime and illegal activities, including illegal parking. Having consulted the Transport and Logistics Bureau, the reply to the question raised by the Member is as follows:<br />
<br />
(1) The HKPF is committed to ensuring road safety and maintaining a smooth traffic flow. Enforcement actions will be prioritised in light of actual traffic situation, focusing on combating contraventions that endanger road safety and cause severe traffic obstruction, in order to make effective use of police manpower and law enforcement resources. The numbers of complaints received by the HKPF and the Transport Department (TD) against illegal parking of vehicles in Wan Chai District, including narrower sections such as Wan Chai Road, and the corresponding numbers of summonses issued over the past three years are set out in the table below:<br />
<br />
Statistics of illegal parking in Wan Chai District<br />
&nbsp;
<table border="1" style="width:100%">
	<tbody>
		<tr>
			<td rowspan="2" style="width:13.18%">&nbsp;</td>
			<td colspan="6" style="width:57.9%">Number of complaints (Note)</td>
