Note: As identical complaints may be received by the HKPF and the TD at the same time, there may be double-counting in the numbers of complaints received by the two departments.<br />
<br />
(2) The HKPF has been committed to combating all forms of illegal prostitution, especially organised prostitution activities. Under section 130 of the Crimes Ordinance (Cap. 200), a person who harbours another person or exercises control, direction or influence over another person for the purpose of or with a view to that person&#39;s prostitution shall be guilty of an offence, and offenders shall be liable to imprisonment for up to 14 years; under section 137 of the Ordinance, a person who knowingly lives wholly or in part on the earnings of prostitution of another shall also be guilty of an offence, and offenders shall be liable to imprisonment for up to 10 years.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;As regards the combat of illegal prostitution activities in Wan Chai District, the HKPF and the Immigration Department have been maintaining close intelligence exchanges and will carry out regular joint operations codenamed &quot;Tigertail&quot; during which suspected vice establishments are raided. The Police will also carry out intelligence-led enforcement actions, conduct in-depth investigations and combat organised prostitution activities, so as to reduce the nuisance these activities may cause to the public. Over the past year, in operations against prostitution conducted in the vicinity of Wan Chai Road, the Wan Chai Police District arrested and prosecuted a total of 15 women for suspected breach of conditions of stay, while no syndicate-controlled operations were involved. The HKPF will continue to take proactive enforcement actions with a view to vigorously combating prostitution activities in the district.<br />
<br />
(3) Good police-community relations are integral to preventing and fighting crime as well as upholding law and order in the community. The HKPF has been working closely with stakeholders in Wan Chai District to maintain public order. In this regard, the Hong Kong Island Region of the HKPF launched Project SAFE in 2023 to proactively share crime information with community stakeholders, and encourage them to enhance collaboration with the Police by, for example, providing criminal intelligence, assisting in the dissemination of crime prevention messages and leveraging technology (such as installing closed-circuit televisions) to enhance crime prevention capabilities. To date, nearly 230 partner organisations in Wan Chai District, including hotels, bars and other businesses, have participated in the project, helping to strengthen the Police&#39;s intelligence-gathering capabilities.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Furthermore, the Wan Chai Police District will, in view of crime trends, strengthen liaison and intelligence exchanges with local stakeholders, including property management and security companies, to jointly combat crime. The Police will proactively convey crime prevention information to building security personnel, provide guidance on how to identify illegal prostitution activities to prevent suspicious persons from entering the buildings, and encourage them to report to the Police whenever a suspected crime is taking place, with a view to fostering police-community co-operation and fighting crime with concerted efforts.]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[Legislative amendments to confer default mediator-appointing power on Hong Kong Mediation Accreditation Association Limited]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/14/P2026101400305.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/14/P2026101400305.htm</link><pubDate>Wed, 14 Oct 2026 14:40:00 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The Government will introduce legislative amendments to the Mediation Ordinance (Cap. 620) to empower the Hong Kong Mediation Accreditation Association Limited (HKMAAL), on application by any party to an agreement to mediate, to decide the number of mediators or appoint mediators in prescribed circumstances where parties fail to reach an agreement, or where the agreed appointment procedure has not been carried out (Proposal).<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;A spokesman for the Department of Justice said, &quot;The Proposal aims to streamline the mediation process, reduce the risk of stalled proceedings arising from any disagreement over mediator appointments, and improve overall efficiency in case administration. These enhancements are especially beneficial for time-sensitive disputes. Granting the HKMAAL the said statutory power is expected to encourage the use of mediation as a dispute resolution mechanism. It will help bolster Hong Kong&#39;s competitiveness as a leading international dispute resolution hub and develop Hong Kong into a global capital of mediation.&quot;<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The Proposal was formulated on the basis of the recommendations made by the Working Group on Mediation Regulatory System in December 2025 following a stakeholder consultation. A primary objective of this legislative amendment is to recognise and further enhance the HKMAAL&#39;s leading status within the mediation sector.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Since its incorporation in 2012, the HKMAAL has been operating as a premier, industry-led mediation accreditation and regulatory body. The HKMAAL is responsible for setting accreditation standards for mediators and mediation training courses, assessing applications for mediator accreditation and training course accreditation, and regulating accredited mediators. The member organisations of the HKMAAL span a diverse range of sectors, including law, dispute resolution, family welfare, construction, architecture, surveying and engineering. Unlike most mediation institutions in Hong Kong, the HKMAAL does not provide any mediation services or mediation training courses, whether on a for-profit or non-profit basis. Also, under &quot;The Government of the Hong Kong Special Administrative Region Mediation Rules (2025 Edition)&quot;, the HKMAAL is designated to appoint a mediator for the parties when they fail to reach a consensus on the choice of a mediator. The HKMAAL&#39;s distinctive role is also reflected in the Judiciary&#39;s requirement that mediators be accredited by the HKMAAL to participate in its mediation-related schemes. Given the HKMAAL&#39;s unique role and standing in the mediation sector, as well as the breadth of sectors covered by its member organisations, the Government considers the HKMAAL the most suitable organisation to exercise the said statutory appointing power.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The Proposal, together with the other recommendations made by the Working Group, including that the HKMAAL will revise the Hong Kong Mediation Code and encourage its adoption, implement a robust complaint-handling and disciplinary framework to enforce the Mediation Code, and actively participate in global discussions on dispute resolution, forms a comprehensive package to enhance the mediation regulatory system in Hong Kong.&nbsp;&nbsp;<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The recommendations and the key provisions in the amendment bill received general support from major legal and dispute resolution bodies, as well as the Judiciary, during further stakeholder consultations conducted from June to July 2026.&nbsp;&nbsp;<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The Mediation (Amendment) Bill 2026 was gazetted on October 2 and was introduced into the Legislative Council today (October 14).]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[LCQ2: Celebration activities for 30th anniversary of Hong Kong's return to the motherland ]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/14/P2026101400343.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/14/P2026101400343.htm</link><pubDate>Wed, 14 Oct 2026 14:33:00 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;Following is a question by the Hon Chan Yung and a reply by the Secretary for Home and Youth Affairs, Miss Alice Mak, in the Legislative Council today (October 14):<br />
<br />
Question:<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;It is learnt that the Government has established a steering committee and a project planning office to co-ordinate the celebration activities for the 30th anniversary of Hong Kong&#39;s return to the motherland. In this connection, will the Government inform this Council:<br />
<br />
(1) of the expected timeframe for announcement of the overall plan, current progress of the preparation work and timetable of the celebration activities for the 30th anniversary of Hong Kong&#39;s return to the motherland, and whether it will study collaborating with the relevant Central authorities and the Mainland cities of the Guangdong-Hong Kong-Macao Greater Bay Area to jointly co-ordinate national-level celebration projects, so as to expand the impact of the activities;<br />
<br />
(2) whether it will roll out celebration activities at three levels, namely community, education and sports, including mobilising resources in 18 districts to organise activities with distinctive characteristics, encouraging various sectors to continue providing special offers on the 1st of July, making good use of the existing patriotic education facilities and red tourism elements to design thematic activities integrating celebration with education, as well as organising the event of Reunification Cup at the Kai Tak Sports Park to bring together tradition and international sports events, with a view to promoting the mega event economy; and<br />
<br />
(3) whether it will organise international forums, exhibitions or economic and trade activities relating to Hong Kong&#39;s return to the motherland, so as to attract visitors and inward investment, and demonstrate the achievements of the successful implementation of &quot;one country, two systems&quot; in Hong Kong over the past 30 years since its return to the motherland, and how the Government assesses the effectiveness of these activities in reinforcing the international image of Hong Kong and telling good stories of Hong Kong, China?<br />
<br />
Reply<br />
<br />
President,<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;July 1, 2027, marks the glorious and historic moment of the 30th anniversary of the establishment of the Hong Kong Special Administrative Region (HKSAR) and the 30th anniversary of Hong Kong&#39;s return to the motherland. This is the first major establishment anniversary Hong Kong celebrates following the full implementation of the principle of &quot;Patriots Administering Hong Kong&quot;. It signifies that Hong Kong has entered a historic phase of advancing from stability to prosperity in the march to fully implementing the &quot;one country, two systems&quot; policy, safeguarding national sovereignty and security, and ensuring long-term prosperity and stability. As the country takes forward the National 15th Five-Year Plan in full steam, the HKSAR Government has also formulated Hong Kong&#39;s first Five-Year Plan to proactively align with the National 15th Five-Year Plan, ensuring that Hong Kong can better seize the development opportunities while integrating into and serving the overall development of the country.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;Leveraging this important historic moment, the Government will celebrate the extraordinary achievements made over the 30 years of return to the motherland together with all sectors of society and citizens of Hong Kong, while showcasing Hong Kong&#39;s ever brighter future to the world. Under the steer of the Chief Executive, the Government has established a steering committee chaired by the Chief Secretary for Administration to provide overall steer on the planning and preparation of celebrations. Designated task forces chaired by the Deputy Chief Secretary for Administration have been set up under the steering committee to co-ordinate and oversee the implementation of specific tasks. The Government has also set up a Project Planning Office to support the steering committee and task forces, and to take forward the planning and preparation work.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;Under the leadership of the steering committee, all Government bureaux and departments are fully committed to organising a diverse and exciting array of celebratory activities for rolling out in 2027. The celebratory activities will cover a wide range of areas and incorporate elements of national education. This will allow members of the public to deepen their understanding of the history and development of the country and Hong Kong while celebrating the anniversary, thereby enhancing their sense of national identity and belonging, and deeply cultivating a sense of patriotism. While sharing the joy of the return to the motherland with the citizens and compatriots in the Chinese Mainland, the Government will also leverage the celebratory activities to showcase Hong Kong&#39;s distinctive edge of enjoying strong support of the motherland while being closely connected to the world under the &quot;one country, two systems&quot; principle, and to highlight Hong Kong&#39;s unique role and status on the international stage. The opportunity will be taken to tell the good stories of China and Hong Kong to the world, and to demonstrate the good opportunities awaiting Hong Kong under the National 15th Five-Year Plan.&nbsp;&nbsp;<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;Celebrating the 30th anniversary of return to the motherland is keenly anticipated by the entire city. The Government looks forward to the participation and support of all sectors of society, including the Legislative Council, in this important celebration in their different ways. The Government is currently making every effort to plan and advance the celebratory activities for the 30th anniversary; announcements will be made at suitable junctures when plans are firmed up.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;Thank you, President.]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[LCQ12: Silver Bond issued in 2026]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/14/P2026101400253.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/14/P2026101400253.htm</link><pubDate>Wed, 14 Oct 2026 14:27:00 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[<a target="_blank">&nbsp;&nbsp; &nbsp;&nbsp;</a>Following is a question by the Hon Maggie Chan and a written reply by the Secretary for Financial Services and the Treasury, Mr Christopher Hui, in the Legislative Council today (October 14):<br />
&nbsp;<br />
Question:<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp; &nbsp;&nbsp;Hong Kong&#39;s First Five-Year Plan emphasises &quot;promoting the enhancement and upgrading of the pension finance system and continuing to diversify pension finance product offerings&quot;. Both the number of valid applicants and the total principal amount of subscription of the Silver Bond issued in 2026 have reached historic highs. In this connection, will the Government inform this Council:<br />
&nbsp;<br />
(1) as it is learnt that the Silver Bond issued in 2026 recorded over 476 000 valid applications with subscription amount close to $120 billion, which is more than double the final issuance amount of $55 billion, whether the Government will, based on the eligible elderly population and subscription situations over the years, regularly review and increase the final issuance amount of Silver Bond, so as to further promote the silver economy; if so, of the details; if not, the reasons for that;<br />
&nbsp;<br />
(2) as it is learnt that under the allocation mechanism of the Silver Bond issued in 2026, the maximum allocation of bonds per person was capped at 13 units, which was far lower than the maximum of 17 units allocated for the Silver Bond in 2025; as a result, over 388 000 applications seeking more than 12 units were only allocated 12 units initially, with the additional unit to be determined by ballot, the success rate of which was only about one in four, whether the Government will study increasing the per capita allocation of Silver Bond (such as increasing the basic guaranteed number of units to be allocated), and evaluate the role of Silver Bond in providing retirement income protection for the elderly; if so, of the details; if not, the reasons for that; and<br />
&nbsp;<br />
(3) as it is learnt that Silver Bond holders may redeem their bonds early if they have financial needs, whether the Government has compiled statistics on the respective numbers of redemption cases and the amounts involved throughout the years since the issuance of Silver Bond in the 2016-2017 financial year, and whether it will review the redemption arrangements with reference to the relevant data, so as to enhance the flexibility of Silver Bond in the financial management of the elderly?<br />
&nbsp;<br />
Reply:<br />
&nbsp;<br />
President,<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp; &nbsp;&nbsp;The Government set up the Government Sustainable Bond Programme (GSBP) and the Infrastructure Bond Programme (IBP) in 2018 and 2024 respectively, with the aim of utilising market resources to meet the funding needs of sustainable projects and public works projects which are conducive to long-term development of Hong Kong, pushing ahead Hong Kong&#39;s infrastructure and urban development, and facilitating the early completion of projects for the good of the economy and people&#39;s livelihood.<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp; &nbsp;&nbsp;As the Government accelerates the development of the Northern Metropolis and other public works projects relating to the economy and people&#39;s livelihood, the total borrowing ceiling of the GSBP and the IBP was raised to HK$900 billion following approval by the Legislative Council in May this year. During 2026-27 to 2030-31, the Government plans to issue about HK$160 billion to $220 billion worth of bonds each year under the two bond programmes. Silver Bond is offered under the IBP (Note).<br />
<br />
Note: Silver bond was first launched in 2016. During 2016 to 2023, it was offered under the Government Bond Programme (GBP); since 2024, it was offered under the IBP. The GBP was a Government initiative to develop the local bond market. In February 2024, the Government announced that the IBP and the GSBP would gradually replace the GBP.<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp; &nbsp;&nbsp;In response to the question raised by Hon Maggie Chan, having consulted the Hong Kong Monetary Authority, our reply is as follows:<br />
&nbsp;<br />
(1) and (2) Currently, the Government issues Silver Bond mainly for raising funds for capital works projects, while at the same time providing senior citizens with a reliable investment option with steady returns. The target issuance amount of the 2026 Silver Bond was HK$50 billion. In view of the positive response in subscription, the final issuance amount was increased to HK$55 billion.<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp; &nbsp;&nbsp;When formulating specific arrangements of the bond issuance plan each year, the Government will take into consideration a basket of factors, including interest rates and tenors of different currencies and types of bonds, issuance costs of the Government, market response, fiscal position of the Government and duration and progress of works. The above factors are also applicable to the formulation of issuance arrangements of Silver Bond. The population of senior citizens and historical subscription results are some of the reference information when considering the issuance arrangements of Silver Bond, but not the decisive factors regarding the issuance amount.<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp; &nbsp;&nbsp;Apart from Silver Bond, the Government has various measures on promoting silver economy. Among them, the Financial Services and the Treasury Bureau is taking the lead to promote the &quot;silver financial and security arrangements&quot;, to assist senior citizens in prudent financial planning and risk management through arrangements such as retirement financial planning products, mortgage and investor education. Silver Bond provides senior citizens with a reliable investment option with steady returns, but is not for retirement income protection. Since the financial situation and investment plans vary significantly among individual senior citizens, we are not able to make an assessment in this regard.<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp; &nbsp;&nbsp;On the &quot;maximum principal allocation amount&quot; of Silver Bond, as the Government aims at allowing more citizens to join the scheme, the allocation mechanism in the Issue Circular of the 2026 Silver Bond specified that the maximum allocation amount per investor is HK$1 million, viz each investor would be allocated 100 units at most. Under the current mechanism via allocation cycles, the highest number of allocated units for each Silver Bond issuance is determined subject to the total number of valid applications and amounts applied for. Using the Silver Bond issued in 2026 as an example, all valid applications received have been allocated bonds up to a maximum of 13 units. For the 88 422 applications seeking 12 or fewer units, they will be allocated the full amounts applied for. The remaining 388 281 applications applying for more than 12 units would be allocated 12 units each and then entered into a ballot. Of these applications, 97 396 applications would be allocated one additional unit.<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp; &nbsp;&nbsp;The current allocation mechanism can cover all valid applications while satisfying the demand of small-lot investors. The Government will continue to take into account of the market response of Silver Bond when formulating suitable bond issuance arrangements in the future.<br />
&nbsp;<br />
(3) An early redemption arrangement has been in place since the launch of Silver Bond in 2016. Currently, a bondholder can submit an early redemption request to the Government through a Placing Bank or Designated Securities Broker on any business day. The bonds will be redeemed at par by the Placing Bank or Designated Securities Broker on the third business day after the submission of the request. The amount equals to the principal amount together with accrued but unpaid interest (for the period up to (but excluding) the Early Redemption Settlement Date), which is calculated at the fixed rate applicable to the respective batches of bonds.<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp; &nbsp;&nbsp;The amount involved in the early redemption of Silver Bond during the past 10 years (up to September 2026) is set out below. The Government does not keep records of the number of early redemption cases.<br />
&nbsp;
<table border="1" style="width:560px">
	<tbody>
		<tr>
			<td style="height:43px; width:21.58%">Year of issuance</td>
			<td style="height:43px; width:40.18%">Early Redemption Amount<br />
			(HK$&#39;billion)</td>
			<td style="height:43px; width:38.24%">Early Redemption Ratio<br />
			(%)</td>
		</tr>
		<tr>
			<td style="height:26px; width:21.58%">2025</td>
			<td style="height:26px; width:40.18%">7.0</td>
			<td style="height:26px; width:38.24%">1.3</td>
		</tr>
		<tr>
			<td style="height:26px; width:21.58%">2024</td>
			<td style="height:26px; width:40.18%">12.2</td>
			<td style="height:26px; width:38.24%">2.2</td>
		</tr>
		<tr>
			<td style="height:26px; width:21.58%">2023</td>
			<td style="height:26px; width:40.18%">18.2</td>
			<td style="height:26px; width:38.24%">3.3</td>
		</tr>
		<tr>
			<td style="height:26px; width:21.58%">2022</td>
			<td style="height:26px; width:40.18%">30.5</td>
			<td style="height:26px; width:38.24%">6.8</td>
		</tr>
		<tr>
			<td style="height:26px; width:21.58%">2021</td>
			<td style="height:26px; width:40.18%">31.6</td>
			<td style="height:26px; width:38.24%">10.5</td>
		</tr>
		<tr>
			<td style="height:26px; width:21.58%">2020</td>
			<td style="height:26px; width:40.18%">15.4</td>
			<td style="height:26px; width:38.24%">10.3</td>
		</tr>
		<tr>
			<td style="height:26px; width:21.58%">2019</td>
			<td style="height:26px; width:40.18%">1.0</td>
			<td style="height:26px; width:38.24%">3.4</td>
		</tr>
		<tr>
			<td style="height:26px; width:21.58%">2018</td>
			<td style="height:26px; width:40.18%">1.0</td>
			<td style="height:26px; width:38.24%">3.4</td>
		</tr>
		<tr>
			<td style="height:26px; width:21.58%">2017</td>
			<td style="height:26px; width:40.18%">1.4</td>
			<td style="height:26px; width:38.24%">4.7</td>
		</tr>
		<tr>
			<td style="height:26px; width:21.58%">2016</td>
			<td style="height:26px; width:40.18%">1.5</td>
			<td style="height:26px; width:38.24%">5.1</td>
		</tr>
	</tbody>
</table>
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;The early redemption ratio of Silver Bond during the aforementioned period is on average around 5 per cent of the issuance amount. This shows that the majority of senior citizens held/are holding their bonds until maturity and also reflects that Silver Bond and its issuance arrangements are supported by senior citizens. The current early redemption arrangements render Silver Bond both a safe and flexible investment option, offering senior citizens considerable liquidity in investment.]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[Postal services to Nepal subject to delays]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/14/P2026101400444.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/14/P2026101400444.htm</link><pubDate>Wed, 14 Oct 2026 14:15:00 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;​Hongkong Post announced today (October 14) that, as advised by airlines, customs clearance for mail at Tribhuvan International Airport, Kathmandu, will be suspended from October 16 to 24. Mail delivery services to Nepal are subject to delays.]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[LCQ5: Supporting development of intelligent computing industry]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/14/P2026101400415.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/14/P2026101400415.htm</link><pubDate>Wed, 14 Oct 2026 14:08:00 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;Following is a question by the Hon Elizabeth Quat and a reply by the Secretary for Innovation, Technology and Industry, Professor Sun Dong, in the Legislative Council today (October 14):<br />
<br />
Question:<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;Computing power is the most crucial factor of production in the era of artificial intelligence. Regarding supporting the development of the intelligent computing industry, will the Government inform this Council:<br />
<br />
(1) as it is learnt that the total supply of computing power in Hong Kong is currently insufficient, and that the utilisation rate of existing computing power facilities (including Cyberport&#39;s Artificial Intelligence Supercomputing Centre) has long remained high with demand exceeding supply, resulting in an interim gap in the supply of computing power in Hong Kong before the Sandy Ridge Data Facility Cluster comes into operation, and the industry has further pointed out that even after the operation of the cluster, the computing power will still be insufficient to support development needs, of the short-, medium- and long-term measures to support the development of the industry;<br />
<br />
(2) whether it will expedite the study on the development of a &quot;computing power exchange&quot; and the establishment of mechanisms for the certification, trading, settlement and pricing of computing power, so as to transform Hong Kong&#39;s traditional financial strengths into advantages in resource allocation in the era of AI, as well as explore ways to leverage the collaboration model with other Mainland cities to open up a new channel for &quot;tokens going global&quot;; and<br />
<br />
(3) as there are views that the existing planning has not fully taken into account the enormous demand for water and electricity required for developing the intelligent computing industry, whether the Government will study the construction of a &quot;dedicated water and electricity supply corridor for the intelligent computing industry&quot; from the Mainland to Hong Kong, and negotiate the addition of a &quot;dedicated cooling water quota for the intelligent computing industry in the Northern Metropolis&quot; under the existing Dongjiang water supply agreement?<br />
<br />
Reply:<br />
<br />
President:<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;The First Five-Year Plan for Economic and Social Development of the Hong Kong Special Administrative Region (2026-2030)&nbsp;identifies AI as an important industry for the future development of Hong Kong. The Hong Kong Special Administrative Region Government will actively promote the development of computing infrastructure, fostering the healthy development of AI and the intelligent computing industry.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;In response to the question raised by the Hon Elizabeth Quat, upon consulting the Development Bureau, the Environment and Ecology Bureau and the Financial Services and the Treasury Bureau, our reply is as follows:<br />
<br />
(1) Hong Kong&#39;s overall computing power has reached 5 000 peta floating-point operations per second (PFLOPS), of which Cyberport&#39;s Artificial Intelligence Supercomputing Centre (AISC) accounts for 3 000 PFLOPS. The current utilisation rate of the AISC has exceeded 90 per cent, reflecting strong market demand for AI computing services. The Government will proactively increase the supply of computing power and enhance resource utilisation through short, medium and long-term measures.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;In the short term, the Government will make good use of existing facilities and the Artificial Intelligence Subsidy Scheme (AISS) to support the research and development, model training and application needs of local research institutes, universities and enterprises. The Committee of the AISS will review arrangements for the allocation of computing power resources and their duration of use as appropriate, having regard to the actual needs of the subsidised projects and overall status of resource utilisation, with a view to enhancing its efficiency. Cyberport will also continue to review the service capacity of the AISC and future demand, and explore feasible options for increasing its computing power.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;In the medium term, the construction of the Sandy Ridge Data Facility Cluster has already commenced. The site will be developed into a regional data facility cluster of advanced data centres and related industries. It is expected to commence its first phase of operations in 2027 and become fully operational in or before 2032, when it will provide 180 000 PFLOPS of computing power, substantially increasing Hong Kong&#39;s computing power.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;In the long term, the Government is preparing to commission a new consultancy study on computing power to analyse Hong Kong&#39;s future demand. Guided by the core directions of &quot;high efficiency computing power, stable electricity supply, low-carbon transition&quot;, the study will optimise the operations of data centres and the layout of computing power in Hong Kong, providing a basis for planning the long-term development of Hong Kong&#39;s intelligent computing industry.<br />
<br />
(2) In the long run, the Government recognises that Hong Kong has the strengths and potential to support the development of activities such as computing power trading and the provision of token-based AI services to overseas markets. At present, however, the priority of Hong Kong is to strengthen its computing power infrastructure. From the perspective of financial markets, establishing a comprehensive product ecosystem, including relevant trading, settlement and price discovery mechanisms requires the foundation of a mature underlying market and a sound market ecosystem.&nbsp;&nbsp;<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;At this stage, the Government will focus on developing Hong Kong&#39;s intelligent computing industry and supporting infrastructure to strengthen the computing power supply. We will also closely monitor the latest developments in the intelligent computing industry in the Chinese Mainland and overseas, maintain communication with the industry, and explore arrangements that can enhance the efficiency of computing power resource allocation and promote the development of AI and the intelligent computing industry.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;On regional collaboration, the Government will continue to leverage Hong Kong&#39;s roles as a &quot;super connector&quot; and a &quot;super value-adder&quot; to strengthen co-operation with other cities in the Guangdong-Hong Kong-Macao Greater Bay Area (GBA) in areas such as computing power resources and market development. We also welcome service providers, including operators of computing power facilities in the GBA, to provide services to Hong Kong enterprises in response to market demand.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;As regards cross-boundary AI and computing services, including the provision of token-based AI services to overseas markets, their development must take into account factors such as data flows, cybersecurity, personal data protection, and applicable laws and regulations. The Innovation, Technology and Industry Bureau is facilitating cross-boundary co-operation and AI innovation through the facilitation measure of the Standard Contract for the Cross-boundary Flow of Personal Information within the Guangdong-Hong Kong-Macao Greater Bay Area (Mainland, Hong Kong), as well as efforts to promote the secure flow of Mainland scientific research data to the Hong Kong Park of the Hetao Shenzhen-Hong Kong Science and Technology Innovation Co-operation Zone, etc. The Government will work together with the industry and relevant stakeholders to actively explore co-operation models conducive to the long-term development of AI industry in Hong Kong, further enhancing Hong Kong&#39;s competitiveness as an international hub for AI and intelligent computing services.<br />
<br />
(3) In planning the development of the intelligent computing industry, the Government will review the supporting water and electricity infrastructure in parallel. The planned development of the industries with high water consumption in the Northern Metropolis (NM) is mainly concentrated in San Tin, Sandy Ridge and Hung Shui Kiu/Ha Tsuen. Having considered the latest development needs of the NM, including the planning information on the intelligent computing industry from the Digital Policy Office, the preliminary assessment of the Water Supplies Department (WSD) indicates that the area as a whole has sufficient supplies of fresh water and recycled water to complement its development.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;To address the water requirements of cooling systems during the operation of intelligent computing facilities, the Government will continue to encourage enterprises to adopt water-saving technologies and make good use of different water sources, including the appropriate allocation of fresh water and recycled water for cooling purposes. To provide a stable and economical water supply, the Government will consider factors such as projected water demand and the associated timetable, cost-effectiveness, the availability of fresh water in the vicinity, and the availability of effluent from tertiary sewage treatment for producing recycled water. The WSD will upgrade water supply infrastructure as appropriate to meet specific needs. There is no need to establish a dedicated water supply corridor or quota for the intelligent computing industry at this stage.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;On electricity supply, the two power companies are required under the Scheme of Control Agreements to provide, operate and maintain sufficient electricity-related facilities and supply electricity to meet demand. The CLP Power Hong Kong Limited has undertaken construction and planning work to meet the electricity demands of Hong Kong&#39;s future development, which would be sufficient to support the overall development of the I&amp;T industry (including data centres).]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[Assessment Committee supports nine applications under Pilot Innovation and Technology Accelerator Scheme]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/14/P2026100700328.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/14/P2026100700328.htm</link><pubDate>Wed, 14 Oct 2026 14:00:00 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;The Innovation and Technology Commission announced today (October 14) that the Pilot Innovation and Technology Accelerator Scheme (PITAS) Assessment Committee (the Assessment Committee) under the Innovation and Technology Fund has supported in principle nine applications. The list is as follows:<br />
&nbsp;
<ul>
	<li>BJ-HK-SZ Innotech Accelerator Limited</li>
	<li>China Outbound Accelerator Limited</li>
	<li>China Resources Corporate Services (HK) Limited</li>
	<li>Hong Kong Branch of The National Torch Academy of Innovation and Entrepreneurship Co., Limited</li>
	<li>Surrich Consulting Co., Limited</li>
	<li>Tenwings Accelevator Company Limited</li>
	<li>ventureLAB Smart City Accelerator Limited</li>
	<li>Xi&#39;an Jiaotong University Hong Kong Institute of Advanced Studies Co., Limited</li>
	<li>Zhong Xing No.1 Investment Fund Limited</li>
</ul>
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;The Secretary for Innovation, Technology and Industry, Professor Sun Dong, said, &quot;The nine selected organisations possess extensive networks and resources in areas such as AI, robotics, biomedicine, new energy and new materials. They will provide value-added services including co-working space, research and development support, investment matching and market development to accelerate the growth of innovation and technology (I&amp;T) companies in Hong Kong. The Hong Kong Special Administrative Region Government will continue to enhance the I&amp;T ecosystem and develop Hong Kong into an international I&amp;T centre.&quot;<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;PITAS aims to attract professional I&amp;T enterprise service providers with proven track records in and beyond Hong Kong to set up accelerator bases in Hong Kong and, through their business networks and expertise, support the development and needs of I&amp;T enterprises in a more comprehensive and targeted manner. Under PITAS, the Government will provide funding support on a one‑to‑two matching basis between the Government and the service provider to cover the necessary expenditure for establishing and operating an I&amp;T accelerator to a ceiling subsidy of $30 million. The project duration is up to three years.&nbsp;<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;Upon its launch, PITAS received an enthusiastic response with over 50 applications. The Assessment Committee selected the above nine organisations for funding support based on a series of assessment criteria, including the experience of I&amp;T enterprise service providers, operational models, capital and resources, quality of the management and project teams, and expected benefits to the I&amp;T ecosystem in Hong Kong, etc.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;Details of PITAS are available on the website below (<a href="https://www.itf.gov.hk/en/funding-programmes/supporting-start-ups/pitas" target="_blank">www.itf.gov.hk/en/funding-programmes/supporting-start-ups/pitas</a>). For enquiries, please contact the Secretariat of the scheme (Tel: 3543 5904; email: <a href="mailto:pitas@itc.gov.hk">pitas@itc.gov.hk</a>).]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[FS concludes visit to Nanjing (with photos/video)]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/14/P2026101400398.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/14/P2026101400398.htm</link><pubDate>Wed, 14 Oct 2026 13:46:00 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;The Financial Secretary, Mr Paul Chan, today (October 14) continued his visit to Nanjing with a delegation, meeting with the leadership of Jiangsu Province and visiting advanced manufacturing and pharmaceutical enterprises.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;Mr Chan had a breakfast meeting with member of the Standing Committee of the CPC Jiangsu Provincial Committee and Executive Vice Governor of Jiangsu Province, Mr Zhang Wenbing, this morning. They exchanged views on deepening economic and trade co-operation between Jiangsu and Hong Kong, strengthening collaboration in innovation and technology (I&amp;T), and supporting enterprises in their international expansion. Mr Chan said that the visit had strengthened the delegation&#39;s ties with the Jiangsu Provincial Government, universities, I&amp;T institutions and enterprises. Hong Kong will actively follow up on various co-operation projects to deliver more concrete results in Jiangsu-Hong Kong co-operation.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;The delegation then visited a Jiangsu-based enterprise specialising in energy and energy-saving equipment to learn about its technology research and development (R&amp;D), commercialisation of research outcomes and business development. With high-efficiency heat transfer technology at its core, the enterprise develops and manufactures key equipment for sectors such as clean energy and chemicals. Its technologies are also applied to industrial waste heat recovery and efficient energy use, helping industries conserve energy, reduce carbon emissions and pursue green transformation.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;The delegation also visited a pharmaceutical enterprise to learn about its drug R&amp;D, production and global marketing operations. They met with the enterprise&#39;s management to learn about its future development plans, including the use of artificial intelligence to enhance drug R&amp;D efficiency, the development of cutting-edge therapies, and strategies for expanding its international business.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;During the visits to the two enterprises, Mr Chan said that I&amp;T is a key area of co-operation between Jiangsu and Hong Kong. The two places have ample scope for collaboration in technology R&amp;D, the commercialisation of research outcomes and international market expansion. They can further promote two-way investment and personnel exchanges, and work together to foster deeper integration of technological and industrial innovation. On innovative drugs, enterprises can leverage Hong Kong&#39;s strengths in drug R&amp;D and the opportunities arising from reforms of its drug approval regime to accelerate the development, application and&nbsp;market expansion of innovative drugs.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;He encouraged the two enterprises to make good use of Hong Kong&#39;s internationally connected capital markets, experience in aligning with the highest international standards, high-quality professional services, and strengths as a hub for international financial institutions and talent, to scale up their businesses and bring more competitive technologies, products and solutions to overseas markets.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;Mr Chan and the delegation concluded their visit to Nanjing this afternoon and will return to Hong Kong in the early evening.<div id="photos"><a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/14/P2026101400398_photo_1341000.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/14/P2026101400398_photo_1341000.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;<a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/14/P2026101400398_photo_1341002.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/14/P2026101400398_photo_1341002.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;<a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/14/P2026101400398_photo_1341003.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/14/P2026101400398_photo_1341003.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;<a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/14/P2026101400398_photo_1341081.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/14/P2026101400398_photo_1341081.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;<a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/14/P2026101400398_photo_1341082.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/14/P2026101400398_photo_1341082.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;<a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/14/P2026101400398_photo_1341083.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/14/P2026101400398_photo_1341083.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;</div>]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[LCQ4: Combating illegal online gambling activities]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/14/P2026101400376.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/14/P2026101400376.htm</link><pubDate>Wed, 14 Oct 2026 13:35:00 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;Following is a question by the Hon Nixie Lam and a reply by the Secretary for Financial Services and the Treasury, Mr Christopher Hui, in the Legislative Council today (October 14):<br />
<br />
Question:<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;It has been reported that, over the years, unlawful organisations have operated illegal gambling activities through online platforms and used concealed channels, such as stooge accounts, to handle gambling funds. In this connection, will the Government inform this Council:<br />
<br />
(1) in the light of the distinctive nature of illegal online gambling, under which betting funds are transferred through online channels, of the measures the authorities have put in place to support financial institutions in introducing smart technology systems that can automatically detect and proactively intercept transactions relating to illegal betting, and issue risk alerts to members of the public in a timely manner;<br />
<br />
(2) of the number of persons arrested in the past three years for money laundering activities involving the use of stooge accounts to handle money from illegal betting, and among such cases, the total number of those in which prosecutions were successful, with a breakdown by type of stooge account (e.g. bank accounts and electronic wallet accounts); and<br />
<br />
(3) as it is learnt that criminal syndicates commonly disseminate information on illegal betting through social media, messaging applications and key opinion leaders, whether the authorities will, with reference to overseas experience, enact legislation requiring online platforms to discharge statutory duties to screen, remove and report information relating to illegal betting, and empowering law enforcement agencies to mandate platforms to remove unlawful content, ban offending accounts and block traffic, while at the same time formulating a penalty mechanism for platforms, with a view to combating the promotion of illegal gambling at source; if so, of the specific legislative approach and timetable; if not, the reasons for that?<br />
<br />
Reply:<br />
<br />
President,<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;Illegal gambling is an indictable offence under the Gambling Ordinance (Cap. 148). Pursuant to the Organized and Serious Crimes Ordinance (Cap. 455) (OSCO), any person who knows or suspects that any property is connected with the proceeds of an indictable offence, including illegal gambling, is required to promptly file a suspicious transaction report (STR) with the Joint Financial Intelligence Unit (JFIU).<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;Having consulted the Home and Youth Affairs Bureau, the Security Bureau, the Hong Kong Monetary Authority (HKMA) and the Securities and Futures Commission (SFC), my reply to the three parts of the question is as follows:<br />
<br />
(1) Under the existing regulatory framework, various regulated entities (including banks and stored value facility (SVF) licensees regulated by the HKMA, as well as licensed corporations and licensed virtual asset trading platforms regulated by the SFC) must establish effective and appropriate anti-money laundering and counter-financing of terrorism (AML/CFT) controls, covering customer due diligence, transaction monitoring, and suspicious transaction reporting.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;Regarding transaction monitoring, regulated entities are required to establish ongoing monitoring and transaction monitoring systems that are commensurate with the nature, size and risk profile of their businesses to promptly identify transactions inconsistent with a client&#39;s background or financial profile. Depending on the level of risk identified, regulated entities should adopt appropriate measures, such as conducting stricter customer due diligence, verifying client instructions, restricting or suspending transactions, and refusing to execute transactions. In addition, after filing STRs, regulated entities have to co-operate with law enforcement agencies on their follow-up actions as appropriate, including restricting or suspending the operation of relevant accounts, and restraining or freezing relevant assets, in accordance with the directions of law enforcement agencies or court orders.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;The HKMA and the SFC have respectively issued detailed guidelines pursuant to the Anti-Money Laundering and Counter-Terrorist Financing Ordinance (Cap. 615), setting out the AML/CFT controls that various regulated entities should implement to combat money laundering and terrorist financing (ML/TF) activities.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;To assist the industry in more promptly and accurately identifying and mitigating ML/TF risks, the HKMA and the SFC will continue to engage with the industry and promote the responsible use of technologies such as artificial intelligence. Specifically, the HKMA has organised various industry sharing sessions to encourage banks and SVF licensees to enhance their application of new and emerging financial technologies and assist them in applying artificial intelligence within their transaction monitoring systems, thereby enhancing their ability to detect illicit financial activities. Meanwhile, in November 2024, the SFC published the Report on the Adoption of Regtech for Anti-Money Laundering and Counter-Financing of Terrorism to share practical use cases and good practices adopted by licensed corporations in key AML (Anti-Money Laundering) processes such as customer due diligence, transaction monitoring, and management information reporting. The aim is to encourage and assist the industry in adopting Regtech (including artificial intelligence) more widely in a responsible manner according to the nature, scale and risk profile of their businesses, thereby enhancing the effectiveness of AML efforts.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;In respect of intelligence sharing, the HKMA and the SFC have maintained close co-operation with the Hong Kong Police Force (HKPF) and the industry to share intelligence in a timely manner, including the latest ML (Money Laundering) typologies and modus operandi, related fund flows, the use of stooge accounts, and red flag indicators of suspicious transactions, with a view to assisting regulated entities in promptly updating their transaction monitoring scenarios and risk-mitigating measures. In addition, the Fraud and Money Laundering Intelligence Taskforce, an intelligence-sharing platform comprising the HKPF, the HKMA, banks, and SVF licensees, has circulated multiple alerts to share emerging threats and the latest modus operandi regarding illegal gambling with the industry.<br />
<br />
(2) Section 25 of the OSCO stipulates that a person commits an offence (i.e. ML) if they deal with any property that they know or have reasonable grounds to believe represents the proceeds of an indictable offense, including illegal gambling. Apart from using stooge accounts to launder money, selling or lending bank accounts to be used as stooge accounts may also constitute the aforementioned criminal offence. Over the past three years, statistics on the numbers of persons arrested, prosecuted and convicted for ML involving stooge accounts are set out in the Annex.<br />
<br />
(3) As a matter of policy, the Government does not encourage gambling. To address the possible problems brought by gambling, the Government adopts a multi-pronged strategy including law enforcement against illegal gambling activities, public education on the harms of gambling addiction, provision of counselling and support services to people in need, and regulation over gambling activities through legislation. The HKPF has been closely monitoring the trend of illegal gambling crimes, conducting cyber patrols and gathering intelligence through different channels, and taking appropriate intelligence-led enforcement actions against these illegal activities. Besides, the HYAB established the Ping Wo Fund (PWF) in 2003 to finance both preventive and remedial measures to address gambling-related problems.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;To address problems arising from illegal online gambling, the PWF has accorded priority to providing funding support for initiatives relating to the prevention of online gambling activities since 2020. The PWF has been disseminating anti-gambling messages to the public through various channels, and will continue to remind the public that participating in illegal gambling (such as betting with an illegal bookmaker) is an offence under the Gambling Ordinance, regardless of whether it takes place online or offline, such as placing bets with an illegal bookmaker through a simple tap on mobile phone. Upon conviction, an offender is liable to a maximum penalty of a $50,000 fine and imprisonment for nine months. Furthermore, under the Gambling Ordinance, no person shall, whether online or offline, knowingly promote or facilitate bookmaking or betting with a bookmaker. Upon conviction, an offender is liable to a maximum penalty of a $5 million fine and imprisonment for seven years. Through various publicity and education programmes, we will continue to enhance public awareness, particularly among young people, on the harms of gambling addiction and the legal consequences of participating in illegal gambling, with a view to further strengthening public awareness of anti-gambling.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;Thank you, President.]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[LCQ3: Review of taxi service arrangements]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/14/P2026101400365.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/14/P2026101400365.htm</link><pubDate>Wed, 14 Oct 2026 13:25:00 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;Following is a question by the Hon Chris Ip and a reply by the Secretary for Transport and Logistics, Ms Mable Chan, in the Legislative Council today (October 14):<br />
<br />
Question:<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;Some residents in the New Territories have complained that it is difficult to hire New Territories taxis (commonly known as &quot;green taxis&quot;) to travel to destinations in the New Territories during peak hours, and that when they attempt to take urban taxis (commonly known as &quot;red taxis&quot;) plying for hire at taxi stands, they have repeatedly been subjected to refusal of hire. In this connection, will the Government inform this Council:<br />
&nbsp;<br />
(1) of the number of complaints received by the authorities and the number of drivers with points incurred since the implementation of the Taxi-Driver-Offence Points system, with a breakdown by the nature of the offences; the number of drivers who have been disqualified from driving a taxi due to accumulated points;<br />
&nbsp;<br />
(2) as there are views pointing out that drivers of red taxis plying for hire at public transport interchanges provided with both New Territories and urban taxi stands generally expect to pick up passengers travelling to urban areas, whether the authorities will, by drawing on the operational model of cross-harbour taxi stands, set up dedicated taxi stands at some interchanges for passengers bound for urban areas; if so, of the details; if not, the reasons for that; and<br />
&nbsp;<br />
(3) as there are views that point-to-point transport demand in the New Territories will continue to increase in the future, whether the Government will, apart from adjusting the number of ride-hailing vehicle licences, review the number of taxi licences in a timely manner or enhance the existing service arrangements for urban and New Territories taxis; if so, of the details; if not, the reasons for that?<br />
&nbsp;<br />
Reply:<br />
<br />
President,<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;The Government has all along endeavoured to enhance taxi service quality and improve the trade&#39;s image. Over the past few years, the Government has implemented a series of measures, including introducing taxi fleets, enhancing the taxi written test, implementing the Taxi-Driver-Offence Points (TDOP) system and the two-tier penalty system, as well as requiring all taxi drivers to accept electronic payments. In addition, Journey Recording Systems, comprising the functions of a dash camera, an in-vehicle recording system and a global navigation satellite system, will be gradually installed in taxi compartments starting from this year, with a view to enhancing passenger experience and deterring malpractices of taxi drivers.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;My reply to the Hon Ip&#39;s question is as follows &ndash;<br />
&nbsp;<br />
(1) The TDOP system has officially come into effect on September 22, 2024, covering 11 relatively serious taxi-driver-related offences under the legislation. A taxi driver who is convicted of any of these offences or is liable to pay a fixed penalty will incur the corresponding points. In accordance with the law, a taxi driver must not, without reasonable excuse, willfully refuse or neglect to accept a hire, nor refuse or neglect to drive the taxi to any place indicated by the hirer (refusing hire). The Police has all along been handling cases involving refusing hire seriously, and will seek advice from the Department of Justice and initiate prosecution if there is sufficient evidence. Any person who commits refusing hire will incur with the maximum of 10 points under the TDOP system.<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;As of October 2, 2026, a total of 229 taxi drivers had incurred points, of which 125 cases were for refusing hire. Out of all the 229 drivers with points incurred, three drivers have been disqualified from driving a taxi (for a period of three months) owing to an incurrence of 15 points or more. Breakdown is set out at Annex I.<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;In addition, from the implementation of the TDOP system to August 2026, the Transport Department (TD) had received a total of 5 944 taxi-driver-related complaints, of which 605 cases involved refusing hire. Breakdown is set out at Annex II. Upon receipt of complaints, TD will conduct investigations and refer them to the law enforcement agency for follow-up depending on the nature of the cases. TD will also issue letters to the taxi owners or drivers concerned to request explanations, reminding them to comply with the legislation and pay attention to their service attitude, with a view to keeping up service quality.<br />
&nbsp;<br />
(2) In accordance with the law, New Territories (NT) taxis have designated operating areas, which mainly cover the north-eastern NT (i.e. north of Sha Tin) and the north-western NT (i.e. north of Tsuen Wan), and their fares are lower than those of urban taxis. On the other hand, urban taxis can pick up passengers and take them anywhere (except Tung Chung Road and roads in South Lantau).<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;TD has set up urban and NT taxi stands at locations with concentrated passenger demand, such as the Hong Kong International Airport and various land boundary control points, with a view to facilitating the diversion of passengers heading for different areas. At present, the operation is generally smooth. However, I must emphasise that a taxi driver must not refuse a hire without reasonable excuse according to the law. In other words, drivers at urban taxi stands are also required to proceed to any destination indicated by the hirers, including the NT. Setting up designated taxi stands for specific destinations may not cater for the diverse point-to-point transportation demand of passengers and may cause confusion (especially for tourists). Therefore, we consider that maintaining the current simple and clear arrangement is more appropriate and preferable.<br />
&nbsp;<br />
(3) The Government has been upholding the public transport-oriented policy with railway as the backbone, complemented by well-networked mass transport modes (such as franchised buses and the Light Rail) to provide efficient services for the public. On the other hand, public light buses and taxis play a supplementary role, with the latter providing personalised point-to-point services, currently accounting for about 6 per cent of the overall public transport patronage.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;At present, there are 18 163 registered taxis in Hong Kong (with NT taxis accounting for 2 838). With the regional development of the NT, there is a steady demand for taxi services in many parts of the region. It is therefore necessary to maintain the arrangement of NT taxis to ensure adequate services available to passengers travelling between districts within the NT as well as residents in rural and remote areas of the NT. In the future, we will continue to optimise the operational arrangements for NT taxis. Firstly, in response to changes in demand, new development areas (including the Northern Metropolis), and the commencement of major infrastructure facilities, we will conduct timely reviews on the permitted operating areas of NT taxis. For example, operating areas of NT taxis have been expanded to cover the Hong Kong-Zhuhai-Macao Bridge Hong Kong Port and the Hong Kong Port Area of the recently commissioned Huanggang Port. Last year, as part of the efforts to optimise airport operations, the Government also permitted NT taxis to operate on the newly designated pre-booked pick-up area and associated roads.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;Furthermore, to better understand passengers&#39; demand for taxi services and the operating status of the taxi trade, TD conducts annual surveys on the overall taxi service level. According to the survey conducted in early 2026, the overall supply of taxis has remained largely stable in recent years. We will continue to closely monitor the situation and maintain communication with the taxi trade, so to ensure the stable and orderly provision of taxi services and the healthy development of the trade.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;Meanwhile, to enhance personalised point-to-point public transport services, the Government has introduced a regulatory regime for ride-hailing services through legislation, thereby providing passengers with more safe and compliant transport options. We will also require licensed platforms to provide taxi-hailing services to facilitate commuters. When licensed ride-hailing platforms commence operation in the future, we will have a full understanding of the platforms&#39; operational data, closely monitor market developments, and take holistic account of the operating environment of taxis, in the course of conducting a dynamic assessment of demand and supply.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;The Government will continue to promote the complementary strengths and healthy competition between taxis and ride-hailing vehicles on the premise of maintaining the efficient operation of the public transport system, with a view to fostering the long-term healthy development of the personalised point-to-point transport service industry.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;Thank you, President.]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[LCQ8: Rodent prevention and control]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/14/P2026101400325.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/14/P2026101400325.htm</link><pubDate>Wed, 14 Oct 2026 13:00:00 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;Following is a question by the Hon Chan Pok-chi and a written reply by the Secretary for Environment and Ecology, Mr Tse Chin-wan, in the Legislative Council today (October 14):<br />
<br />
Question:<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;Rodent infestation has persisted in Hong Kong in recent years, affecting the living environment of the public and public health. In this connection, will the Government inform this Council:<br />
<br />
(1) of the number of public complaints received by the Food and Environmental Hygiene Department (FEHD) about illegal disposal of food waste and refuse by restaurants in the 18 districts across the territory over the past year; in view of the risk of rodent infestation posed by the disposal of food waste by restaurants, of the specific plans put in place by the FEHD for combating the problem, cleansing and enforcement, so as to eliminate the food sources of rodents;<br />
<br />
(2) of the frequency and implementation details of the FEHD&#39;s current inspections and blitz checks to monitor outsourced rodent control contractors; how to prevent contractors from falsely reporting the effectiveness of rodent control work through improper handling of trapped rodents; whether it will further improve the deployment of baits and bait boxes, including different bait options such as heated sweet potatoes and other suitable food items, thereby enhancing the actual effectiveness of rodent trapping and control;<br />
<br />
(3) of the total number of participants that have signed the Anti-rodent Charter in Hong Kong to date; of the regular monitoring and effectiveness review mechanisms put in place by the FEHD for the participants to ensure their due performance of rodent prevention and control commitments; in view of the recurrent rodent infestation in Hong Kong, of the regular cross-departmental long-term plans put in place by the FEHD to eliminate at source the three fundamental survival conditions of rodents, namely food, harbourages and dispersal routes, thereby sustaining the overall environmental hygiene standards across the territory;<br />
<br />
(4) given that the Chief Executive&#39;s 2026 Policy Address has proposed to enhance 1823 services, whether the FEHD will make use of the rodent complaint data to better identify rodent blackspots and adjust prevention and control resources; if so, of the details and timetable; if not, the reasons for that; and<br />
<br />
(5) of the coverage, trial progress and effectiveness data of the FEHD&#39;s current application of various technological rodent control tools in various districts; whether it will fully expand the application of technologies such as smart monitoring and digitalised rodent infestation assessment, establish a regular data monitoring and precise enforcement mechanism to replace the traditional approach of relying solely on manual inspections, and formulate long-term strategies and performance indicators for tech-driven rodent control?<br />
<br />
Reply:<br />
<br />
President,<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;Pest and rodent control is an important and ongoing task of the Government. The Food and Environmental Hygiene Department (FEHD) has all along been adopting targeted measures to eliminate the fundamental survival conditions of rodents, namely food, harbourage and passages. Regarding the questions from the Hon Chan Pok-chi, the reply is as follows:<br />
<br />
(1) In the past year (i.e. from October 2025 to September 2026), the FEHD received a total of 654 complaints concerning the illegal disposal of food waste and refuse by restaurants.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;The FEHD attaches great importance to the environmental hygiene problems (including rodent infestation) caused by the illegal disposal of food waste and refuse by restaurants. It has therefore been conducting regular inspections of licensed or permitted food premises across the territory while taking stringent enforcement action against non-compliance. For premises with repeated contraventions, the FEHD also conducts surprise operations to step up enforcement.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;In recent years, the FEHD has implemented a trial scheme to regulate disposal arrangement of waste generated by food premises, which allows licensed food premises, subject to specific conditions, to place large-size covered waste containers in the adjoining rear lanes to temporarily store refuse awaiting collection, with a view to improving the environmental hygiene of the rear lanes concerned and thereby effectively prevent rodent infestation. At present, 192 rear lanes across the territory have been incorporated into the scheme.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;In response to recent incidents involving pest and rodent infestation and food hygiene in food premises, the FEHD launched a two-week special inspection and enforcement operation codenamed &quot;Operation Skynet&quot; from September 30. In addition to stepping up inspections of the environmental hygiene and food handling conditions of food premises, the operation also focuses on examining whether premises have implemented prevention and control at source, including the handling of refuse and food waste, food storage, rodent prevention facilities, hygiene in back-of-house and storage areas, as well as environmental factors that may facilitate the ingress and egress of rodents or pests. Based on the inspection results, the FEHD will take enforcement action against the operators, put forward specific improvement requirements, and conduct follow-up inspections on key or problematic premises to ensure that operators continuously implement the improvement measures. As at October 6, the FEHD has inspected 715 supermarkets, 123 food premises and 114 other food premises, issued 82 Notices of Elimination of Vermin, 38 Notices of Requirement to Cleanse and Repair Food Premises and one warning, and instituted 25 prosecutions.<br />
<br />
(2) The FEHD has established a stringent mechanism for monitoring its outsourced pest control contractors. In addition to conducting regular inspections, the FEHD reviews and approves operational plans submitted by contractors and, based on a risk-based approach, carries out surprise inspections and spot checks having regard to complaints received, inspection findings, contractors&#39; performance and the actual circumstances of individual locations. The FEHD also verifies contractors&#39; work records to comprehensively monitor whether rodent control operations are carried out in accordance with contractual requirements and operational guidelines.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;To further strengthen the monitoring of contractors&#39; handling of captured rodents and their work records, the FEHD has implemented enhancement measures, requiring contractors to specify in their work plans the designated work location with greater detail, strengthening trap management measures to ensure the accuracy of rodent control reports, and maintaining photographic records with time and geolocation stamps. In addition, the FEHD requires contractors to ensure that all frontline workers and supervisory staff are fully familiar with and comply with the relevant operational guidelines, and to provide professional training for frontline pest control staff.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;In respect of the use of baits for rodent trapping, the FEHD tests different food baits at individual locations according to the actual circumstances and deploys suitable mixed baits, as rodents&#39; food preferences may be affected by environmental factors, human activity patterns and rodent behaviour. Frontline staff also regularly inspects and replaces the baits to ensure their effectiveness in attracting rodents.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;Apart from conventional rodent cages, the FEHD also deploys a variety of trapping devices, having regard to site conditions and the level of rodent infestation. These include Ekomille in public markets and refuse collection points, as well as glue traps placed during nighttime operations.<br />
<br />
(3) Effective rodent control requires the concerted efforts of stakeholders and members of the public, in maintaining good hygiene habits and proactively adopt rodent prevention measures. The Environment and Ecology Bureau coordinates the Cross-sectoral Territory-wide Anti-rodent Action, consolidating strengths from the government departments and various sectors of the community, including the property management sector, market stall operators and hawkers, the catering industry, the construction industry and the pest control trade, for a cross-sectoral, multidisciplinary and community-wide approach to rodent prevention and control. The Under Secretary for Environment and Ecology also chairs the interdepartmental Pest control Steering Committee, which regularly reviews measures on rodent prevention and control implemented across various departments and organisations, formulates strategies and drives departments to take the lead in strengthening rodent prevention and control efforts within the sectors under their purview.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;The FEHD has also invited property management companies of residential premises and shopping centres, owners&#39; corporations and residents&#39; organisations to sign the Anti-rodent Charter, and to assign anti-rodent liaison ambassadors to coordinate anti-rodent efforts within their premises. The FEHD will conduct on-site inspections and provide targeted rodent prevention and control advice. The Charter was launched at the end of 2024. To date, about 840 residential premises and about 130 shopping centres signed the Charter, covering about 780 000 households and about 8 400 shops respectively.<br />
<br />
(4) and (5) The FEHD has fully adopted thermal imaging cameras with AI technology to conduct Rodent Activity Survey (RAS) and generate Rodent Absence Rate (RAR) to monitor the rodent infestation situation in districts. At present, the scope of the RAS covers public places under the FEHD, public housing estates under the Housing Department, parks managed by the Leisure and Cultural Services Department, rental housing estates under the Hong Kong Housing Society, fixed-pitch hawker areas and village house areas.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;The FEHD adopts a data-driven and risk-based rodent prevention and control strategy, analysing data including from the RAR, frontline inspections, public complaints, actual environmental conditions and the views of local stakeholders, to comprehensively assess the rodent infestation risks of different locations. Resources are deployed, on a risk-based approach, to locations with higher rodent infestation risks, to continuously conduct targeted rodent control operations, with a view to optimising resource utilisation and enhancing the overall effectiveness of rodent control work. Should private premises be found to be affected by rodent infestation, the FEHD will proactively conduct inspections and provide professional technical advice. Where necessary, Notice of Elimination of Vermin will be issued to the responsible persons, requiring them to properly handle the rodent infestation within a specified period.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;The Government will continue to monitor the rodent infestation situation in Hong Kong and encourage more stakeholders to participate in rodent prevention and control, with a view to jointly building a rodent-free community.]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[LCQ1: Foreign domestic helpers]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/14/P2026101400341.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/14/P2026101400341.htm</link><pubDate>Wed, 14 Oct 2026 12:33:48 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Following is a question by the Hon Tommy Chung and a reply by the Secretary for Labour and Welfare, Mr Chris Sun, in the Legislative Council today (October 14):<br />
<br />
Question:<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;There are views relaying that given the lack of flexibility in the existing Standard Employment Contract and supporting policies, employers of foreign domestic helpers (FDHs) are facing a number of problems. In this connection, will the Government inform this Council:<br />
<br />
(1) whether it will study introducing a statutory probation period (e.g. two to three months) in the employment contracts of FDHs, reviewing the arrangement under which employers are required to provide free passage so that FDHs have to pay for their airfares in proportion to their length of service, and limiting the agency fees charged for re-hiring to a specific percentage of the amount paid for initial hiring so as to protect the rights and interests of employers; if so, of the details; if not, the reasons for that;<br />
<br />
(2) whether it will formulate in collaboration with the Equal Opportunities Commission the practical guidelines on compliant departure and contract termination targeting FDHs to provide objective medical assessment standards and standard mediation procedures for situations where FDHs are unable to perform duties due to serious illness or other reasons; if so, of the details; if not, the reasons for that; and<br />
<br />
(3) whether it will amend the legislation to stipulate that FDHs who are dismissed upon conviction for criminal offences will immediately forfeit their eligibility to any statutory compensation, payment in lieu of notice and passage allowance, while the Immigration Department will also prohibit them from coming to Hong Kong to work again; if so, of the details; if not, the reasons for that?<br />
<br />
Reply:<br />
<br />
President,<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The Government attaches great importance to the reasonable rights and interests of both foreign domestic helpers (FDHs) and employers, and is committed to balancing the concerns and interests of both parties, while maintaining Hong Kong as an attractive work destination for a sufficient number of FDHs, so as to meet the community&#39;s increasing domestic care needs. Having consulted the Immigration Department (ImmD) and the Equal Opportunities Commission (EOC), my reply to the question is as follows:<br />
<br />
(1) Under the Standard Employment Contract (SEC) for employing FDHs, either party may terminate the employment contract by giving the other party not less than one month&#39;s notice in writing, or paying one month&#39;s wages in lieu of notice. If an FDH wilfully disobeys a lawful and reasonable order, misconducts himself/herself, is guilty of fraud or dishonesty, or is habitually neglectful in his/her duties, the employer may also summarily dismiss the FDH under the Employment Ordinance (Cap. 57) (EO) without notice or payment in lieu of notice. Therefore, under the existing arrangements, employers may already terminate employment contracts in accordance with the EO and the terms of the SEC where necessary. The Government considers that there is no need to introduce into the SEC an additional provision prescribing a probationary period.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;The SEC also provides that the employer shall be responsible for the FDH&#39;s passage for returning to his/her place of origin upon termination or expiry of the contract. This arrangement is one of the necessary conditions for allowing employers to recruit domestic helpers from overseas to work in Hong Kong. Its purpose is to ensure that FDHs who come to Hong Kong will not be left stranded in Hong Kong due to a lack of means for travel, thereby avoiding potential public order and other social problems. Other labour importation schemes also require employers to bear the same responsibility. The Government has no plan to amend this requirement.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;At present, employers may hire FDHs through employment agencies (EAs) or on their own. The fees charged by EAs depend on their scope of services and their agreements with employers. These are a matter of market practice, and it is not appropriate for the Government to prescribe the mode and level of relevant fees. To strengthen the protection of the rights and interests of FDH employers as consumers, the Labour Department (LD) revised the Code of Practice for Employment Agencies in May 2024, requiring EAs to clearly state whether, if an FDH prematurely terminates the contract, they will provide a refund, a replacement FDH or other arrangements, and whether additional fees will be charged, with a view to enhancing the transparency of charging and service arrangements and enabling employers to make informed decisions when choosing EAs. EAs breaching the Code of Practice for Employment Agencies may be subject to disciplinary actions by the LD.<br />
<br />
(2) The EOC has formulated the Code of Practice on Employment under the Disability Discrimination Ordinance, which provides practical guidance for employers and employees in all types of occupations (including FDHs and their employers), explaining how to prevent disability discrimination in the workplace and how to effectively implement the Disability Discrimination Ordinance (Cap. 487) (DDO) in the workplace. Upon receipt of a complaint of disability discrimination, the EOC will handle it in accordance with the established procedures, which include, with the consent of the parties concerned and having regard to all relevant information, assisting FDHs and their employers to clarify the issues in dispute and reach a settlement through conciliation.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;Under the DDO, it is unlawful for an employer to discriminate against or dismiss an employee on the ground of the employee&#39;s disability. Nevertheless, the law does not stipulate that employers are prohibited from dismissing the employees concerned under all circumstances. An exception may apply if the employer can present objective evidence to prove that the employee concerned is unable to perform the inherent requirements of the job due to the employee&#39;s disability. If an employee is still able to perform the inherent requirements of the job, but requires adjustments to the working arrangements, the employer may consider providing appropriate accommodation having regard to the specific circumstances of the case. Such accommodation may include flexible working arrangements to allow the sick employee to attend medical appointments, take rest, etc. An exception under the DDO may also apply if the accommodation required would impose an unjustifiable hardship on the employer.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;The Government encourages employers and FDHs to make reference to the Code of Practice on Employment under the Disability Discrimination Ordinance and work together to foster a mutually beneficial work environment for both parties.<br />
<br />
(3) As there are many types of criminal offences and their severity varies, they cannot be generalised. However, as I have just mentioned, if an FDH, in relation to his/her employment, is guilty of fraud or dishonesty, for example, where he/she is convicted by the court of stealing property from the employer&#39;s home, the EO currently already provides that the employer may summarily dismiss the FDH without notice or payment in lieu of notice. However, the employer is still required to provide the FDH with return passage to his/her place of origin. This arrangement is one of the necessary conditions for allowing employers to recruit domestic helpers from overseas to work in Hong Kong, and is intended to ensure that FDHs who come to Hong Kong will not be left stranded in Hong Kong due to a lack of means for travel.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;The ImmD has all along been assessing employment visa applications from FDHs in a rigorous manner, and will consider each application having regard to its individual circumstances. An FDH must meet the eligibility criteria set out in the Guidebook for the Employment of Domestic Helpers from Abroad, including having no known record to the detriment of him/her, and must also meet the normal immigration requirements, such as being of clear criminal record, before his/her application will be considered for approval. If an FDH has any adverse record or has committed any breach, the ImmD will normally refuse his/her application.]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[LCQ20: Strengthening promotion of vocational and professional education and training]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/14/P2026101400287.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/14/P2026101400287.htm</link><pubDate>Wed, 14 Oct 2026 12:33:00 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Following is a question by the Hon Jimmy Ng and a written reply by the Secretary for Education, Dr Choi Yuk-lin, in the Legislative Council today (October 14):<br />
&nbsp;<br />
Question:<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; The Government has been promoting the development of vocational and professional education and training (VPET) for many years. With the Government&#39;s support, the Vocational Training Council (VTC) has implemented the Pilot Incentive Scheme to Employers (PISE) under the Training and Support Scheme (the Support Scheme) to encourage employers to provide workplace learning and assessment for learners, thereby enhancing their work performance. It is learnt that there are public concerns as to whether the Government will further promote and support the development of VPET. In this connection, will the Government inform this Council:<br />
&nbsp;<br />
(1) as far as the Support Scheme is concerned, (i) of the respective numbers of employers and learners participating in the Support Scheme in the past three school years, with a breakdown by industry; (ii) whether it will increase the training places and raise the ceiling on the monthly subsidy for learners under the Support Scheme; if so, of the details; if not, the reasons for that; (iii) whether it will provide more incentives for employers to encourage more industrial and commercial enterprises to participate in the Support Scheme; if so, of the details;<br />
&nbsp;<br />
(2) as far as the PISE is concerned, (i) of the respective numbers of employers and learners participating in the PISE in the past three school years, with a breakdown by industry; (ii) whether it will study raising the ceiling on the subsidy for employers under the PISE; if so, of the details; (iii) whether it will consider extending the PISE to cover VPET courses organised by institutions other than the VTC; (iv) whether it will study formulating industry-recognised standards for skills tests; if so, of the details; (v) whether it will further extend or regularise the PISE; if so, of the details; if not, the reasons for that; and<br />
&nbsp;<br />
(3) whether it will consider launching more new schemes to strengthen VPET and apprenticeship training; if so, of the details; if not, the reasons for that?<br />
&nbsp;<br />
Reply:<br />
&nbsp;<br />
President,<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; In line with the strategic deployment of the National 15th Five-Year Plan to promote the integrated development of education, technology and talent, the Hong Kong Special Administrative Region Government&#39;s First Five-Year Plan clearly states that the Government will advance the high-quality development of vocational and professional education and training (VPET). To this end, the Education Bureau (EDB) continues to adopt the strategy of &quot;fostering industry-institution collaboration and diversified development&quot; under a multi-pronged approach to promote VPET as a pathway parallel to conventional academic education, encouraging industry-education collaboration and the collaboration between schools and businesses, providing diversified learning and employment opportunities, thereby nurturing more high-quality talent equipped with applied skills.<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; The reply to the Hon Jimmy Ng&#39;s question is as follows:<br />
&nbsp;<br />
(1) The Government has supported the Vocational Training Council (VTC) to launch the Training and Support Scheme (the Support Scheme) on a pilot basis since the 2014/15 academic year (AY), and has regularised it since the 2019/20 AY. The Support Scheme aims to provide structured classroom learning and on-the-job training, as well as a stable income for trainees undergoing on-the-job training, so as to attract and retain talent for industries with a keen manpower demand. The Support Scheme currently covers six professional fields, namely Engineering and Technology; Transportation; Design, Jewellery, Creative Industry; Healthcare Services; Testing and Certification; and Hospitality, and offers 1 200 training places each financial year.<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The numbers of employers and trainees participating in the Support Scheme in the recent three AYs (i.e. 2024/25, 2025/26 and 2026/27 AYs) are as follows:
<table border="1" style="width:567px">
	<tbody>
		<tr>
			<td rowspan="2" style="height:26px; width:142px">Professional field</td>
			<td rowspan="2" style="height:26px; width:180px">Industry and relevant programme</td>
