			<td style="height:21px; width:76px">180/783</td>
		</tr>
	</tbody>
</table>
* Participating employers may employ trainees in more than one industry.<br />
# The figures of 2026/27 AY are up to September 30, 2026.<br />
^ No relevant programme is offered in that year.<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; The Support Scheme has received positive responses since its launch. The training places and monthly allowance ceiling that are currently offered can generally meet the demand, and industry participation has been satisfactory. The VTC has also maintained close communication with the industries to ensure that the Support Scheme aligns with industry needs and the development of emerging industries while providing sufficient training places. The VTC will continue to closely monitor the implementation of the Support Scheme and feedbacks from the industries, and will review and make appropriate adjustments timely when needed.<br />
&nbsp;<br />
(2) The Government has supported the VTC to implement the Pilot Incentive Scheme to Employers (the Pilot Scheme) under the Support Scheme since the 2019/20 AY. The Pilot Scheme mainly encourages employers to conduct systematic assessment on workplace learning for trainees participating in the Support Scheme. Through assessing and observing directly trainees&#39; abilities and performance, employers could keep track of trainees&#39; learning progress and make timely adjustments to the training content based on the assessment results, thereby effectively enhancing the trainees&#39; overall performance. To encourage employers&#39; participation in the Pilot Scheme, the Government provides participating employers with a subsidy of up to $36,000 per trainee, with 1 000 places offered each AY.<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; The numbers of employers and trainees participating in the Pilot Scheme in the past three AYs# (i.e. 2023/24, 2024/25 and 2025/26 AYs) are as follows:
<table border="1" style="width:577px">
	<tbody>
		<tr>
