# As the 2026/27 AY has just commenced and the Scheme has not yet been implemented, the relevant figures are not yet available.<br />
* Participating employers may employ trainees in more than one industry.<br />
^ No relevant programme is offered in that year.<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; According to the survey conducted by the VTC, most participating employers were satisfied with the Pilot Scheme and indicated that it enabled them to better understand trainees&#39; abilities. Most trainees indicated that the Pilot Scheme allowed them to demonstrate their learned skills in the workplace, and agreed that the assessment results could enhance the effectiveness of on-the-job training. On the whole, the Pilot Scheme has received positive responses since its launch. The VTC will continue to closely monitor the implementation of the Pilot Scheme and feedbacks from the industries, and will review various arrangements timely, including whether to extend the Pilot Scheme to cover more industries and their relevant programmes.<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; In addition, under the Qualifications Framework (QF), Industry Training Advisory Committees have been established for 23 industries. They have respectively developed &quot;Specification of Competency Standards&quot; (SCS) tailored to different job functions and roles, setting out the skills and knowledge practitioners must possess, as well as the competency standards they must attain to perform various job functions in the industries. These SCS serve as key reference for talent development, curriculum design and qualifications recognition. Among them, 19 industries have established Recognition of Prior Learning mechanisms based on their SCS, enabling practitioners to have their work-based knowledge, skills and experience formally recognised through relevant tests or assessments, thereby obtaining corresponding QF-recognised qualifications.<br />
&nbsp;<br />
(3) In recent years, the VTC has strengthened VPET at the secondary school level to help students gain an earlier understanding of the industries and career pathways. With the support of the EDB, the VTC has launched the following two pilot schemes to enable students to gain earlier exposure to VPET, discover their interests and broaden their VPET articulation pathways:<br />
&nbsp;
<ol>
	<li>The Pilot Diploma of Vocational Baccalaureate Programme was launched in the 2018/19 AY, offered by the Youth College under the VTC. It is a three-year vocationally-oriented programme with reference to international standards.&nbsp;Upon completion, students will be awarded an internationally recognised diploma, and may choose to directly articulate to local or overseas undergraduate programmes, to Higher Diploma programmes offered by the VTC, or pursue a career in their respective industries. The Programme has been regularised starting from the 2026/27 AY; and</li>
	<li>The Pilot Project on Diploma of Vocational Education (Senior Secondary Curriculum) (DVE(SSC)) was launched in the 2023/24 AY, allowing senior secondary school students to pursue VPET-related programme embedded in the Hong Kong Diploma of Secondary Education (HKDSE) curriculum. Upon completion, students will be awarded, alongside their HKDSE qualification, a DVE(SSC) at QF Level 3, enabling them to apply for relevant higher diploma programmes offered by the VTC. The Project has been extended for three AYs starting from the 2026/27 AY.</li>
</ol>
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; As regards apprenticeship training, in view of the persistent shortage of local technical professionals, the Government will continue to promote the Apprenticeship Scheme through the VTC, providing structured apprenticeship training and clear progression pathways. To encourage more young people to join the Scheme and enter the relevant trades, the 2026 Policy Address announced that the provision of an additional monthly training allowance of $1,000 to registered apprentices since 2024-25 will be extended for two years to 2028-29.]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[LCQ19: Providing support to centenarians]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/14/P2026101400298.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/14/P2026101400298.htm</link><pubDate>Wed, 14 Oct 2026 12:26:00 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;Following is a question by the Hon Kingsley Wong and a written reply by the Secretary for Labour and Welfare, Mr Chris Sun, in the Legislative Council today (October 14):<br />
<br />
Question:<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;A study has pointed out that the population of centenarians in Hong Kong increased from 1 890 in 2011 to 11 575 in 2021, representing an increase of over five times. There are views suggesting that with the continued increase in the number of centenarians in Hong Kong, the Government should formulate targeted support measures for them. However, due to inadequate publicity and promotion for the existing Birthday Greetings to Centenarians programme (under which the Administration sends birthday greeting letters and cards every year to Hong Kong residents who are 100 years old) (the programme), most people are even unaware of the programme, thus limiting its effectiveness. In this connection, will the Government inform this Council:<br />
<br />
(1) of the current number of Hong Kong residents aged 100 or above in the 18 districts of Hong Kong, with a breakdown by District Council district;<br />
<br />
(2) of the total number of greeting letters and cards sent by the authorities since the launch of the programme; the respective total numbers of greeting letters and cards sent by the authorities to eligible persons aged (i) 100, (ii) 105 and (iii) 106 or above in each of the past five years;<br />
<br />
(3) of the details of the major publicity and promotion work undertaken by the authorities since the launch of the programme; whether they will step up the relevant work in the future to enhance public awareness of the programme; if so, of the details; if not, the reasons for that;<br />
<br />
(4) as participation in the programme at present relies mainly on nominations of centenarians by members of the public or organisations, whether the authorities will consider making use of the information referred to in part (1) to proactively send greeting letters and cards to all eligible centenarians, introduce the presentation of birthday red packets, and arrange visits in collaboration with District Services and Community Care Teams, so as to show further care for centenarians and gain a deeper understanding of their needs; if so, of the details; if not, the reasons for that; and<br />
<br />
(5) whether the Labour and Welfare Bureau and the Elderly Commission will conduct research into the physical and mental health of very elderly persons and their service needs, with a view to gaining a more precise understanding of their needs and formulating targeted support measures; if so, of the research directions and timetable; if not, the reasons for that?<br />
<br />
Reply:<br />
<br />
President,<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;In consultation with the Census and Statistics Department (C&amp;SD) and the Social Welfare Department (SWD), a consolidated reply to the Member&#39;s question is as follows:<br />
<br />
(1) The population censuses/by-censuses conducted by the C&amp;SD every five years is the major source of data on the number of Hong Kong residents aged 100 and above. The corresponding figures for other years are not available from the C&amp;SD due to data limitations. Furthermore, the C&amp;SD has not compiled a breakdown of residents aged 100 and above by District Council district.<br />
<br />
(2) to (5) Since 2002, the Elderly Commission (EC) annually invites relevant government departments, elderly service providers, and district organisations to nominate centenarians. The Secretary for Labour and Welfare and the EC Chairman respectively send birthday greeting letters and cards to Hong Kong residents aged 100, and annually thereafter on their 105th and subsequent birthdays as a token of respect. We will also make corresponding arrangements upon receipt of nominations and information of the concerned elderly persons from individual citizens or organisations. Over the past five years, the total numbers of greeting letters and cards sent to Hong Kong residents aged 100 and 105 or above are as follows:<br />
&nbsp;
<table border="1" style="width:500px">
	<tbody>
		<tr>
			<td colspan="6">Number of greeting letters and cards sent to elderly persons aged 100 and 105 or above</td>
		</tr>
		<tr>
			<td>&nbsp;</td>
			<td>2021</td>
			<td>2022</td>
			<td>2023</td>
			<td>2024</td>
			<td>2025</td>
		</tr>
		<tr>
			<td>Aged 100</td>
			<td>870</td>
			<td>899</td>
			<td>940</td>
			<td>1 004</td>
			<td>1 583</td>
		</tr>
		<tr>
			<td>Aged 105 or above</td>
			<td>242</td>
			<td>433</td>
			<td>639</td>
			<td>462</td>
			<td>192</td>
		</tr>
		<tr>
			<td>Total</td>
			<td>1 112</td>
			<td>1 332</td>
			<td>1 579</td>
			<td>1 466</td>
			<td>1 775</td>
		</tr>
	</tbody>
</table>
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;To strengthen support for elderly persons and carers, the SWD has extended the District Services and Community Care Teams - Scheme on Supporting Elderly and Carers to all 18 districts across the city in April 2025. Through visits and daily contacts, the Care Teams proactively identify elderly persons (including those aged 100 or above) in need and their carers to extend care, provide appropriate support to them, and refer persons in need to relevant social welfare service units for follow-up.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;The Labour and Welfare Bureau (LWB) will review the overall arrangements for birthday greeting letters and cards in due course, and will continue to enhance publicity through suitable channels, including various service units under the SWD as well as the Elder Academies (EA) Clusters in five districts under the EA Scheme (<a href="https://www.elderacademy.org.hk/" target="_blank">www.elderacademy.org.hk</a>) jointly launched by the LWB and the EC.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;The Chief Executive announced in the 2025 Policy Address the establishment of the Working Group on Ageing Society Strategies (Working Group), with the Deputy Chief Secretary for Administration leading all policy bureaux to formulate strategies to tackle challenges of an ageing society. The Working Group completed and announced the Report on Ageing Society Strategies on September 18 this year, putting forward 73 measures across 11 areas, including enhancing the collection of information on the elderly population by the C&amp;SD to provide a solid data foundation for policy formulation. Furthermore, the C&amp;SD has been collecting data on the health, elderly care, and social inclusion of elderly persons through thematic household surveys conducted in recent years. The LWB and the EC will continue to closely monitor the demographic changes and service demands of the elderly population. There is currently no plan to carry out specific research projects targeting the oldest-old.]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[LCQ15: Impact of oil price fluctuations on public ferry services]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/14/P2026101400237.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/14/P2026101400237.htm</link><pubDate>Wed, 14 Oct 2026 12:20:00 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Following is a question by the Hon Chan Hok-fung and a written reply by the Secretary for Transport and Logistics, Ms Mable Chan, in the Legislative Council today (October 14):<br />
<br />
Question:<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Over the past three years, international oil prices have risen sharply, resulting in a surge in fuel costs for Hong Kong&#39;s licensed ferry operators (operators). Given that outlying island ferries are the only means of external public transport for the majority of outlying island residents, there are concerns regarding the impact of oil price fluctuations on public ferry services. In this connection, will the Government inform this Council:<br />
<br />
(1) of the changes in the average price of commercial diesel used by ferries in Hong Kong over the past three years;<br />
<br />
(2) whether the authorities know the respective overall total fuel consumption and rates of increase in fuel expenditure for each public ferry operator (for both inner harbour and outlying island routes) over the past three years; and whether they have assessed the impact of soaring oil prices on the revenue and profit position of the various operators;<br />
<br />
(3) of the details of the various subsidy schemes introduced by the authorities for operators over the past three years (including short-term diesel subsidies), as well as the amount of subsidy per litre of diesel available to each operator, the subsidy period and the coverage of the subsidy; whether the authorities have assessed if the subsidy amounts can offset the additional costs arising from the increase in oil prices;<br />
<br />
(4) of the number of fare adjustments, the average rate of increase and the highest rate of increase for each public ferry route over the past three years; the details of the number of ferry trips reduced, routes suspended or reduced, and adjustments to service hours during the same period as a result of cost pressures (provide the above data in tabular form);<br />
<br />
(5) whether the authorities have assessed the actual impact of the above measures of fare adjustment and ferry trip reduction on the commuting of outlying island residents, students travelling to school, elderly people seeking cross-district medical treatment, local tourism and commercial activities; and<br />
<br />
(6) whether the authorities will introduce short to medium-term measures to support ferry services; if so, of the timetable and details?<br />
<br />
Reply:<br />
<br />
President,<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The Government has been paying close attention to the impact of the rising international oil prices on Hong Kong&#39;s transport and freight industries. In the light of the sharp rise in international oil prices since March this year, the Transport and Logistics Bureau and Transport Department (TD) have been maintaining close liaison with various public transport operators (including ferry operators) to understand their operating situation. Having consulted the Environment and Ecology Bureau, the reply to Member&#39;s question is as follows:<br />
<br />
(1) and (2) Generally speaking, local retail fuel prices are determined by oil companies having regard to market principles, and depend on a host of factors, such as the procurement costs, transportation and storage costs, operating costs, land premium and tax. Most ferry operators generally procure diesel from oil companies/diesel distributors through contracts, and do not follow the prices displayed at petrol filling stations. At present, international crude oil prices remain volatile. The price of commercial diesel used by ferries surged starting from March this year. Despite a subsequent decline in the middle of the year, it has climbed back to a high level in recent months.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;According to the information provided by ferry operators, during the period from 2024 to August 2026, the fuel consumption of the various operators remained largely stable. The average monthly fuel consumption and fuel costs per litre were as follows:<br />
&nbsp;
<table border="1" style="width:482px">
	<tbody>
		<tr>
			<td style="width:151px">Year</td>
			<td style="width:104px">2024</td>
			<td style="width:104px">2025</td>
			<td style="width:123px">2026<br />
			(as at August)</td>
		</tr>
		<tr>
			<td style="width:151px">Average monthly fuel consumption<br />
			(million litre)</td>
			<td style="width:104px">3.2</td>
			<td style="width:104px">3.2</td>
			<td style="width:123px">3.3</td>
		</tr>
		<tr>
			<td style="width:151px">Average fuel cost per litre</td>
			<td style="width:104px">$6.4</td>
			<td style="width:104px">$5.2</td>
			<td style="width:123px">$7.6</td>
		</tr>
	</tbody>
</table>
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Fuel expenditure is one of the major cost items of ferry operations. In particular for outlying island ferry routes with longer journeys, fuel expenses generally account for about 30 per cent to 40 per cent of their total operating expenses. The sharp rise in fuel prices has increased the overall operating costs of ferry services, placing operational pressure on operators.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The TD will continue to maintain close communication with various operators and monitor their operational and financial situations, with particular attention to the potential pressure that oil prices may have on the operators&#39; financial situation.<br />
<br />
(3) In view of the outbreak of the conflict in the Middle East region, the Government implemented a two-month Diesel Subsidy Scheme (the Scheme) on April 30, 2026, under which various public ferry operators were eligible for a subsidy of $3 per litre of diesel. Based on their actual fuel consumption, the subsidy amounts received by individual ferry operators under the Scheme ranged from approximately tens of thousands to several million dollars, alleviating some of the operational pressure arising from fluctuations in diesel prices.<br />
<br />
(4) Details of the fare adjustments made by various ferry operators and their service adjustments implemented due to cost considerations during the period from 2024 to August 2026 are set out in Annex. Generally speaking, operators will review service levels having regard to factors such as the actual operational needs of individual routes, passenger demand, and cost savings, with a view to making optimal use of ferry resources.<br />
<br />
(5) The Government attaches great importance on the role of ferry services in meeting the day-to-day commuting needs of outlying island residents and supporting local economic activities. Overall speaking, when considering operators&#39; applications for fare adjustments, the TD will carefully consider various factors in accordance with the established mechanism, including public acceptability, as well as the financial position, service performance, operating data and long-term financial sustainability. of the operators. When considering applications for service adjustments, the TD will examine whether the proposed service adjustments align with changes in the transport demands in the district and holistically consider the operator&#39;s operating conditions, including whether the proposed adjustments would help the operator make better use of resources and enhance overall operational efficiency while safeguarding its financial sustainability, with a view to ensuring that the service remains stable.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The aforementioned fare and service adjustments were made following careful consideration by the TD, with a view to striking a balance between the financial sustainability of ferry services and the travel needs of the public, thereby minimising the impact on residents as far as practicable. Taking the fare adjustments implemented on the six major outlying island ferry routes in April 2026 as an example, after assessing the applications, the TD substantially reduced the fare increase rate from the 15 to 30 per cent as applied by ferry operators to 8 per cent for multi-journey tickets and 12.5 per cent for single-journey tickets, and required the operators to introduce more diverse multi-journey ticket arrangements to alleviate the fare burden on the outlying island residents. Based on the TD&#39;s observations, the fare and service adjustments concerned could still address the travel needs of the residents in general.<br />
<br />
(6) The Government has all along encouraged ferry operators to maintain their long-term financial stability by a multipronged approach of concurrently enhancing farebox and non-farebox revenue. Meanwhile, the Government continues to alleviate the operating costs of ferry operators through different ongoing measures, such as providing Special Helping Measures to outlying island ferry routes to reimburse operators with certain operating expenses on an accountable basis, as well as reimbursing pier rentals and exempting vessel licence fees for eligible operators including those operating in-harbour routes. The Government also purchases new vessels for outlying island ferry routes under the Vessel Subsidy Scheme to enhance the quality of the fleet, operational efficiency and passenger experience. The TD established an inter-departmental task force in June 2026 to expedite the approval of ferry operators&#39; applications related to commercial activities and proposals to improve pier facilities, thereby facilitating the operators&#39; generation of non-farebox revenue, with a view to enhancing their financial sustainability. The TD will also maintain close communication with ferry operators to explore different measures that help enhance operational efficiency, facilitate operation, as well as increase revenue and reduce costs, so as to ensure the sustainable and stable operation of ferry services.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Furthermore, the Inter-departmental Task Force on Monitoring Fuel Supply led by the Financial Secretary will continue to dynamically assess the situation in the Middle East region, and fuel supply and prices, so as to ensure the stability of Hong Kong&#39;s energy supply. Given the uncertainty of the future trend of oil prices, the Government will continue to closely monitor the development to ensure that the operation of public transport services (including ferry services) remains normal.]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[LCQ11: Enhancing waterborne transport services and ancillary facilities]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/14/P2026101400198.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/14/P2026101400198.htm</link><pubDate>Wed, 14 Oct 2026 12:10:11 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Following is a question by the Hon Vincent Cheng and a written reply by the Secretary for Transport and Logistics, Ms Mable Chan, in the Legislative Council today (October 14):<br />
<br />
Question:<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;There are views that the authorities should further enhance waterborne transport services in Hong Kong and improve the ancillary facilities, so as to facilitate travel by members of the public and enhance visitors&#39; experience of visiting Victoria Harbour, thereby injecting vitality into the harbourfront and promoting the development of the tourism industry. In this connection, will the Government inform this Council:<br />
<br />
(1) of the total patronage, frequency and average occupancy rate of the various ferry routes and water taxi routes in Victoria Harbour in the past three years, and the ratio of residents to visitors taking such routes;<br />
<br />
(2) whether the authorities will review the existing ferry routes or water taxi services, and explore the feasibility of extending the routes to more locations with development potential (e.g. the Kai Tak Sports Park and the Cheung Sha Wan Promenade), or introducing special routes to tie in with large-scale events held at the harbourfront; if so, of the details; if not, the reasons for that;<br />
<br />
(3) as it is reported that water taxis suffer from low public awareness, whether the authorities have studied with the operator ways to step up publicity work, such as drawing reference from overseas examples to set up service stations with clearer signage and greater recognition near the shores, or reviewed the existing mode of operation;<br />
<br />
(4) whether the authorities will launch improvement programmes targeting the ferry piers of in-harbour routes in Victoria Harbour, as well as some public piers in Kowloon and on Hong Kong Island with higher utilisation rates (e.g. the landing facilities in the vicinity of Tsim Sha Tsui East), including enhancing barrier-free facilities, installing electronic display panels and carrying out beautification works; and<br />
<br />
(5) given that under the Pilot Scheme for Electric Ferries implemented by the Government, some electric ferries have progressively commenced sea trials, of the current effectiveness of the trials of the various routes, and the respective timetables for formally putting the various electric ferries into service?<br />
<br />
Reply:<br />
<br />
President,<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;In-harbour ferry services (including water taxi) play a supplementary role in Hong Kong&#39;s public transport system. Apart from serving the commuting needs of local residents, they also attract visitors exploring Victoria Harbour. The Transport Department (TD) has been closely monitoring the operations of ferry services, and keeping in close communications with ferry operators for service adjustments in view of changes in passenger demand.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Having consulted the Environment and Ecology Bureau, the reply to the Member&#39;s question is as follows:<br />
<br />
(1) The total patronage, headway and average occupancy rate of franchised and licensed in-harbour ferry routes (including water taxi in the past three years (i.e. from 2023 to 2025)) are set out in the Annex. Ferry operators do not keep data on the ratio of residents to visitors among its passengers. According to the observations of the TD and ferry operators, licensed ferry services mainly serve local passengers, while tourists account for about 60 per cent of the passengers of the Star Ferry.&nbsp;<br />
<br />
(2) The TD has welcomed and encouraged ferry operators to proactively consider introducing suitable new routes to attract more members of the public and tourists on board. The TD will take into account factors such as passenger demand, availability of pier or landing steps facilities, financial viability, and existing public transport services according to the established procedures, and discuss with the operators and advise on service details of the proposed routes.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;For instance, following the commissioning of WestK Quay, the water taxi operator, with the support of the TD, launched the new West Kowloon &ndash; Central route on November 10, 2025, providing pet co-ride service. The route has been well received since its launch.<br />
<br />
(3) The water taxi is mainly of a sightseeing and tourism nature, providing services for the public and tourists travelling across the Victoria Harbour since its commissioning in July 2021. The TD has been actively assisting the water taxi operator in co-operating with the West Kowloon Cultural District Authority, the Hong Kong Tourism Board (HKTB) and other government departments to promote water taxi services, including promoting the water taxi at the HKTB&#39;s Visitor Centres and website, enhancing signage and providing water taxi service information in tourist areas such as Wan Chai and Tsim Sha Tsui (including the West Kowloon Cultural District). In addition, regarding facilities, the water taxi operator has set up a ticketing counter at the Avenue of Stars in Tsim Sha Tsui East to promote water taxi services and facilitate the handling of enquiries from tourists and ticket purchases. The water taxi operator has also set up a passenger waiting area and enhanced publicity at Central Pier No. 9 since February 2026 to attract passengers.<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The TD will work with the water taxi operator to closely monitor its operation, and enhance water taxi services in response to passenger demand.<br />
<br />
(4) For ferry piers (such as the Hung Hom (South) Ferry Pier) used by licensed or franchised ferry operators and other public landing facilities (such as the Central and Wan Chai public piers and public landing steps), the TD would co-ordinate with relevant departments (including the Architectural Services Department, the Civil Engineering and Development Department, and the Electrical and Mechanical Services Department) to continuously inspect the pier structures and facilities, and arrange repair and maintenance works as necessary to ensure their safety and normal operations.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;At the same time, the Government has been committed to improving the waiting environment and facilities at ferry piers. In particular, lifting platform installation works were carried out at the Hung Hom (South) Ferry Pier in 2023 to facilitate access by wheelchair passengers. In addition, accessible toilets, emergency call bells, and wide gates/turnstiles have been added at most in-harbour ferry piers; electronic display panels are also installed at some ferry piers to provide passengers with sailing information.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The Government will continue to review the utilisation of public landing facilities from time to time and listen to views of stakeholders, with a view to enhancing the design and operational arrangements of landing facilities.<br />
<br />
(5) To promote the trial of electric ferries in Hong Kong, the Government has earmarked $350 million to implement the Pilot Scheme for Electric Ferries (Pilot Scheme) to introduce four electric ferries (e-ferries). Among them, three e-ferries have commenced trials successively since December 2024 (for the North Point &ndash; Hung Hom / Kowloon City routes), May 2025 (for the water taxi route), and June 2026 (for the Sai Wan Ho &ndash; Kwun Tong route), and have been progressively deployed to provide passenger services. Their battery range has been sufficient to meet the electricity demands for the daily total sailing distances of their respective ferry operators, and the overall operations have generally been smooth. Tender invitation for the remaining e-ferry is underway, with its trial targeted to commence in 2029.&nbsp;<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The Government has established an inter-departmental working group to oversee and evaluate the operational and environmental performance and effectiveness of the e-ferries. The effectiveness of the e-ferries and their associated facilities will be evaluated and concluded upon completion of all trials. Should the Pilot Scheme yield positive results, the Government intends to encourage ferry operators to continue operating the relevant e-ferries at their own cost after completion of the Pilot Scheme, in order to help reduce emissions.]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[Employer sanctioned for violating requirements under Enhanced Supplementary Labour Scheme]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/14/P2026101400301.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/14/P2026101400301.htm</link><pubDate>Wed, 14 Oct 2026 12:00:00 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The Labour Department (LD) today (October 14) announced the imposition of an administrative sanction on Wilson Logistics Limited. With immediate effect, the LD has terminated the processing of an application for importing labour previously submitted by the company under the Enhanced Supplementary Labour Scheme (ESLS). In parallel, the LD will, from today, refuse to process any applications submitted by the company for a period of one year.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;A spokesman for the LD said, &quot;After an investigation, it was established that the above company had breached the following requirements under the ESLS. An administrative sanction is thus imposed for the following reasons:&nbsp;<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;(i) providing the LD with false information on the number of full-time local employees; and<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;(ii) failing to fulfil the 2:1 manning ratio of full-time local employees to imported workers continuously after importing labour.&quot;<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The spokesman stressed, &quot;Employers applying for the importation of workers under the ESLS should strictly comply with the scheme requirements, including according priority to employing suitable local workers. The ESLS stipulates that if an employer violates labour or immigration laws, the requirements of the ESLS, or the Standard Employment Contract, the LD will impose an administrative sanction on the employer. Such sanctions may include terminating the processing of submitted applications of the employer, withdrawing approvals for the importation of workers previously granted, and refusing to process any subsequent applications submitted by the employer.&quot;]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[LCQ22: MTR Fare Savers]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/14/P2026101400227.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/14/P2026101400227.htm</link><pubDate>Wed, 14 Oct 2026 12:00:00 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;​Following is a question by the Hon Chu Lap-wai and a written reply by the Secretary for Transport and Logistics, Ms Mable Chan, in the Legislative Council today (October 14):<br />
<br />
Question:<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The MTR Corporation Limited (MTRCL) set up the MTR Fare Savers in 2002 to provide adult Octopus card holders with a fare discount of $2 per trip. However, the MTRCL has indicated that with market changes over the years, the ways it provides fare promotions and concessions have evolved as well, and the&nbsp;MTRCL is reviewing the sustainability of such scheme. It has been reported that the&nbsp;MTRCL&#39;s remarks have aroused concerns among the grass roots about an increase in transport expenditure. In this connection, will the Government inform this Council:<br />
<br />
(1) whether it knows the respective numbers and locations of new MTR Fare Savers set up by the&nbsp;MTRCL in each of the past two years, and provide a breakdown of the monthly patronage, operating expenditure, and total amount of fare revenue foregone for each MTR Fare Saver across Hong Kong;<br />
<br />
(2) whether the Government has regularly assessed and reviewed the utilisation rates of various MTR Fare Savers and their impact on the passenger flow of the surrounding public transport modes; if so, of the details; if not, the reasons for that;<br />
<br />
(3) whether it knows the purpose of setting up the Futian MTR Fare Saver, and of its patronage and total amount of fare revenue foregone in the past two years;<br />
<br />
(4) whether it knows if the&nbsp;MTRCL has regularly collected views from and held discussions with various District Councils about setting up MTR Fare Savers at suitable locations in the districts concerned; if so, of the number of new MTR Fare Savers proposed to be set up in each of the past two years;<br />
<br />
(5) given that MTR Fare Savers are not set up in quite a number of districts in Hong Kong at present, rendering local residents unable to be benefited from the scheme, whether the Government has urged the&nbsp;MTRCL to regularly review the locations of MTR Fare Savers and formulate a timetable for conducting such reviews; if so, of the details; if not, the reasons for that;<br />
<br />
(6) since I have learnt that many members of the public consider that MTR Fare Savers are important platform for saving transport expenditure, and given that the Government is the majority shareholder of the&nbsp;MTRCL, whether the Government will ensure that the&nbsp;MTRCL consults the Legislative Council and the public before making any decision to reduce the number of or cancel MTR Fare Savers, and also ensure the introduction of alternative options offering concessions which are no less favourable than those currently provided;<br />
<br />
(7) as the&nbsp;MTRCL has indicated earlier that it is actively exploring new promotion offers and platforms, with the growing popularity of the MTR mobile application, whether the Government knows if the&nbsp;MTRCL will fully transition from collecting concessions by tapping physical card to collection via the MTR mobile application in the future; if so, how the Government will ensure that the&nbsp;MTRCL takes into account the ability of the grass roots to apply technology, so as to alleviate the problem of the digital divide; and<br />
<br />
(8) whether the Government and the&nbsp;MTRCL will consider utilising the MTR mobile application to further identify underprivileged groups such as low-income persons and carers, so as to allocate promotional resources more accurately and fulfil corporate social responsibility?<br />
<br />
Reply:<br />
<br />
President,&nbsp;<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;MTR Corporation Limited (MTRCL) has been providing various fare promotions and concessions for its passengers, such as the regular fare concessions offered to the elderly, children, eligible students, and persons with disabilities. In 2025, this concession benefited over 600 million passenger trips, reflecting a financial commitment exceeding $3.2 billion, reaching historical high.&nbsp;<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;In response to Member&#39;s questions, having consulted the&nbsp;MTRCL, the reply is as follows:<br />
<br />
(1) and (2) The MTRCL established the MTR Fare Saver scheme in 2002 with a view to attracting passengers who are situated further away from MTR stations to walk to a nearby MTR station and travel by MTR by offering fare discounts. Passengers could enjoy a $2 fare discount by tapping their adult Octopus over the reader on the MTR Fare Saver and boarding a train at the corresponding designated MTR station. Currently, the&nbsp;MTRCL has set up MTR Fare Savers at 41 locations in Hong Kong, with four, 18 and 19 readers located respectively on/in Hong Kong Island, Kowloon and New Territories. The list of locations of all MTR Fare Savers is at Annex. Amongst them, the most utilised MTR Fare Saver is located at Central-Mid-Levels Escalators and Walkway System, which encourages passengers to walk to Central, Hong Kong or Sheung Wan Station to ride the MTR. MTR Fare Saver is part of the MTRCL&#39;s commercial promotion schemes. The fare discounts and concessions amount are fully borne by the MTRCL, and information regarding its utilisation, operating expenses and concession amount is internal commercial information of the MTRCL. It is understood that the&nbsp;MTRCL currently has no plan to adjust the arrangement of the existing MTR Fare Saver. The MTRCL will continue to review and update various promotion measures from time to time, and maintain communication with stakeholders.&nbsp;<br />
<br />
(3) In order to attract passengers from Shenzhen to Hong Kong through Lok Ma Chau Spur Line Control Point to travel by MTR at Lok Ma Chau Station, the&nbsp;MTRCL has set up a MTR Fare Saver at Futian Checkpoint Station on the Shenzhen Metro Line 4, which it operates. Passengers having tapped their adult Octopus or JoyYou Card (for passengers aged 60 to 64) at the reader will enjoy a $3 fare discount for their journey.&nbsp;<br />
<br />
(4) to (6) The MTRCL always maintains close communication with various stakeholders (including the Legislative Council, District Councils, and local communities) regarding MTR services, and will upgrade its facilities continuously taking into consideration various factors such as district development and residents&#39; needs. For instance, following the commissioning of the full Tuen Ma Line and the opening of Sung Wong Toi and To Kwa Wan stations, the&nbsp;MTRCL set up MTR Fare Savers at Jubilant Plaza in To Kwa Wan and To Kwa Wan Sports Centre respectively in 2022 and 2023, providing concessions to local residents and passengers.&nbsp;<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;In light of market conditions, the Government encourages public transport operators to endeavour to provide fare concessions to passengers, having regard to the operating environment as well as social and economic conditions. The MTRCL has launched multiple fare concessions while taking into account its operating situation and the need to maintain its long-term financial sustainability. Apart from regular fare concessions provided to the elderly, children, eligible students and persons with disabilities, the&nbsp;MTRCL also provides a series of fare promotions benefitting those commuting to work, such as City Saver, various Monthly Passes, Early Bird Discount and interchange discount for green minibus routes.&nbsp;<br />
<br />
(7) and (8) Along with the continuous expansion of the MTR network and the changes in passengers travelling patterns and behaviours over the years, the MTRCL reviews its concessions from time to time and actively explores different models and platforms to deploy promotional resources in a more precise manner. Currently, passengers can earn MTR points by riding MTR with registered Octopus cards linked to their MTR Mobile accounts, and redeem rewards such as free MTR rides. The MTR Mobile application also features various types of mini-games, &quot;MTR Instant Bonus&quot; quiz game and surveys for users to earn MTR points. The MTRCL also continuously enhanced its promotion efforts via different channels, including educational demonstrations and videos on its official MTR website, mobile application and social media platforms to introduce how to link Octopus cards to the MTR Mobile application and redeem ride concessions, thereby supporting grassroots citizens and the elderly.&nbsp;<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;In addition, the Government has implemented the Public Transport Fare Subsidy Scheme (the Scheme) since 2019 to alleviate the financial burden on commuters with relatively high public transport expenses. There are no age or income restrictions under the Scheme. Currently, the Government subsidises one-third of the actual monthly public transport expenses in excess of the monthly threshold (i.e. $500) incurred by commuters, subject to a maximum of $400 per month for each Octopus and each AlipayHK account.]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[Hong Kong Academy of Finance's 2027 intake of Financial Leaders Programme is open for application]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/14/P2026101300387.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/14/P2026101300387.htm</link><pubDate>Wed, 14 Oct 2026 12:00:00 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[The following is issued on behalf of the Hong Kong Monetary Authority:<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The Hong Kong Academy of Finance (AoF) announces that the 2027 intake of the Financial Leaders Programme (FLP) will open for application tomorrow (October 15). Since its launch in 2022, with the support of the financial regulators in Hong Kong&nbsp;(Note) and the participating institutions, the programme has cultivated a vibrant community of more than 100 future financial leaders for Hong Kong.&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The FLP offers unique opportunities for participants to engage in direct dialogues with global and local distinguished leaders across diverse sectors and further expand cross-sector professional networks, strengthening their strategic mindset needed to navigate the continual shifts in macroeconomic and geopolitical dynamics.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;The Chief Executive of the Hong Kong Monetary Authority and the Chairman of the AoF, Mr Eddie Yue,&nbsp;said, &quot;In today&#39;s dynamic environment, our future financial leaders must possess the long-term vision, innovative mindset and robust expertise to uphold Hong Kong&#39;s status as a premier international financial centre. I strongly encourage senior financial executives to seize this opportunity to join or nominate promising candidates.&quot;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Tailored for high-potential senior financial executives from diverse backgrounds who are approximately one to two levels below the chief executive officers position, this programme will be offered on a part-time basis. Applications of the 2027 intake will be accepted on or before December 4. Further information about the FLP, including&nbsp;<a href="https://www.aof.org.hk/flp/feedback-from-participants/alumni" target="_blank">feedback from past participants</a>&nbsp;and the application form, is available on the&nbsp;<a href="https://www.aof.org.hk/flp" target="_blank">AoF website</a>.<br />
<br />
Note: Namely the Hong Kong Monetary Authority, the Insurance Authority, the Mandatory Provident Fund Schemes Authority and the Securities and Futures Commission.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[LCQ9: Licensing and regulatory regime for massage establishments]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/14/P2026101400297.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/14/P2026101400297.htm</link><pubDate>Wed, 14 Oct 2026 11:45:00 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Following is a question by the Hon Leung Man-kwong and a written reply by the Secretary for Security, Mr Tang Ping-keung, in the Legislative Council today (October 14):<br />
&nbsp;<br />
Question:<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; It is reported that massage services advertised with terms such as &quot;foot fetish&quot; and &quot;face-stepping&quot; have proliferated in the community in recent months. There are views that the promotional content and business models of such services cause nuisance to the nearby communities and have aroused public concerns about the possible involvement of such premises in illegal sex trade. However, as these premises only provide localised massage services on body parts such as feet and face, they are currently not regulated under the licensing regime of the Massage Establishments Ordinance. In this connection, will the Government inform this Council:<br />
&nbsp;<br />
(1) of the respective number of inspections of and law enforcement actions against massage establishments conducted by the Government in each of the past three years; among such cases, the number of those involving premises providing localised massage services on body parts such as feet and face; and the number of prosecutions brought against and convictions of the owners or operators of such premises for alleged offences under the Crimes Ordinance;<br />
&nbsp;<br />
(2) whether the Government will consider amending the Massage Establishments Ordinance, including changing the licensing threshold for massage establishments from being defined by &quot;body parts&quot; to taking into account the nature and context of services, so as to comprehensively regulate all massage premises suspected of providing pornographic services; and<br />
&nbsp;<br />
(3) as it is reported that the aforesaid premises carry out promotional and solicitation activities through online social media platforms and discussion forums using terms such as &quot;unusual fetishes&quot; and &quot;sexual innuendo&quot;, whether the Government will strengthen interdepartmental intelligence gathering and law enforcement in the future to combat such promotional activities; if so, of the details; if not, the reasons for that?<br />
<br />
Reply:<br />
&nbsp;<br />
President,<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The Massage Establishments Ordinance (Cap. 266) aims to regulate massage establishments through a licensing regime in order to prevent and combat vice or illegal prostitution activities committed by criminals in these establishments. The Ordinance was amended in 2001 to narrow the regulatory scope so that only massage premises offering full-body massage (i.e. covering that part of the body below the shoulders and above the knees) to customers of the opposite sex are required to obtain a licence, with a view to striking a proper balance between controlling vice activities and not hindering legitimate massage businesses.<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Under the Ordinance, any person who operates, keeps, manages, assists in any capacity in the operation of, or assists in the management of, a massage establishment for the operation of which a licence is not in force commits an offence and is liable to a fine of $50,000 and imprisonment for six months on first conviction, and a fine of $100,000 and imprisonment for two years on subsequent conviction.<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;My reply to the questions raised by the Member is as follows:<br />
<br />
(1)&nbsp;The Hong Kong Police Force (HKPF) conducts regular surprise inspections of licensed massage establishments under section 11(1) of the Ordinance to combat vice activities or premises in breach of relevant licensing conditions. If a licensee breaches any licensing condition, or ceases to satisfy any condition relating to the operation of a massage establishment as prescribed in the Ordinance, the HKPF may, depending on the circumstances, issue a warning or consider revoking, suspending or refusing to renew the licence.&nbsp;<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The HKPF will take resolute law enforcement actions against any premises involved in illegal activities, including those suspected of operating unlicensed massage establishments. For instance, in July this year, the Sham Shui Po District of the HKPF, acting on intelligence, conducted a surprise inspection and cracked down an unlicensed massage establishment in Cheung Sha Wan which advertised so-called &quot;barefoot facial&quot; massage. Two persons were arrested on suspicion of &quot;operating an unlicensed massage establishment&quot; and &quot;assisting in the management of an unlicensed massage establishment&quot;. The case is currently under investigation.<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The relevant statistics of the HKPF&#39;s efforts against unlicensed massage premises over the past three years are tabulated below:<br />
&nbsp;
<table border="1" style="width:548px">
	<tbody>
		<tr>
			<td style="height:8px; width:95px">Year</td>
			<td style="height:8px; width:142px">Number of persons arrested</td>
			<td style="height:8px; width:161px">Number of persons prosecuted</td>
			<td style="height:8px; width:151px">Number of persons convicted</td>
		</tr>
		<tr>
			<td style="height:8px; width:95px">2024</td>
			<td style="height:8px; width:142px">114</td>
			<td style="height:8px; width:161px">13</td>
			<td style="height:8px; width:151px">13</td>
		</tr>
		<tr>
			<td style="height:8px; width:95px">2025</td>
			<td style="height:8px; width:142px">65</td>
			<td style="height:8px; width:161px">11</td>
			<td style="height:8px; width:151px">11</td>
		</tr>
		<tr>
			<td style="height:8px; width:95px">2026<br />
			(as at end-August)</td>
			<td style="height:8px; width:142px">17</td>
			<td style="height:8px; width:161px">2</td>
			<td style="height:8px; width:151px">1 (another prosecution case is under trial)</td>
		</tr>
	</tbody>
</table>
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The above figures include arrests made in respect of offences including &quot;prohibition on operating etc. massage establishment without a licence&quot; under section 4 of the Ordinance, &quot;keeping a vice establishment&quot; under section 139 of the Crimes Ordinance (Cap. 200), &quot;breach of condition of stay&quot; and &quot;offence to be employer of a person who is not lawfully employable&quot; under section 41 and section 17I respectively of the Immigration Ordinance (Cap. 115). The HKPF does not maintain information on the types of services provided by the premises involved.<br />
<br />
(2)&nbsp;The HKPF has all along been committed to combating all forms of illegal prostitution activities. In respect of premises suspected of providing pornographic services, regardless of whether they hold a licence issued under the Ordinance, section 139 of the Crimes Ordinance (Cap. 200) (&quot;keeping a vice establishment&quot;) provides that any person who keeps any premises, vessel or place as a vice establishment; or manages or assists in the management, or is otherwise in charge or control, of any premises, vessel or place kept as a vice establishment, shall be guilty of an offence and shall be liable on conviction to a maximum penalty of imprisonment for ten years.&nbsp;<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;As mentioned above, the Government amended the Ordinance in 2001 to narrow the regulatory scope, including specifically stipulating that only massage establishments offering full-body massage to customers of the opposite sex are required to obtain a licence, and defining &quot;full-body massage&quot; as covering that part of the body below the shoulders and above the knees. The Government currently has no plan to amend the licensing threshold under the Ordinance. The HKPF will continue to closely monitor the modes of operation of massage premises using different descriptions to attract customers, and take resolute law enforcement actions against any premises involved in illegal activities, including those providing pornographic services.<br />
<br />
(3)&nbsp;On intelligence gathering and law enforcement against illegal activities involving prostitution, the HKPF, apart from patrolling black spots, conducts intelligence-led operations, including deploying officers to disguise as customers to collect evidence and strengthening inspection of suspicious premises. The HKPF also adopts an interdepartmental collaboration strategy by conducting joint enforcement actions with relevant departments (including the Labour Department and the Immigration Department) to combat the offences concerned. At the same time, the HKPF will continue to closely monitor the trend of using websites and online social media platforms to conduct illegal prostitution activities, and gather intelligence via online channels to facilitate the deployment of targeted enforcement actions.&nbsp;<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The Government will continue to monitor market developments, including the modes of operation of massage establishments, and review the effectiveness of the implementation of the Ordinance on an ongoing basis. The Government will remain committed to preventing and combating the use of massage premises by criminals for vice and illegal prostitution activities, while not hindering the operation of legitimate massage businesses.]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[Keynote speech by PSITI at LSCM Logistics Summit 2026 (English only) (with photo)]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/14/P2026101400272.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/14/P2026101400272.htm</link><pubDate>Wed, 14 Oct 2026 11:45:00 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;Following is the keynote speech by the Permanent Secretary for Innovation, Technology and Industry, Mr Kevin Choi, at the LSCM Logistics Summit 2026 today (October 14):<br />
<br />
Deputy Chief Secretary for Administration, Mr Cheuk Wing-hing, Alan (Chairman of the Board of Directors of the Logistics and Supply Chain MultiTech R&amp;D Centre (LSCM), Professor Alan Lam), Sunny (Chairman of the Board of Directors of the San Tin Technopole Company Limited, Dr Sunny Chai), Mr Mei (Group Senior Vice President and President of the Overseas Enterprise Business of ZTE Corporation, Mr Mei Zhonghua), Mr Yang (Director and Executive Vice President of China Mobile Hong Kong Co Ltd, Mr Yang Qian), distinguished guests, industry leaders, ladies and gentlemen,<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;Good morning. I am most honoured to join you at the LSCM Summit today to celebrate the centre&#39;s 20th anniversary, and its contribution to Hong Kong&#39;s technological evolution.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;LSCM was initially established to support the development of Hong Kong&#39;s logistics sector. Nowadays, logistics is not just about moving goods through sea, air, and land routes. It is the management of complex, interconnected systems in a volatile global environment shaped by shifting geopolitics and accelerating cross-border e-commerce. In addition to our world-class freight infrastructure and systems, efficiency must be paired with agility through technology deployment: supply chains must be resilient to disruption, end-to-end visible, environmentally sustainable, and powered by intelligent, real-time decision-making systems that enable instant operational pivots. Achieving this level of transformation requires the adoption of innovation and technology (I&amp;T).<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;Last month, the Chief Executive announced both our city&#39;s first-ever Five-Year Plan and the 2026 Policy Address, setting out clear targets for a new paradigm for the development of I&amp;T, reaffirming our unwavering commitment to becoming an international I&amp;T hub.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;For example, the rise of AI is impacting almost every aspect of our lives including modern supply chains and cybersecurity management, and computing power is a core engine for the development of AI. To ensure Hong Kong has this vital fuel for AI development, the construction works of the Sandy Ridge Data Facility Cluster have begun. By 2032, the computing power of the cluster is expected to reach a level equivalent to 36 times Hong Kong&#39;s current computing power. This represents our solid and unwavering support to the development of the intelligent computing industry. With a cumulative investment expected to reach at least $23.8 billion, Sandy Ridge will provide the robust infrastructure that allows our enterprises to deploy advanced AI models. In future, companies in the sector can run real time global supply chain simulations under secure and reliable IT (information technology) systems, and process complex predictive analytics in a timely manner.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;As we deploy more cloud platforms, connected sensors, automation systems, and AI-driven decision tools across the logistics chain, cybersecurity management becomes an essential enabler, not an afterthought. The same connectivity that improves visibility and speed also expands the cyber threat surface. From ransomware and data leakage to tampering with operational systems, cyber risks can directly disrupt logistics operations, compromise customer trust, and undermine compliance. That is why secure-by-design architecture, resilient networks, and robust identity and access management are indispensable. We also need to strengthen incident response capabilities, supply-chain risk assessment, and continuous monitoring so that enterprises can detect, contain, and recover quickly, maintaining business continuity even under attacks. I encourage you to join the afternoon session of today&#39;s Summit, where my colleagues of the Digital Policy Office will share more expertise in this regard.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;While digital infrastructure and computing power are crucial to the sector&#39;s development, smart robotics and automation are other essential elements. In a city like Hong Kong where warehouse space is premium, modern warehouses have to shift into a highly automated ecosystem where autonomous mobile robots, automated guided vehicles, and robotic picking arms work side by side with human operators. These robotic technologies are drastically reducing order cycle times, optimising vertical storage space, and eliminating human error in high-velocity sorting environments.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;In this regard, many of you are aware that the Government has established the AIR@InnoHK as a dedicated research cluster focused on AI and robotics technologies. To further our efforts, we have successfully facilitated the United Nations Industrial Development Organization to establish its first centre of excellence on global advanced manufacturing in the Asia-Pacific region in Hong Kong, with a focus on the fields of AI+ manufacturing, robotics, new energy and industrial design.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;Yet, hardware and computing power are only part of the solution. We also need a vibrant ecosystem where technology is researched, tested, and tailored for practical business applications, including cybersecurity technologies and solutions that are fit for logistics operations. Under the &quot;north-central-south&quot; spatial layout, the development of our three major I&amp;T parks, namely the Hong Kong Park of the Hetao Shenzhen Hong Kong Science and Technology Innovation Co-operation Zone and the San Tin Technopole, the Science Park, and Cyberport, will establish a truly global industry academia research collaborative innovation platform. Working together with the industries, our top-class universities and different public research and development (R&amp;D) centres, Hong Kong will become a globally competitive advanced R&amp;D centre and pilot production and transformation base for technology industries, benefitting all of us here today.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;In parallel, we are solving the historic constraint of physical space. The Northern Metropolis provides a vast amount of new land, directly driving Hong Kong&#39;s evolution into an I&amp;T centre under the new &quot;South-North dual engine (finance-IT/I&amp;T)&quot;. Within the Northern Metropolis, there will be industrial space designed for prototyping, pilot and mass production. For the logistics and supply chain sector, this offers a very good opportunity for the physical integration of advanced manufacturing and high-end logistics. Enterprises can actively utilise the New Industrialisation Support Scheme to turn R&amp;D outcomes into reality. This includes funding and support for setting up brand new, state-of-the-art smart production lines right within the Northern Metropolis. When you pair smart production lines with automated, AI-driven facilities in the same geographical area, you eliminate transit friction, slash lead times, and create a highly responsive domestic supply chain network.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;With proximity to the Guangdong-Hong Kong-Macao Greater Bay Area (GBA), the Chinese Mainland&#39;s powerhouse for innovation and economic growth, the Northern Metropolis occupies a vital strategic position in the region. By aligning our local technological frameworks with the industrial mass of the GBA, we are building a seamless, highly integrated ecosystem. Looking ahead, after 2030, we are expecting Hong Kong as an international I&amp;T centre based in the GBA, serving the entire country and deeply connected to the world.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;Finally, please once again accept my warmest congratulations to LSCM on its 20th anniversary. I wish you all a highly productive, successful and inspiring Summit.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;Thank you very much.<div id="photos"><a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/14/P2026101400272_photo_1341001.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/14/P2026101400272_photo_1341001.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;</div>]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[LCQ10: Nuisance behaviour of passengers on public transport]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/14/P2026101400267.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/14/P2026101400267.htm</link><pubDate>Wed, 14 Oct 2026 11:42:30 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Following is a question by the Hon Chong Ho-fung and a written reply by the Secretary for Transport and Logistics, Ms Mable Chan, in the Legislative Council today (October 14):<br />
<br />
Question:<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;It has been reported that in recent years, some uncivic-minded passengers have frequently engaged in discourteous behaviour on public transport. This includes playing videos or music loudly on mobile phones inside the carriage, making phone calls, and even placing their feet on seats, causing hygiene issues and nuisance. Numerous cities in the Mainland, as well as Japan, Singapore and London in the United Kingdom, have effectively curbed malpractices such as noise in passenger compartments and placing feet on seats through legal regulation, clear penalties, technological assistance or long-term publicity campaigns. In this connection, will the Government inform this Council:<br />
<br />
(1) of the respective numbers of complaints received in each of the past three years by the Transport Department, the Environmental Protection Department and public transport operators concerning &quot;noise nuisance&quot; on public transport (such as the use of electronic devices with audio played through loudspeakers) and &quot;improper or unhygienic behaviour&quot; (including placing feet or shoes on seats and other discourteous conduct); whether the authorities have conducted any studies to assess the impact of such behaviour on the quality of public transport services and passengers&#39; travelling experience;<br />
<br />
(2) whether the authorities will make reference to overseas experience and amend the relevant legislation in Hong Kong to expressly designate &quot;playing audio from electronic devices through loudspeakers&quot; and &quot;placing feet or shoes on seats&quot; as acts of contravention, and introduce streamlined enforcement procedures as well as review and increase the existing penalties for causing nuisance on public transport; and<br />
<br />
(3) whether the authorities will study, in collaboration with the MTR Corporation Limited and various bus operators, the introduction of pilot &quot;quiet zones/compartments&quot;, and draw on overseas publicity strategies by installing additional warning signs in train compartments and at platforms, as well as making regular reminder announcements, with a view to promoting a culture of courteous travel?<br />
<br />
Reply:<br />
<br />
President,<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The Government has been closely monitoring the service of public transport and working closely with the public transport operators (operators) to promote the message, through measures such as publicity and education, that passengers should be considerate and maintain mutual respect towards other passengers when using public transport services. In consultation with the Environmental Protection Department (EPD), our reply to the questions raised by the Hon Chong is as follows:<br />
<br />
(1) The number of complaints received annually in the past three years by the Transport Department (TD) and the EPD regarding &quot;noise nuisance&quot; (e.g. excessive volume of electronic devices) and &quot;improper or unhygienic behaviour&quot; (e.g. stepping on seats) on public transport is set out in the Annex. The operators do not maintain statistics on complaints categorised by individual types of behaviour.<br />
<br />
(2) At present, the legislation of various public transport services (e.g. the Mass Transit Railway By-laws (Cap. 556B), the Public Bus Services Regulations (Cap. 230A) and the Road Traffic (Public Service Vehicles) Regulations (Cap. 374D)) contains provisions regulating improper passenger conduct with corresponding penalties. Each franchised bus operator has drawn up Passenger Notices stipulating that passengers are refrained from playing mobile devices loudly or placing their feet on seats. The operators also remind passengers of the regulations through on-board announcements and signage.&nbsp;<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The operators have also put in place guidelines to assist frontline staff members in handling situations relating to passenger conduct in an appropriate manner based on the actual circumstances, such as offering verbal advice and taking follow-up actions, as well as referring to law enforcement authorities when necessary. As for the MTR Corporation Limited (MTRCL), its staff, in the course of their routine inspections, will give reminders and take appropriate follow-up actions if passengers were found to have breached the rules.&nbsp;<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;In addition, the TD has been collaborating with and encouraging operators to promote through publicity and educational activities the importance of passenger safety, consideration for others and respect for other passengers when using public transport services. The MTRCL also organises annual courtesy campaign to remind passengers not to affect or cause nuisance to others.<br />
<br />
(3) At present, there are quiet cars designated on all MTR heavy rail trains equipped with in-train televisions, where televisions are muted so as to provide passengers with a relatively quiet travelling environment. As for High Speed Rail, the Vibrant Express trains operated by the MTRCL have designated Quiet Cars where in-train announcements are broadcast at a lower volume and the in-train televisions are muted. Furthermore, passengers in Quiet Cars of the High Speed Rail are required to set their mobile phones to silent and wear headphones when using electronic devices. Passengers on the phone are required to move to either end of the compartment. When considering the introduction of &quot;quiet zones/compartments&quot;, operators need to prudently consider their mode of operation, operational arrangements such as space and design of compartment as well as the impact on overall carrying capacity in order to strike a proper balance. The Government will continue to keep in view passenger behaviour on public transport and collaborate with operators to promote travelling etiquette through various means, with a view to fostering a comfortable and harmonious travelling environment.]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[Speech by SITI at HKU Innovation Week 2026 (English only) (with photo)]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/14/P2026101400310.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/14/P2026101400310.htm</link><pubDate>Wed, 14 Oct 2026 11:35:00 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Following is the speech by the Secretary for Innovation, Technology and Industry, Professor Sun Dong, at HKU Innovation Week 2026 today (October 14):<br />
<br />
The Honourable Vice-Chairman Leung (Vice-Chairman of the National Committee of the Chinese People&#39;s Political Consultative Conference, Mr CY Leung), Professor Richard Wong (Acting President of University of Hong Kong (HKU)), George (Ambassador George Hara, Group Chairman and Chief Executive Officer of DEFTA Partners), Anderson (Vice-President and Pro-Vice-Chancellor (Research), HKU, Professor Anderson Shum), distinguished guests, ladies and gentlemen,<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;Good morning. It is my great pleasure to join you today at HKU Innovation Week 2026, alongside distinguished scholars, industry leaders, and community partners. These are exactly the key partners in driving academic discovery, scientific advancement, and the pioneering spirit that defines Hong Kong&#39;s innovation landscape.<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;Following the release of Hong Kong&#39;s First Five-Year Plan and the 2026 Policy Address last month, the HKSAR (Hong Kong Special Administrative Region)&#39;s goal is clear: Hong Kong will basically have established an international I&amp;T (innovation and technology) centre based in the Greater Bay Area, serving the entire country and being connected to the world after 2030. This brings me to the theme of this year&rsquo;s event - &quot;The Innovation Reef: Where Ideas Breed and Thrive.&quot; A healthy innovation ecosystem, like a reef, relies on different parties performing different but complementary roles. Our universities play an indispensable role in advancing knowledge, nurturing talents, and transforming research into real-world impact. Talents aside, we need to drive the synergistic development among the Government, industries, academia, research and investment sectors.&nbsp;<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;To this end, the Government has deployed strategic resources through the $10 billion RAISe+ Scheme to accelerate the commercialisation of university research and development. Since its launch in 2023, 73 projects in various technology areas have been approved, with the Government funding commitment totalling over $3 billion. Among them, HKU&#39;s projects, covering advanced medical therapeutics to AI platforms and green materials, demonstrates that world-class research can be seamlessly translated into market-ready, deep-tech ventures. The Government also remains committed to consolidating the &quot;dual base&quot; advantages of Hong Kong&#39;s State Key Laboratories and the InnoHK Research Clusters, enabling universities to conduct basic and applied research that better serves national scientific and technological needs.<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;Looking ahead, the Loop Academy One in the Hong Kong Shenzhen Innovation and Technology Park will be the flagship project for launching the Northern Metropolis University Town, bringing together the frontier research capabilities of leading local and non-local universities and institutions, including InnoHK and other new initiatives. It will focus on the development of medicine, life and health technology, artificial intelligence, robotics and microelectronics, with an aim to linking up academic research with real-life application scenarios and industrial supply chains - both physically and organically.<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;I am also glad to see that HKU is already participating in this wider development - the University serves as the operator of the Greater Bay Area International Clinical Trials Institute in the Loop Hong Kong Park to promote medical innovation and clinical trial development. I encourage HKU to continue anchoring this ecosystem, collaborating closely with industry leaders and the Greater Bay Area&#39;s massive manufacturing base.&nbsp;&nbsp;<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;Ladies and gentlemen, I believe the Innovation Week will provide a useful platform for you to make connections that lead to further collaboration as you extend the boundaries of knowledge. I wish the event every success.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;Thank you very much.<div id="photos"><a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/14/P2026101400310_photo_1341033.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/14/P2026101400310_photo_1341033.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;</div>]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[Speech by DCS at LSCM Logistics Summit 2026 (English only) (with photos)]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/14/P2026101400317.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/14/P2026101400317.htm</link><pubDate>Wed, 14 Oct 2026 11:18:00 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Following is the speech by the Deputy Chief Secretary for Administration, Mr Cheuk Wing-hing, at the LSCM (Logistics and Supply Chain MultiTech R&amp;D Centre) Logistics Summit 2026 today (October 14):<br />
<br />
Deputy Director Wang (Deputy Director of the Eighth Division, General Office of the Liaison Office of the Central People&#39;s Government in the Hong Kong Special Administrative Region Mr Wang Zongru), Chairman Lam (Chairman of the Board of Directors of the LSCM, Professor Alan Lam), distinguished guests, industry leaders, ladies and gentlemen,<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Good morning. It is my great pleasure to join you all today at the LSCM Logistics Summit 2026 to celebrate the 20th Anniversary of the Logistics and Supply Chain MultiTech R&amp;D Centre. This milestone is not only a celebration of the LSCM&#39;s achievements, but also a testament to the remarkable evolution of Hong Kong&#39;s logistics and supply chain ecosystem.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;For two decades, the LSCM has served as a primary engine behind this evolution, which is propelled by advanced innovation and tech-driven transformation. By building a vibrant network of collaboration between our leading universities, industry partners and global stakeholders, the LSCM has successfully translated cutting-edge applied research into powerful core competencies that keep the Hong Kong&#39;s logistics industry globally competitive.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The LSCM&#39;s contribution goes far beyond commercial logistics into the heart of our community. During our COVID-19 response, the LSCM rapidly deployed electronic wristbands to anchor smart quarantine logistics and leveraged its tracking and inventory management systems to put into effect the city&#39;s mass testing and vaccination measures. This is a classic example of how home-grown, practical technology can directly benefit our entire society.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The LSCM has long been one of the Government&#39;s closest partners and advisers. Since joining the Hong Kong Logistics Development Council in 2014, the LSCM has steadfastly helped to shape our forward-looking policies and materialise the Government&#39;s Action Plan on Modern Logistics Development. This plan designates digitalisation as a core pillar for the industry&#39;s future, a direction for which the LSCM has been blazing the trail.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;This momentum in digitalising our logistics sector is not just an industry achievement. It is a critical engine to drive Hong Kong&#39;s First Five-Year Plan. The Five-Year Plan charts an ambitious roadmap with innovation and technology (I&amp;T) at its core, demanding that we strengthen our original scientific research to anchor our economic future. As logistics is the lifeblood of Hong Kong&#39;s economy, modernising this sector through the LSCM&#39;s technologies provides the exact robust foundation needed to achieve our primary goal, i.e. after 2030, we will have established an international I&amp;T centre based in the Guangdong-Hong Kong-Macao Greater Bay Area, serving the entire country and connecting to the world.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;To bring this grand vision to life, the Government will continue to step up our support for R&amp;D (research and development), and drive deeper collaboration among the industry, academia and research sectors. In this endeavour, I am confident that the LSCM will continue to be an indispensable conduit between scientific research and commercial application.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Ladies and gentlemen, the complex supply chain challenges are catalysts for brilliant breakthroughs when great minds meet. By uniting the finest brains across disciplines, this summit shines as a platform for collaboration. Together, let us continue &quot;Linking Minds, Empowering Innovation&quot;, and seize the opportunities ahead to further heighten Hong Kong&#39;s digital transformation and economic growth.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;In closing, I wish you all an inspiring, riveting and successful summit. Thank you very much.<div id="photos"><a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/14/P2026101400317_photo_1341080.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/14/P2026101400317_photo_1341080.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;<a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/14/P2026101400317_photo_1341079.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/14/P2026101400317_photo_1341079.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;<a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/14/P2026101400317_photo_1341078.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/14/P2026101400317_photo_1341078.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;</div>]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[LCQ16: Universal Accessibility Programme]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/14/P2026101400199.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/14/P2026101400199.htm</link><pubDate>Wed, 14 Oct 2026 11:15:00 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Following is a question by the Hon Kwok Fu-yung and a written reply by the Secretary for Transport and Logistics, Ms Mable Chan, in the Legislative Council today (October 14):<br />
<br />
Question:<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The Government launched the Universal Accessibility Programme (UA Programme) in August 2012 to provide more barrier-free access facilities (i.e. lifts and ramps). As at the end of December 2025, a total of 270 items under the UA Programme had been completed. The Highways Department has commissioned the Electrical and Mechanical Services Department to engage registered lift contractors for the repair and maintenance of the completed lifts. In this connection, will the Government inform this Council:<br />
<br />
(1) of the total expenditure on the repair and maintenance of completed lift items under the UA Programme in each of the past five years, and the average annual maintenance expenditure per lift;<br />
<br />
(2) of the respective numbers of lifts under the UA Programme that had to be suspended from service for more than 24 hours and for more than seven days due to breakdowns in each of the past three years; and the details of the case involving the longest service suspension due to a single breakdown and the case involving the highest number of breakdowns in a year (including the location involved, cause of the breakdown, number of days of suspension and the repair costs concerned);<br />
<br />
(3) of the respective numbers of lift items completed, under construction and at the planning stage under the UA Programme in the southwest New Territories (including Tsuen Wan, Kwai Chung and Tsing Yi) at present; and the respective average numbers of hours of service suspension of completed lifts in the southwest New Territories due to routine maintenance and breakdowns over the past three years;<br />
<br />
(4) whether the authorities will attach greater importance to the whole-life cycle maintenance costs of lifts when vetting and assessing lift maintenance contracts; if so, of the details; if not, the reasons for that; and<br />
<br />
(5) as there are views that with the continuous increase in the number of completed lift items under the UA Programme, both the day-to-day repair and maintenance and the emergency repairs of these items rely on professional engineering talent, whether the Government has assessed the demand for and the shortfall in manpower for lift repair and maintenance at present and over the next five years; if so, of the details; if not, the reasons for that?<br />
<br />
Reply:<br />
<br />
President,<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The Universal Accessibility (UA) Programme aims to retrofit public walkways with barrier-free lifts and ramps to enhance the convenience of the public in using these walkways. The Government attaches great importance to the maintenance works of the lifts. The lifts under the UA Programme are maintained by registered lift contractors appointed by the Electrical and Mechanical Services Department (EMSD). The lift contractors perform routine maintenance inspections and arrange preventive maintenance early as needed, in order to enhance the safety and reliability of the lifts.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;In consultation with the Highways Department (HyD) and the EMSD, my reply to the Member&#39;s question is as follows:<br />
<br />
(1) and (4) The recurrent expenditure for each lift under the UA Programme, including electricity charges, the necessary maintenance costs and other relevant expenses, varies depending on the design of each lift (including capacity, size specifications, other ancillary facilities, service age, usage conditions etc) and is estimated to be on average around $370,000 per lift per annum. The HyD and the EMSD do not maintain breakdown figures of maintenance expenditures.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;When preparing lift maintenance service contracts, the HyD and the EMSD take into account a range of factors, including the lift&#39;s condition, age, usage rate, maintenance cost-effectiveness, and breakdown risk. Furthermore, when assessing lift maintenance service contracts, the EMSD considers the technical aspects of the contractors, such as their maintenance proposals and technical support.<br />
<br />
(2) According to the EMSD&#39;s records, over the past three years (i.e. from October 2023 to September 2026), 87 and 13 lifts completed under the UA Programme had to be suspended from service for more than 24 hours and for more than seven days respectively because of a single breakdown. The longest suspension caused by a single breakdown lasted for 19 days. The lift concerned is located at the footbridge across Aberdeen Praya Road near Nam Ning Street. Its ground floor landing door glass was damaged by an external object, and it took time to order replacement parts for the repair. During the service suspension, members of the public could alternatively use the footbridge&#39;s barrier-free ramp.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The lift with the highest number of breakdowns is located at the King&#39;s Road Flyover across Kornhill Road near Taikoo Shing. The breakdowns were mainly caused by debris in the door track and obstructed door sensors, which stopped the lift doors from opening and closing properly. The lift broke down 14 times in total over the above three-year period, and each suspension lasted about two hours on average. Most of the breakdowns happened in 2024. Upon the replacement of components, the number of breakdowns of the lift dropped significantly in 2025 and 2026. The EMSD does not maintain breakdown figures of the maintenance expenditure of each case.<br />
<br />
(3) As at the end of September 2026, 19 and five UA Programme items had been completed and were under construction respectively in Tsuen Wan District, and 27 and 11 items respectively in Kwai Tsing District. After consulting the Tsuen Wan and Kwai Tsing District Councils in August this year, six newly proposed items in Tsuen Wan District and 10 newly proposed items in Kwai Tsing District are initially identified. The HyD will carry out preliminary planning and technical feasibility studies for the newly proposed items in phases.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;According to the EMSD&#39;s records, over the past three years (i.e. from October 2023 to September 2026), among the 65 completed lifts under the UA Programme in New Territories South West, the average service suspension time due to routine maintenance was about 1.2 hours per lift per week. There were about 330 cases of service suspension involving breakdown repairs (including repairs requiring the replacement of parts), with each suspension lasting about 11 hours on average.<br />
<br />
(5) There are currently over 70 000 lifts in Hong Kong, of which about 580 are under the UA Programme. These lifts therefore have little impact on the overall demand for lift and escalator maintenance across the territory. Furthermore, for the completed lifts under the Programme, the EMSD&#39;s lift contractors have met the performance pledge in terms of the time taken to complete repairs. The EMSD has been closely monitoring the manpower situation of the lift and escalator industry, and regularly conducts manpower surveys with registered contractors. The number of registered engineers and general workers increased from about 7 600 in 2019 to about 9 670 in September 2026, representing an increase of about 27 per cent, which is significantly higher than the about 11 per cent increase in the total number of lifts and escalators during the same period. Overall, the industry&#39;s manpower situation has improved over the past few years. There are now enough lift and escalator workers to meet the manpower needs of lift and escalator maintenance. The Government has also implemented various measures to attract local youth to join the industry, enhance talent training, establish clear career promotion pathways, and introduce various talent admission schemes, so as to prepare for the growing engineering demands for lifts and escalators in the long run.]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[Three property owners fined nearly $320,000 in total for persistently not complying with removal orders]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/14/P2026101400230.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/14/P2026101400230.htm</link><pubDate>Wed, 14 Oct 2026 11:10:00 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;​Three owners were convicted and fined $317,440 in total at the Kwun Tong Magistrates&#39; Courts yesterday (October 13) for persistently failing to comply with removal orders issued under the Buildings Ordinance (BO) (Cap. 123).&nbsp;&nbsp;<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The first case involved several projecting structures with a total area of about 5.4 square metres in a composite building on Woosung Street, Jordan. As the unauthorised building works (UBWs) were carried out without prior approval and consent from the Buildings Department (BD), a removal order was served on the owner under section 24(1) of the BO. Failing to comply with the removal order, the owner had been prosecuted by the BD two times and was fined upon conviction by the court. As the owner persisted in not complying with the removal order, the owner was prosecuted for the third time and was fined $139,700 upon conviction by the court, of which $123,700 was the fine for the number of days that the offence continued.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The second case involved an unauthorised structure with an area of about 20 sq m on the flat roof and the removal of a fire-resisting door at the unit entrance in a composite building on Hip Wo Street, Kwun Tong. As the unauthorised works were carried out without prior approval and consent from the BD, and affected the fire-resisting construction of the building, contravening the Building (Construction) Regulation, a removal order was served on the owner under section 24(1) of the BO. Failing to comply with the removal order, the owner had been prosecuted by the BD and was fined upon conviction by the court. As the owner persisted in not complying with the removal order, the owner was prosecuted for the second time and was fined $105,640 upon conviction by the court, of which $90,640 was the fine for the number of days that the offence continued.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The third case involved an unauthorised structure erected in the means of escape and the removal of several fire-resisting concrete block walls and fire-resisting doors in an industrial building on Kwun Tong Road, Kwun Tong. As the alteration and addition works were carried out without prior approval and consent from the BD, and affected the fire-resisting construction of the building, contravening the Building (Construction) Regulation, a removal order was served on the owner under section 24(1) of the BO. Failing to comply with the removal order, the owner had been prosecuted by the BD three times and was fined upon conviction by the court. As the owner persisted in not complying with the removal order, the owner was prosecuted for the fourth time and was fined $72,100 upon conviction by the court, of which $32,100 was the fine for the number of days that the offence continued.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;A spokesman for the BD said today (October 14), &quot;UBWs, including the unauthorised alterations affecting the fire-resisting construction of a building, may lead to serious consequences. Owners must comply with removal orders without delay. The BD will continue to take stringent enforcement action and instigate prosecutions against owners again if they persist in not complying with removal orders to ensure building and public safety.&quot;<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Failure to comply with a removal order without reasonable excuse is a serious offence under the BO. The maximum penalty upon conviction is a fine of $200,000 and one year&#39;s imprisonment, and a further fine of up to $20,000 for each day that the offence continues.]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[Red flags hoisted at Stanley Main Beach and Clear Water Bay Second Beach]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/14/P2026101400281.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/14/P2026101400281.htm</link><pubDate>Wed, 14 Oct 2026 11:00:00 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[Attention TV/radio announcers:<br />
<br />
Please broadcast the following as soon as possible:<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;Here is an item of interest to swimmers.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;The Leisure and Cultural Services Department announced today (October 14) that due to big waves, red flags have been hoisted at Stanley Main Beach in Southern District, Hong Kong Island, and Clear Water Bay Second Beach in Sai Kung District. Beachgoers are advised not to swim at these beaches.]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[FSTB welcomes inaugural issuance of "sidecar" insurance-linked securities in Hong Kong]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/14/P2026101300441.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/14/P2026101300441.htm</link><pubDate>Wed, 14 Oct 2026 11:00:00 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The Financial Services and the Treasury Bureau welcomed an inaugural issuance of insurance-linked securities (ILS) in the form of &quot;sidecar&quot; by the PICC Property and Casualty Company Limited (PICC P&amp;C) in Hong Kong, with a size of US$12 million (equivalent to about HK$94 million), providing quota share reinsurance protection for offshore windfarm property insurance businesses on the Chinese Mainland.&nbsp;<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The Secretary for Financial Services and the Treasury, Mr Christopher Hui, said, &quot;To support the National 15th Five-Year Plan, which identifies the enhancement of the catastrophe risk protection framework and risk-pooling mechanisms as a key task, the Hong Kong&#39;s First Five-Year Plan published last month proposed fully leveraging Hong Kong&#39;s competitive edge in the international insurance, reinsurance, and capital markets to advance the synergised development of traditional insurance and non-traditional risk management tools. This will enable us to unleash the synergy effect of multilayered risk transfer and pooling mechanisms through ILS and other tools, thus consolidating our strategic role as a regional and global catastrophe and specialty risk management hub.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&quot;We welcome PICC P&amp;C&rsquo;s issuance of Hong Kong&#39;s first &#39;sidecar&#39;. This demonstrates Hong Kong&#39;s combined strengths in reinsurance and capital markets. The issuance marks the significant progress gained for Hong Kong&#39;s insurance industry and capital markets in risk transfer and the use of alternative capital under the support of our country. It will further enhance the market&#39;s capacity to manage catastrophe and other specialty risks, and consolidate Hong Kong&#39;s position as an international risk management centre. As announced by the Chief Executive in the 2026 Policy Address, we will continue to work with the Insurance Authority in promoting ILS, narrowing the protection gap for catastrophe and other specialty risk, and reviewing investor restrictions to invigorate the ILS fund trading market. Meanwhile, we will explore legislative amendments to introduce a &#39;protected cell company&#39; structure, which will help further reduce the costs of issuing ILS, thereby attracting more companies of different scales to choose Hong Kong as a base for managing group or regional risks.&quot;<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Mr Hui added, &quot;ILS and captive insurance, which we have long been promoting, can provide enterprises &#39;going global&#39; with diversified means of spreading and managing risks. We expect this combination of financial policy measures will allow enterprises to leverage Hong Kong as a safe haven for &#39;going global&#39; with greater ease of mind.&quot;<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Since the establishment of the bespoke regulatory regime for ILS and the launch of the pilot grant scheme in 2021, seven catastrophe bonds and one &quot;sidecar&quot; have been issued in Hong Kong to date, with a total issuance amount of about HK$6.3 billion, securing protection against catastrophe and other specialty risks on the Chinese Mainland and overseas. In September 2025, the National Financial Regulatory Administration issued the &quot;Notification on the Issuance of Insurance-linked Securities on Hong Kong Market by Domestic Chinese Insurers&quot;, which supports interested Mainland insurers to issue &quot;sidecar&quot; ILS in Hong Kong, with a view to diversifying the channels for dispersing catastrophe insurance risks.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;A &quot;sidecar&quot; is an ILS structure through which investors provide capital to support a specific insurance or reinsurance portfolio. Generally speaking, a &quot;sidecar&quot; is typically capitalised through equity or a debt instrument (such as preference shares or participating notes), and enables investors to share, on a quota-share basis with the sponsor the premiums, claims and underwriting results of a designated insurance or reinsurance portfolio.]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[Speech by SCED at HKGCC Silver Economy Summit (English only)]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/14/P2026101400271.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/14/P2026101400271.htm</link><pubDate>Wed, 14 Oct 2026 10:31:00 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Following is the speech by the Secretary for Commerce and Economic Development, Mr Algernon Yau, at the Hong Kong General Chamber of Commerce (HKGCC) Silver Economy Summit today (October 14):<br />
&nbsp;<br />
Mr Douglas Woo (Vice Chairman of the HKGCC), Mr Zhou (Deputy Director-General of the Economic Affairs Department and Head of the Commercial Office of the Liaison Office of the Central People&#39;s Government in the Hong Kong Special Administrative Region, Mr Zhou Qiang), distinguished speakers and guests, ladies and gentlemen,<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Good morning. It gives me great pleasure to join you at the HKGCC Silver Economy Summit. I would like to thank the HKGCC for organising this timely Summit and bringing together leaders from business, finance, healthcare, technology, professional services and other sectors. The diversity of expertise represented here demonstrates that the silver economy offers opportunities across a wide range of sectors.<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Hong Kong is entering a new era of longevity. As at mid-2025, there were nearly 1.8 million persons aged 65 and above in Hong Kong, accounting for almost one quarter of our population. By 2046, this figure is projected to rise to 2.74 million, or 36 per cent of the population. This demographic shift will drive growing demand for products and services that support healthier, more independent and more fulfilling later lives.<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; The silver economy is about much more than elderly care. It covers different aspects of daily life, from food, retail and travel to healthcare, financial planning, lifestyle and technology. It is about recognising the diverse needs and preferences of older persons, and providing them with more suitable, accessible and quality choices. These needs also create new business opportunities, encouraging enterprises to develop age-friendly products and services, meet growing demand and strengthen their competitiveness while bringing economic and social benefits.<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; The Government has established the Working Group on Promoting Silver Economy and rolled out 30 measures under five areas of work since May 2025, including boosting &quot;silver consumption&quot;, developing &quot;silver industry&quot;, promoting &quot;quality assurance of silver products&quot;, enhancing &quot;silver financial and security arrangements&quot;, and unleashing &quot;silver productivity&quot;. The two panel discussions today reflect these priorities and highlight the diverse opportunities presented by the silver economy.<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; These measures cover various policy areas and are being implemented by the relevant policy bureaux. My Bureau has been working closely with the industry to promote &quot;silver consumption&quot; and quality assurance, with encouraging progress. We have been encouraging businesses to incorporate silver economy elements into major exhibitions and to introduce products and services tailored to the needs of older persons. For example, more restaurant chains and food companies are now offering soft meals for older persons with swallowing difficulties. We are also working with the Hong Kong Council of Social Service and the Hong Kong Trade Development Council to make more senior-friendly products available in physical stores and e-commerce platforms.<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Quality assurance is equally important in building consumer confidence and supporting the sustainable development of the silver economy. The Hong Kong Registration &ndash; Silver-friendly Series launched by the Hong Kong Quality Assurance Agency has received more than 160 applications covering venues, products and services across sectors. Meanwhile, the Guangdong-Hong Kong-Macao Greater Bay Area Standard for care food and Hong Kong&#39;s first Care Food Product Certification Mark Scheme are helping businesses build trust, improve their offerings and capture opportunities in the silver market.<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Beyond promoting the silver economy, the Government is taking a broader and more comprehensive approach to addressing the challenges and opportunities arising from an ageing population. Last year, we set up the Working Group on Ageing Society Strategies to review the policies and measures of various bureaux. The Working Group released its report last month, which sets out 73 measures across 11 areas. These include developing the silver economy to meet the changing needs of older persons and create new business opportunities.<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Among other initiatives, we will collaborate with the industry and relevant organisations to explore extending silver certification to a wider range of age-friendly products and services. In particular, we will study the development of standards for mobility aids, such as walking sticks, wheelchairs and exoskeleton systems, and explore the possibility of incorporating these standards into the Guangdong-Hong Kong-Macao Greater Bay Area Standards framework.<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; We will also continue to boost &quot;silver consumption&quot; by encouraging the industry to better incorporate the needs and preferences of the elderly into its business strategies and planning. Our efforts include staging more silver economy exhibitions, offering retail and catering concessions to the elderly, encouraging e-commerce platforms to diversify their range of silver products and facilitate online shopping, and expanding the availability of silver products in restaurants and other retail outlets.<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Ladies and gentlemen, the silver economy is about creating better choices and better lives for older persons. It is also about fostering innovation, unlocking new market opportunities and creating fresh momentum for Hong Kong&#39;s economic development. I am confident that, by bringing together the strengths of the business community, professional sectors and different partners, we can develop more quality products and services, capture the opportunities in the Guangdong-Hong Kong-Macao Greater Bay Area, the Mainland and beyond, and make Hong Kong a more age-friendly and vibrant city.<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; I wish the HKGCC Silver Economy Summit 2026 every success, and all participants a fruitful and rewarding morning. Thank you.]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[Special traffic arrangements for race meeting in Happy Valley]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/14/P2026101400218.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/14/P2026101400218.htm</link><pubDate>Wed, 14 Oct 2026 09:20:30 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The Police will implement special traffic arrangements in Happy Valley today (October 14). These arrangements will remain in effect until the race meeting has concluded, spectators have dispersed, and traffic conditions return to normal.<br />
<br />
A. Traffic arrangements before the commencement of the first race<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The following road closure and traffic diversions will be implemented from noon for day meetings or from 6pm onwards for night meetings:<br />
<br />
1. Road closure<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Southbound Wong Nai Chung Road between Queen&rsquo;s Road East and the up-ramp outside the Hong Kong Jockey Club (HKJC) will be closed, except for vehicles heading for Aberdeen Tunnel.<br />
<br />
2. Traffic diversions<br />
<br />
- Southbound Wong Nai Chung Road between Village Road and the up-ramp outside the HKJC will be rerouted one way northbound;<br />
- Traffic along eastbound Queen&#39;s Road East heading for Wan Chai and Happy Valley will be diverted to turn left to Morrison Hill Road;<br />
- Traffic along southbound Morrison Hill Road heading for Happy Valley will be diverted via Sports Road and Wong Nai Chung Road;<br />
- Traffic along Queen&#39;s Road East cannot turn right to Wong Nai Chung Road, except for vehicles heading for Aberdeen Tunnel;<br />
- Traffic from Cross Harbour Tunnel heading for Queen&#39;s Road East will be diverted via the down-ramp leading from southbound Canal Road flyover to Morrison Hill Road to turn right at the junction of Wong Nai Chung Road and Queen&#39;s Road East; and<br />
- Traffic from Cross Harbour Tunnel heading for Happy Valley or Racecourse will be diverted via the down-ramp leading from southbound Canal Road flyover to Canal Road East, southbound Morrison Hill Road, Sports Road and Wong Nai Chung Road.<br />
<br />
B. Traffic arrangements before the conclusion of race meeting<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;The following road closure and traffic diversions will be implemented from about 35 minutes before the start of the last race:<br />
<br />
1. Road closure<br />
<br />
- The up-ramp on Wong Nai Chung Road outside the HKJC leading to Aberdeen Tunnel;<br />
- Southbound Wong Nai Chung Road between Queen&#39;s Road East and the up-ramp leading to Aberdeen Tunnel;<br />
- Southbound Wong Nai Chung Road between Village Road and the Public Stands of the HKJC; and<br />
- Southbound Morrison Hill Road between Leighton Road and Queen&#39;s Road East.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;In addition, southbound Wong Nai Chung Road between the up-ramp leading to Aberdeen Tunnel and the Public Stands of the HKJC will be closed from about 10 minutes before the start of the last race.<br />
<br />
2. Traffic diversions<br />
<br />
- Eastbound Queen&#39;s Road East at its junction with Morrison Hill Road will be reduced to one-lane traffic heading for northbound Canal Road flyover;<br />
- Traffic from Cross Harbour Tunnel heading for Wan Chai will be diverted via the down-ramp leading from southbound Canal Road flyover to Canal Road East, U-turn slip road beneath Canal Road flyover, Canal Road West and Hennessy Road;<br />
- Traffic along southbound Morrison Hill Road heading for Happy Valley will be diverted via Sports Road and Wong Nai Chung Road; and<br />
- Traffic from Cross Harbour Tunnel heading for Happy Valley or Racecourse will be diverted via the down-ramp leading from southbound Canal Road flyover to Canal Road East, southbound Morrison Hill Road, Sports Road and Wong Nai Chung Road.<br />
<br />
C. Prohibition for learner drivers<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;Learner drivers will be prohibited to turn left from Caroline Hill Road to Leighton Road between one and a half hours before the start of the first race and one hour after the last race. In addition, learner drivers will be prohibited from accessing the following roads within the above period of time:&nbsp;<br />
<br />
- Shan Kwong Road between Yik Yam Street and Wong Nai Chung Road;<br />
- Village Road between its upper and lower junctions with Shan Kwong Road;<br />
- Percival Street between Hennessy Road and Leighton Road;<br />
- Canal Road East; and<br />
- The service road leading from Gloucester Road to Canal Road flyover.<br />
<br />
D. Suspension of parking spaces<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;Parking spaces on southbound Wong Nai Chung Road between Sports Road and Blue Pool Road will be suspended from 11am to 7pm for day meetings, and from 5pm to 11.59pm for night meetings respectively.&nbsp;<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;During the implementation of the special traffic arrangements, any vehicles found illegally parked within the precincts of the specified areas will be towed away without prior warning, and may be subject to multiple ticketing.&nbsp;<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Depending on the prevailing conditions, other traffic control measures will be implemented. Motorists are advised to exercise patience and tolerance, and take heed of instructions of the police officers on site.]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[Red flags hoisted at Shek O Beach and Silverstrand Beach]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/14/P2026101400213.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/14/P2026101400213.htm</link><pubDate>Wed, 14 Oct 2026 09:20:00 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[Attention TV/radio announcers:<br />
<br />
Please broadcast the following as soon as possible:<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Here is an item of interest to swimmers.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The Leisure and Cultural Services Department announced today (October 14) that due to big waves, red flags have been hoisted at Shek O Beach in Southern District, Hong Kong Island, and Silverstrand Beach in Sai Kung District. Beachgoers are advised not to swim at these beaches.]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[Hong Kong Customs seizes suspected smuggled pharmaceutical products worth about $18 million (with photo)]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/13/P2026101300672.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/13/P2026101300672.htm</link><pubDate>Tue, 13 Oct 2026 21:48:00 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;​Hong Kong Customs today (October 13) detected a suspected smuggling case involving an outgoing lorry at the Hong Kong-Zhuhai-Macao Bridge Hong Kong Port and seized about 320 000 numbers of suspected pharmaceutical products with an estimated market value of about $18 million.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Based on risk assessment, Customs officers intercepted an outgoing lorry declared to contain supplements, skincare products and clothes, etc. at the above-mentioned Port. Upon inspection, the batch of suspected pharmaceutical products was found inside the cargo compartment.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;After an investigation, Customs arrested a 39-year-old Mainland male driver suspected to be connected with the case. He was charged with one count of attempting to export unmanifested cargo and will appear at the West Kowloon Magistrates&#39; Courts tomorrow (October 14).<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Customs will continue to combat cross boundary smuggling activities with firm enforcement action based on risk assessment and intelligence analysis.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Smuggling is a serious offence. Under the Import and Export Ordinance, any person found guilty of importing or exporting unmanifested cargo is liable to a maximum fine of $2 million and imprisonment for seven years. Any person who exports pharmaceutical products and medicines without a valid export licence commits an offence. The maximum penalty upon conviction is a fine of $500,000 and imprisonment for two years.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Members of the public may report any suspected smuggling activities to Customs&#39; 24-hour hotline 182 8080 or its dedicated crime-reporting email account (<a href="mailto:crimereport@customs.gov.hk">crimereport@customs.gov.hk</a>) or online form (<a href="https://eform.cefs.gov.hk/form/ced002/en/" target="_blank">eform.cefs.gov.hk/form/ced002</a>).<div id="photos"><a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/13/P2026101300672_photo_1340990.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/13/P2026101300672_photo_1340990.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;</div>]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[Woman holding charged with murder]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/13/P2026101300690.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/13/P2026101300690.htm</link><pubDate>Tue, 13 Oct 2026 21:46:51 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Police today (October 13) laid a holding charge against a 52-year-old foreign woman with one count of murder.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The woman was arrested yesterday (October 12) in suspected connection with a murder case happened in Ngau Tau Kok on October 11, in which a 93-year-old woman died.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The case will be mentioned at the Kwun Tong Magistrates&#39; Courts tomorrow (October 14) morning.]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[CHP investigates confirmed Mpox case and continues to take multipronged approach to break transmission chains]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/13/P2026101300621.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/13/P2026101300621.htm</link><pubDate>Tue, 13 Oct 2026 20:47:19 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The Centre for Health Protection (CHP) of the Department of Health (DH) announced today (October 13) that it is investigating a confirmed Mpox (also known as Monkeypox) case. Due to the recent significant increase in Mpox cases in Hong Kong, the CHP is implementing targeted prevention and control measures through a multi-pronged approach in order to break the transmission chains. Owing to these measures, the number of vaccines administered under the Mpox vaccination programme has risen significantly, with the number of doses administered earlier this month surging by more than 70 per cent compared to the same period last month. The CHP urges individuals who have engaged in high-risk sexual contact, particularly with strangers, to remain vigilant and take appropriate personal protective measures, including receiving the Mpox vaccination and avoiding high-risk sexual contact, so as to lower the risk of infection. The CHP also strongly urges the public to retain their sexual partners&#39; contact information to facilitate contact tracing when necessary.<br />
&nbsp;<br />
Case details<br />
--------------<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The new case involves a 39-year-old man who developed rash over his right foot, face, back and buttock since October 5. He sought medical consultation at a private clinic on October 8, and was referred to Yan Chai Hospital on the following day for isolation treatment. Upon laboratory testing by the CHP&#39;s Public Health Laboratory Services Branch (PHLSB), his clinical specimen tested positive for the Monkeypox virus. The patient is currently in stable condition. He was transferred to Princess Margaret Hospital (PMH) to continue isolation treatment.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; According to the information provided by the patient, during the incubation period, he visited a premises called &quot;Hutong&quot; on Shanghai Street, Mong Kok, on multiple occasions and had multiple high-risk sexual contacts with male strangers there. The CHP has conducted joint on‑site investigation at the premises with the Police. Furthermore, the CHP has provided health education to the staff of the premises. To date, all staff members remain asymptomatic. As the CHP cannot rule out the transmission risk at the premises, as a prudent measure it is recommended that &quot;Hutong&quot; be temporarily closed with immediate effect from today.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The CHP appeals to the members of the public who have visited &quot;Hutong&quot; since September 14, especially those who have had&nbsp;sexual contact with strangers there, to call the Mpox telephone hotline (2125 2373) as soon as possible, to facilitate the CHP to provide health assessments and advice. The CHP will arrange persons with relevant symptoms to undergo testing.&nbsp;<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; The CHP is continuing its epidemiological investigations of the case, and will report the case to the World Health Organization.<br />
<br />
Latest situation of Mpox cases<br />
------------------------------------<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Since 2022, Hong Kong has recorded 115 Mpox cases (91 local cases and 24 imported cases). All patients are males. As of today, 31 cases have been recorded this year, including 24 local cases and seven imported cases. Since August, there have been 22 new cases, accounting for 70 per cent of the total number of cases this year.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Epidemiological investigations showed that all the 22 cases recorded since August reported having engaged in high-risk sexual activities during the incubation period, including having sex with male strangers or multiple partners, and having sex without a condom. Most of them have not received the Mpox vaccinations.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The CHP&#39;s PHLSB conducted genetic analysis of the virus and found that some cases had identical genetic sequences. This suggests that the cases may have shared a common source of infection, with multiple transmission chains existing among men who have sex with men population.<br />
&nbsp;<br />
New Mpox vaccination venues<br />
-----------------------------------------<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Mpox vaccine can prevent infection and severe disease. Since late September, in addition to regular vaccination venues, the CHP has set up an additional Mpox vaccination centre at the CHP Building, and has collaborated with three NGOs (namely Community Health Organisation for Intervention, Care and Empowerment; Hong Kong AIDS Foundation; and Hong Kong Christian Service) to provide outreach vaccination services. Together with the various existing vaccination venues, the CHP has successfully administered the Mpox vaccine to approximately 380 high-risk individuals over the past two weeks.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;To further reduce the risk of community infection and transmission, the CHP will continue to actively increase the locations and time slots for Mpox vaccination. Following the completion of three outreach vaccination sessions recently, the CHP has arranged the following services:<br />
<br />
(i) Hong Kong AIDS Foundation - Mong Kok Service Centre<br />
Date and time: October 14 (Wednesday), 5pm to 8pm<br />
Location: Bright Way Tower, 33 Mong Kok Road, Mong Kok, Kowloon<br />
(Online appointments:&nbsp;<a href="https://forms.gle/ytDgGsn9V2R8wL4VA" target="_blank">forms.gle/ytDgGsn9V2R8wL4VA</a>)<br />
<br />
(ii) Yau Ma Tei Integrated Treatment Centre (additional session)<br />
Date and time: October 24 (Saturday), 10am to 1pm<br />
Location: 7/F, Yau Ma Tei Jockey Club Polyclinic, 145 Battery Street, Yau Ma Tei<br />
(Phone reservations: Please call Yau Ma Tei Integrated Treatment Centre at 2195 6588 from 8.30am to 5pm, Monday to Friday, to make an appointment.)<br />
&nbsp;<br />
Strengthening public education and awareness<br />
------------------------------------------------------<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The CHP&#39;s Mpox hotline (2125 2373) provides health assessments, advice and vaccination arrangements for affected individuals. The hotline operates daily from 9am to 6pm.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The CHP has consistently conducted comprehensive and in-depth epidemiological investigations into every Mpox case, striving to contact individuals who have had high-risk contact with the patients. The CHP&#39;s epidemiological investigations showed that patients commonly used specific dating apps to find casual sexual partners. After testing positive, some patients were unable to contact their newly met sexual partners, which hindered the CHP&#39;s ability to conduct effective contact tracing and implement corresponding prevention and control measures. In view of this, the CHP has increased its outreach on social media platforms to promote Mpox prevention and vaccination, and has collaborated with the operators of relevant dating apps to regularly push information about Mpox prevention and vaccination to all users in Hong Kong.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The CHP staff visited various entertainment venues, including saunas, to promote health and remind individuals at high risk of exposure that vaccination protects against infection and reduces the likelihood of transmitting it to others. Due to the current surge in Mpox cases, individuals should limit the number of sexual partners and use condoms in addition to getting vaccinated to further reduce the risk of infection.<br />
&nbsp;<br />
More information on Mpox<br />
----------------------------------<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Mpox is not transmitted through respiratory droplets or aerosols in general, and transmission would not occur through social contact. The CHP reminds the public, especially those at higher risk of exposure, to take precautions and avoid close physical contact with persons or animals suspected of being infected. They should seek medical attention as soon as possible if they experience relevant symptoms, including rash, fever, chills, swollen lymph nodes, exhaustion, muscle pain, and severe headaches. They should not engage in activities with others that may involve contact with skin rash or body fluids.<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The DH has been providing Mpox vaccination services for high-risk groups, including individuals with high-risk sexual practices (such as having multiple sexual partners), sex workers, or having a history of sexually transmitted infection within the past 12 months. Apart from the aforementioned newly added Mpox vaccination locations, high-risk target groups can receive Mpox walk-in vaccinations at any of the DH&#39;s social hygiene service clinics (SocHS) (namely Chai Wan SocHS, Wan Chai Male SocHS, Wan Chai Female SocHS, Yau Ma Tei Male SocHS, Yau Ma Tei Female SocHS, Yung Fung Shee SocHS, Fanling SocHS and Tuen Mun SocHS) and the DH&#39;s Yau Ma Tei Integrated Treatment Centre, without prior appointments. Meanwhile, the DH&#39;s Kowloon Bay Integrated Treatment Centre and the Hospital Authority&#39;s Special Medical Clinics at Queen Elizabeth Hospital and PMH also provide Mpox vaccination services for their clients.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;For more details, please visit the CHP&#39;s webpages on&nbsp;<a href="https://www.chp.gov.hk/en/features/105683.html" target="_blank">Mpox</a>&nbsp;and the&nbsp;<a href="https://www.chp.gov.hk/en/features/106090.html" target="_blank">Mpox Vaccination Programme</a>.]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[Speech by SCST at closing reception of Hong Kong Performing Arts Expo 2026 (English only)]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/13/P2026101300606.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/13/P2026101300606.htm</link><pubDate>Tue, 13 Oct 2026 20:42:00 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Following is the speech by the Secretary for Culture, Sports and Tourism, Miss Rosanna Law, at the closing reception of the Hong Kong Performing Arts Expo (HKPAX) 2026 today (October 13):<br />
<br />
Kenneth (Chairman of the Hong Kong Arts Development Council, Mr Kenneth Fok), distinguished guests, ladies and gentlemen,<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Good evening. This is actually a very hard job for me, coming after Kenneth&#39;s such a passionate and genuinely heartfelt speech. I have to give you the official remarks of course, but I think everybody is touched by the commitment and devotion of Kenneth and the Hong Kong Arts Development Council under his leadership. And from the bright atmosphere here, I can tell that a lot of actions have been made, a lot of exchanges have been strengthened within the past five days, and that is what we love to experience and know. It is my great pleasure to join you all at the closing reception of HKPAX 2026.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;This year&#39;s theme is &quot;Passion in Motion&quot;, and Hong Kong has come alive with creativity, dialogue and exploration. Over the past few days - from the opening prologue to this afternoon&#39;s final showcase - the Expo has presented a rich array of performances and programmes spanning the full spectrum of performing arts. More importantly, it has served as a dynamic platform for local and international participants to engage in inspiring dialogue like what we are doing right now, exchange innovative ideas and explore new collaborations. I am truly delighted that HKPAX has kindled a deeper passion for performing arts among all who have taken part.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;International exchange and collaboration are essential to a world-class arts and cultural sector. The Expo is Hong Kong&#39;s flagship platform for cross-cultural and artistic exchange, and it has facilitated meaningful encounters that will invigorate our performing arts landscape. The connections forged this week - from showcases to pitching sessions - will continue to bear fruit long after the Expo curtain falls.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Beyond its long-term goals, the Expo&#39;s immediate impact is already evident in the numbers that the Chairman has just mentioned. We welcomed more than 2 000 arts leaders and professionals from 60 countries and regions, along with over 39 000 audience members and participants. For an expo that is only two years old, this level of participation speaks volumes about its rising international standing.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The vitality of Hong Kong&#39;s performing arts sector is powered by a diverse network of professional-grade venues. From the iconic Hong Kong Cultural Centre to the newly commissioned East Kowloon Cultural Centre, venues across the city have played host to an extraordinary array of performances and showcases this week. And we have something new to welcome. Next year, the WestK Performing Arts Centre will set to open, and Hong Kong&#39;s arts infrastructure will be better placed than ever to support ambitious and groundbreaking productions. We look forward to the next episode of HKPAX in 2028.&nbsp;<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Culture thrives on enduring commitment and the Hong Kong SAR (Hong Kong Special Administrative Region) Government remains committed to investing in talent development, fostering creative experimentation and supporting the industry in reaching beyond Hong Kong. Leveraging the distinctive advantages of strong support from our country and close connections with the world, Hong Kong has once again demonstrated, through HKPAX, that it is a premier hub for international cultural exchange. Looking ahead, we will continue to support HKPAX as a platform for showcasing projects and building partnerships.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;My heartfelt gratitude goes to Kenneth, and the Hong Kong Arts Development Council under his capable leadership for the meticulous organisation and tireless dedication in staging this episode of HKPAX. Special thanks also go to the participating artists, performing groups and other delegates, as well as all our sponsors, partners and volunteers. It is you guys&#39; collective effort that make HKPAX such a resounding success. I wish you all the very best in your future endeavours, and as Kenneth has said, for those of you who are set to go to Shanghai, to the Greater Bay Area, a very good and safe trip and rewarding exchanges. I look forward to seeing you all in 2028. Thank you.]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[108 more nomination forms for Election Committee Subsector Ordinary Elections received today]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/13/P2026101300597.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/13/P2026101300597.htm</link><pubDate>Tue, 13 Oct 2026 20:00:00 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The Returning Officers for the various subsectors of the 2026 Election Committee Subsector Ordinary Elections received a total of 99 nomination forms for candidates and nine nomination forms from designated bodies today (October 13). This brings to 1 061 the total number of nomination forms for candidates and 32 the total number of nomination forms from designated bodies received since nominations opened on October 7. The nomination period will end on October 20.<br />
