&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Particulars of the nominations received today will be uploaded to the election website (<a href="https://www.elections.gov.hk/ecss2026/eng/index.html" target="_blank">www.elections.gov.hk</a>).]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[Inter-departmental Working Group on Alternative Uses for Vacated Market Space holds first meeting (with photo)]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/13/P2026101300581.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/13/P2026101300581.htm</link><pubDate>Tue, 13 Oct 2026 19:40:00 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The Inter-departmental Working Group on Alternative Uses for Vacated Market Space, led by the Secretary for Environment and Ecology, Mr Tse Chin-wan, held its first meeting today (October 13).<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;In view of structural changes in the public&#39;s grocery shopping habits, the 2026 Policy Address announced that the Secretary for Environment and Ecology would lead an interdepartmental working group to explore alternative uses for vacated market spaces (e.g. community facilities).<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;At the first meeting, Mr Tse said, &quot;Our policies on public markets must keep pace with the times, while the allocation of premises must also be adjusted flexibly in response to service needs. Markets with persistently low occupancy rates can be consolidated to free up valuable land resources for better alternative uses, adding value to the community and benefiting the public.&quot;<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;To take forward these objectives, the Working Group discussed its overall work plan and was briefed by departments on the current situation of individual markets. Among them, the Quarry Bay Market and Cooked Food Centre has an occupancy rate of only about 40 per cent with no shortage of fresh food retail outlets nearby. Consideration could be given to putting the premises to alternative uses and providing additional public facilities for the convenience of residents. The Working Group agreed that the Food and Environmental Hygiene Department could further consult the District Council and other stakeholders.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The membership of the Working Group is as follows:<br />
<br />
Chairperson<br />
--------------<br />
Secretary for Environment and Ecology<br />
<br />
Vice-Chairperson<br />
--------------------<br />
Permanent Secretary for Environment and Ecology (Food)<br />
<br />
Members<br />
----------<br />
Director of Food and Environmental Hygiene<br />
Director of Home Affairs&nbsp;<br />
Director of Architectural Services&nbsp;<br />
Government Property Administrator<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Representatives of other bureaux and departments will attend meetings as necessary.<div id="photos"><a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/13/P2026101300581_photo_1340989.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/13/P2026101300581_photo_1340989.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;</div>]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[FS continues visit to Nanjing (with photos/video)]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/13/P2026101300550.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/13/P2026101300550.htm</link><pubDate>Tue, 13 Oct 2026 18:39:00 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;The Financial Secretary, Mr Paul Chan, today (October 13) continued his visit to Nanjing with a delegation, meeting with Jiangsu provincial leaders and business representatives, attending a promotion conference on supporting enterprises in going global, and visiting a platform for the commercialisation of research outcomes. The visit seeks to deepen co-operation between Jiangsu and Hong Kong, and support Mainland enterprises in leveraging Hong Kong to expand their global business.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;In the morning, Mr Chan attended a promotion conference on supporting Mainland enterprises in going global, themed &quot;Jiangsu-Hong Kong Partnership: Setting Sail Together&quot;. In his address, he said that this year marks the beginning of the National 15th Five-Year Plan. Through closer co-operation, Jiangsu and Hong Kong can seize the opportunities arising from the country&#39;s pursuit of high-level opening up and translate them into tangible results for enterprises expanding into international markets. He encouraged Jiangsu enterprises to use Hong Kong as a base for their long-term development when planning their global operations.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;Mr Chan noted that Jiangsu and Hong Kong enjoy a solid foundation of co-operation. Hong Kong is both Jiangsu&#39;s largest source of foreign investment and its largest destination for outward investment. As at the end of September this year, 129 Jiangsu enterprises were listed in Hong Kong, with a combined market capitalisation of about HK$1.44 trillion. He said that the two places have a sound basis for co-operation, with ample scope for further collaboration in innovation-driven development and expansion into international markets.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;He said that Hong Kong&#39;s strengths extend beyond stock market listings and fundraising to its high concentration of international capital, financial institutions and professional talent. Last year, Hong Kong recorded 119 new listings, raising over HK$280 billion and ranking first globally in initial public offering fundraising. In the first nine months of this year, Hong Kong recorded over 110 new listings, raising more than HK$380 billion, more than double the amount raised in the same period last year. More than 70 of the world&#39;s top 100 banks operate in Hong Kong. Enterprises can connect with international investors and partners through Hong Kong, broadening their financing channels to support business development at different stages.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;On talent and innovation and technology (I&amp;T), Mr Chan said that Hong Kong&#39;s free, open and international environment helps enterprises attract research, engineering and management talent from the Mainland and overseas. Hong Kong has more than 50 international schools offering a diverse range of curricula. Together with its convenient transport network and vibrant lifestyle, these provide a favourable environment for talent and their families to settle and pursue long-term development in Hong Kong. The Hong Kong Special Administrative Region (HKSAR) Government is taking forward the development of the Northern Metropolis and university towns, bringing together quality education and research resources from Hong Kong, the Mainland and overseas to foster the co-ordinated development of education, technology, talent and industries, and accelerate the commercialisation of research outcomes. He invited Jiangsu enterprises to visit Hong Kong and the Northern Metropolis to explore co-operation opportunities.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;Mr Chan also pointed out that, in attracting enterprises and nurturing industries, the HKSAR Government looks not only at the scale of investment, but also at enterprises&#39; technological capabilities, capacity for innovation, and long-term contributions to Hong Kong&#39;s I&amp;T ecosystem and talent development. The Government is willing to explore investment co-operation with promising enterprises aligned with Hong Kong&#39;s future development priorities, and offer support in areas including land, taxation and other complementary measures. He expressed the hope of building long-term partnerships with Jiangsu enterprises and supporting more Mainland enterprises in becoming multinational corporations with global influence, bringing Chinese technologies, products and solutions to markets around the world.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;Mr Chan also had breakfast with a group of young Jiangsu entrepreneurs in the morning, exchanging views on business development and co-operation opportunities between the two places. He also attended a sharing session with Hong Kong business people and enterprises in Jiangsu to learn about their business development in the province.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;In the afternoon, the delegation visited the National Regional Technology Transfer and Transformation Centre for Higher Education Institutions (Jiangsu) to learn about its work in promoting the transfer and commercialisation of university research outcomes, matching them with enterprises&#39; needs, and fostering industry-academia-research collaboration. Focusing on areas including biomedicine, information and communications, and advanced materials, the centre provides support covering technology research and development, proof of concept and pilot testing. It promotes the deeper integration of technological and industrial innovation, helps nurture emerging and future industries, and advances the integrated development of education, technology and talent.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;The delegation subsequently met with officials and representatives of Nanjing&#39;s Jiangbei New Area and the centre, as well as representatives of local industries and enterprises. They exchanged views on promoting two-way co-operation between Jiangsu and Hong Kong, and between Nanjing and Hong Kong, as well as establishing more regular mechanisms for I&amp;T collaboration.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;At the meeting, Mr Chan proposed strengthening two-way collaboration in three areas to deepen co-operation and deliver more concrete results. First, Jiangsu enterprises can make good use of Hong Kong&#39;s financing and treasury management platforms to support their international business expansion. Second, they can leverage Hong Kong&#39;s professional services to align with international standards, creating favourable conditions for their products and services to enter overseas markets. Third, enterprises can capitalise on Hong Kong&#39;s strengths in international data resources and talent to drive product and service innovation, and better respond to the evolving needs of international markets. He also encouraged the two places to strengthen connections among science and technology parks, universities, enterprises, investment institutions and the professional services sector, facilitate talent exchanges, and bring more promising research projects to fruition.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;In the evening, Mr Chan called on the Governor of Jiangsu Province, Mr Liu Xiaotao. They exchanged views on deepening economic and trade co-operation between Jiangsu and Hong Kong, strengthening connections between innovation and industrial resources, and jointly supporting enterprises in expanding into overseas markets. Mr Chan said he looked forward to further broadening co-operation in youth development, talent and cultural exchanges, alongside economic, trade and I&amp;T collaboration, to provide fresh impetus for the long-term partnership between Jiangsu and Hong Kong.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;Mr Chan and the delegation will continue their visit to Nanjing tomorrow morning and depart for Hong Kong in the afternoon.<div id="photos"><a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/13/P2026101300550_photo_1340963.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/13/P2026101300550_photo_1340963.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;<a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/13/P2026101300550_photo_1340964.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/13/P2026101300550_photo_1340964.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;<a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/13/P2026101300550_photo_1340965.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/13/P2026101300550_photo_1340965.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;<a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/13/P2026101300550_photo_1340966.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/13/P2026101300550_photo_1340966.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;<a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/13/P2026101300550_photo_1340962.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/13/P2026101300550_photo_1340962.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;<a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/13/P2026101300550_photo_1340961.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/13/P2026101300550_photo_1340961.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;<a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/13/P2026101300550_photo_1340967.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/13/P2026101300550_photo_1340967.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;<a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/13/P2026101300550_photo_1340968.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/13/P2026101300550_photo_1340968.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;<a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/13/P2026101300550_photo_1340981.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/13/P2026101300550_photo_1340981.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;<a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/13/P2026101300550_photo_1340985.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/13/P2026101300550_photo_1340985.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;<a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/13/P2026101300550_photo_1340986.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/13/P2026101300550_photo_1340986.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;<a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/13/P2026101300550_photo_1340987.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/13/P2026101300550_photo_1340987.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;<a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/13/P2026101300550_photo_1340988.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/13/P2026101300550_photo_1340988.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;</div>]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[Judiciary alerts public to suspicious emails]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/13/P2026101300527.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/13/P2026101300527.htm</link><pubDate>Tue, 13 Oct 2026 18:26:00 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[The following is issued on behalf of the Judiciary:<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;The Judiciary today (October 13) reiterated its call for the public to stay alert to suspicious emails and avoid opening them or following any instructions or requests from them.<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; The Judiciary recently received public enquiries about a number of emails sent from the domain &quot;@pt.lu&quot;. Each email falsely claims that it was issued by the Registrar of the District Court, and has a fraudulent summons attached, purportedly issued by the Judiciary.<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; The Judiciary solemnly points out that a summons issued by the Judiciary is served either by post or by hand. The Judiciary does not serve summonses by electronic means. The suspicious emails and summonses were not issued by the Judiciary, and the case has been reported to the Police.<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Anyone who has provided information to the email senders should contact the Police immediately.]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[Health risk category for Air Quality Health Index reached "Serious" level]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/13/P2026101300514.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/13/P2026101300514.htm</link><pubDate>Tue, 13 Oct 2026 17:54:21 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;The Environmental Protection Department (EPD) has recorded higher than normal air pollution levels since this afternoon (October 13), and the health risk category of the Air Quality Health Index (AQHI) at some air quality monitoring stations has reached the &quot;Serious&quot; level.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;Winds are weak over Hong Kong this afternoon, which are unfavourable for pollutant dispersion. The intense sunshine enhances photochemical smog activity, leading to the rapid formation of ozone and fine particulates in the Pearl River Delta region. The high ozone level also promotes the formation of nitrogen dioxide.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;According to the Hong Kong Observatory, a fresh easterly airstream&nbsp;is expected to reach the coast of Guangdong later tomorrow (October 14). It is expected that air quality in Hong Kong will improve by then.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;With a health risk category in the &quot;Very High&quot; range or above, children, the elderly and persons with existing heart or respiratory illnesses are advised to reduce physical exertion and outdoor activities to a minimum or avoid such activities. The general public is advised to reduce, or reduce to a minimum, outdoor physical exertion, and to reduce time outdoors, especially in areas with heavy traffic. As health effects of air pollutants may vary for individuals, persons who are in doubt or experience discomfort should seek advice from healthcare professionals. The public may visit the Centre for Health Protection&#39;s website (<a href="https://www.chp.gov.hk/en/content/9/460/3557.html" target="_blank">www.chp.gov.hk/en/content/9/460/3557.html</a>) for more information on health effects of air pollution and relevant health advice.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;The Education Bureau (EDB) urges all schools to visit the EDB website for appropriate measures to safeguard students&#39; health and take note of changes in the index. The relevant hyperlink is as follows:&nbsp;<a href="https://www.edb.gov.hk/en/sch-admin/admin/about-activities/sch-activities-guidelines/index.html" target="_blank">www.edb.gov.hk/en/sch-admin/admin/about-activities/sch-activities-guidelines/index.html</a>.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;When the air quality health risk category is at the &quot;Very High&quot; level, employers of outdoor workers performing heavy manual work are advised to assess the risk of outdoor work and take appropriate preventive measures to protect the health of their employees, such as reducing outdoor physical exertion and the time of their stay outdoors, especially in areas with heavy traffic. At the &quot;Serious&quot; level, employers of all outdoor workers are advised to assess the risk of outdoor work and take appropriate preventive measures to protect the health of their employees.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;Members of the public can check the current AQHI readings on the EPD&#39;s website (<a href="https://www.aqhi.gov.hk/en/index.html" target="_blank">www.aqhi.gov.hk/en.html</a>).]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[Hong Kong Customs detects duty-not-paid manufactured tobacco smuggling case at Shenzhen Bay Control Point (with photos)]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/13/P2026101300480.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/13/P2026101300480.htm</link><pubDate>Tue, 13 Oct 2026 17:05:00 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Hong Kong Customs detected a duty-not-paid manufactured tobacco smuggling case at the Shenzhen Bay Control Point yesterday (October 12). A total of about 8 000 kilograms of duty-not-paid manufactured tobacco, with an estimated market value of about $53.4 million and a duty potential of about $32 million, was seized from an inbound medium goods vehicle (MGV). A male local driver involved in the case was arrested.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Based on intelligence analysis, Customs officers intercepted the MGV, declared as carrying wood-plastic flooring in the above-mentioned Control Point. Upon examination, Customs officers did not find any declared goods but about 300 boxes of unmanifested and duty-not-paid manufactured tobacco in the MGV, and immediately arrested the 51-year-old local male driver.&nbsp;<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The case is still under investigation. Customs will continue to trace the source and flow of the manufactured tobacco. The likelihood of further arrests is not ruled out.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Customs will continue its risk assessment and intelligence analysis to vigorously combat cross-border smuggling activities.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Customs stresses that smuggling is a serious offence. Under the Import and Export Ordinance (Cap. 60), any person found guilty of importing or exporting unmanifested cargo is liable to a maximum fine of $2 million and imprisonment for seven years. Moreover, under the Dutiable Commodities Ordinance (Cap. 109), any person who deals with, possesses, sells or buys illicit cigarettes commits an offence. The maximum penalty upon conviction is a fine of $2 million and imprisonment for seven years.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Members of the public may report any suspected illicit cigarette and smuggling activities to Customs&#39; 24-hour hotline 182 8080 or its dedicated crime-reporting email account&nbsp;(<a href="mailto:crimereport@customs.gov.hk" target="_blank">crimereport@customs.gov.hk</a>) or online form (<a href="https://eform.cefs.gov.hk/form/ced002/en/" target="_blank">eform.cefs.gov.hk/form/ced002</a>).<div id="photos"><a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/13/P2026101300480_photo_1340975.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/13/P2026101300480_photo_1340975.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;<a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/13/P2026101300480_photo_1340976.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/13/P2026101300480_photo_1340976.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;</div>]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[Exchange Fund Bills Tender Results]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/13/P2026101300362.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/13/P2026101300362.htm</link><pubDate>Tue, 13 Oct 2026 16:55:00 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[The following is issued on behalf of the Hong Kong Monetary Authority:<br />
