<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The commendation ceremony was officiated by the Chief Executive, Mr John Lee. In his speech, he expressed heartfelt gratitude to the Central Authorities for their regard and cordial care for the growth of Hong Kong young people and patriotic education. He said that the Delegation was honoured to be received by Member of the Standing Committee of the Political Bureau of the Communist Party of China Central Committee and Vice Premier of the State Council Mr Ding Xuexiang, which was a great encouragement to Hong Kong students. He hoped that students would bear in mind Vice Premier Ding&#39;s exhortations and turn this rare experience into lasting motivation for their future endeavours.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Mr Lee encouraged students to revisit the history of the Red Army&#39;s Long March and deepen their sense of patriotism through visiting the thematic exhibition. He said, &quot;The national flag is the symbol of the country. It represents national sovereignty, dignity and unity, and carries the Chinese nation&#39;s course of struggle and common ideals. To salute the national flag is to pay tribute to our country, and also to the Red Army soldiers who did not fear sacrifice, embraced national ideals and continuously strove for self-improvement.&quot; He urged students to promote the spirit of the Long March through practical actions in the future, take the Long March of the new era well, and become builders and successors of the &quot;one country, two systems&quot; cause.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The &quot;Salute to the National Flag&quot; Hong Kong and Macao Youth Flag-Raising Teams&#39; Beijing Visit Delegation is a high-level key youth exchange programme. It conducted its thematic activity in Beijing from September 18 to 22. About 1 200 flag-raising team representatives from tertiary institutions, secondary and primary schools, teachers, students and youth group representatives from Hong Kong and Macao took part. The visit aimed to enable participating students to witness in person the solemn flag-raising ceremony at Tiananmen Square and experience the historical culture of the capital and the country&#39;s development achievements, deepen their sense of national identity and patriotism, and root the spirit and sentiment of patriotism in the hearts of young people.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;At today&#39;s ceremony, a number of school representatives were commended to encourage them to combine their first-hand experience in Beijing with what they had learned in class and to transform their patriotic feelings into a resolve to contribute to the country. The school representatives also reported their learning outcomes during the Beijing visit to the guests.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Guests attending today&#39;s ceremony included Deputy Director of the Liaison Office of the Central People&#39;s Government in the HKSAR Mr Sun Shangwu; the Secretary for Education, Dr Choi Yuk-lin; the Secretary for Constitutional and Mainland Affairs, Miss Janice Tse Siu-wa; the Permanent Secretary for Education, Ms May Chan; the Under Secretary for Education, Dr Sze Chun-fai; the Convenor of the Working Group on Patriotic Education, Dr Eliza Chan; and other guests, as well as over 230 principals, teachers and students from tertiary institutions, secondary and primary schools.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;After the ceremony, the students visited the thematic exhibition. By learning about the great journey of 90 years ago, which traversed thousands of mountains and rivers and overcame countless obstacles, they experienced the spirit of firm conviction, fearlessness of sacrifice, close unity and arduous struggle demonstrated during the Long March, thereby nurturing their sense of patriotism, developing proper values and aspiring to contribute to the country.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The thematic exhibition &quot;Unswerving Original Aspiration; Steadfast Conviction: The 90th Anniversary of the Victory of the Red Army&#39;s Long March&quot; is organised by the Education Bureau and co-organised by the Patriotic Education Centre. It features exhibits including interactive installations and oil paintings. The Centre also provides schools with guided tours, talks and learning activities. The exhibition is on display at the Patriotic Education Centre from today onwards for primary and secondary students across Hong Kong. Schools interested in visiting may apply through the Patriotic Education Centre. For details, please visit the Centre&#39;s website (<a href="https://www.hkpec.hk" target="_blank">www.hkpec.hk</a>).<div id="photos"><a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/13/P2026101300445_photo_1340974.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/13/P2026101300445_photo_1340974.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;<a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/13/P2026101300445_photo_1340977.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/13/P2026101300445_photo_1340977.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;<a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/13/P2026101300445_photo_1340978.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/13/P2026101300445_photo_1340978.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;<a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/13/P2026101300445_photo_1340979.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/13/P2026101300445_photo_1340979.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;<a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/13/P2026101300445_photo_1340980.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/13/P2026101300445_photo_1340980.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;<a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/13/P2026101300445_photo_1340982.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/13/P2026101300445_photo_1340982.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;<a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/13/P2026101300445_photo_1340983.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/13/P2026101300445_photo_1340983.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;<a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/13/P2026101300445_photo_1340984.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/13/P2026101300445_photo_1340984.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;</div>]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[InvestHK welcomes Honor's continued expansion in Hong Kong, adding momentum to local I&T ecosystem (with photo)]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/13/P2026101200550.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/13/P2026101200550.htm</link><pubDate>Tue, 13 Oct 2026 16:42:12 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;​Invest Hong Kong (InvestHK) today (October 13) welcomed the opening of Honor&#39;s seventh experience store in Hong Kong, expanding its local footprint toward a 10-store target in 2026. The Acting Director-General of Investment Promotion, Mr Arnold Lau, and the General Manager of Honor Device Hong Kong and Macao, Mr Tang Kei Tang, officiated at the ribbon-cutting ceremony for the new store today.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Mr Lau said, &quot;We are pleased to witness Honor further expand its retail network in Hong Kong. The new store reflects the company&#39;s confidence in the local market and offers consumers greater access to innovative technology products. Hong Kong&#39;s First Five-Year Plan and the 2026 Policy Address set out directions for promoting innovation and technology (I&amp;T), as well as AI development, creating a favourable environment for technology enterprises. InvestHK is committed to building Hong Kong into an international I&amp;T hub, and working with the industry to foster a robust, reliable, global, and sustainable AI ecosystem.&quot;<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Mr Tang said, &quot;AI is a strategic focus for Honor, and we will continue to develop a full-scenario smart device ecosystem. As demand among Hong Kong consumers for premium smart devices and AI experiences continues to rise, Hong Kong serves not only as an important platform for Honor to showcase innovative technologies, but also a key indicator for the brand to gain insight into the high-end market and consumer experience needs. We are confident about Honor&#39;s prospects in Hong Kong, and through the new store we look forward to bringing local consumers a more comprehensive and convenient smart living experience.&quot;<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Since its entry into Hong Kong in 2022, Honor&#39;s local business strategy has evolved from product introduction and channel development to a full-scenario ecosystem centred on AI innovation. Its products now cover smartphones, tablets, laptops, wearable devices, and Artificial Intelligence of Things&nbsp;(AIoT) ecosystem products. Building on its existing network of self-operated experience stores, Honor continues to expand its scale, further strengthening consumer interaction and after-sales services.<div id="photos"><a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/13/P2026101200550_photo_1340942.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/13/P2026101200550_photo_1340942.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;</div>]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[Hong Kong Customs raids suspected illicit cigarette storage site in Kwun Tong (with photos)]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/13/P2026101300430.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/13/P2026101300430.htm</link><pubDate>Tue, 13 Oct 2026 16:35:00 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Hong Kong Customs raided a suspected illicit cigarette storage site in Kwun Tong yesterday (October 12). A total of about 430 000 suspected illicit cigarettes were seized. The estimated market value was about $1.94 million, while the duty potential was about $1.42 million. A local woman involved in the case was arrested.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;During an anti-illicit cigarette operation in Kwun Tong yesterday afternoon, Customs officers intercepted a suspicious woman on Yee On Street, and found 1 200 suspected illicit cigarettes from her backpack upon search. Customs officers immediately arrested the 63-year-old local woman, who claimed to be a cleaner, and escorted her to her residential address in a tenement building nearby for search. About 430 000 suspected illicit cigarettes were further seized.&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The case is still under investigation. The arrested woman has been charged and the case will be brought up at the Kwun Tong Magistrates&#39; Court tomorrow (October 14). Customs will continue to trace the source of the illicit cigarettes. The likelihood of further arrests is not ruled out.<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Customs will continue its risk assessment and intelligence analysis for interception at source, as well as through its multipronged enforcement strategy targeting storage, distribution and peddling to spare no effort in combating illicit cigarette activities.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Customs stresses that it is an offence to buy or sell illicit cigarettes. Under the Dutiable Commodities Ordinance (Cap. 109), any person who deals with, possesses, sells or buys illicit cigarettes commits an offence. The maximum penalty upon conviction is a fine of $2 million and imprisonment for seven years.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Members of the public are urged to report any suspected illicit cigarette activities to Customs&#39; 24-hour hotline 182 8080, its dedicated crime-reporting email account&nbsp;(<a href="mailto:crimereport@customs.gov.hk" target="_blank">crimereport@customs.gov.hk</a>) or online form (<a href="https://eform.cefs.gov.hk/form/ced002/en/" target="_blank">eform.cefs.gov.hk/form/ced002</a>).<div id="photos"><a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/13/P2026101300430_photo_1340972.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/13/P2026101300430_photo_1340972.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;<a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/13/P2026101300430_photo_1340973.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/13/P2026101300430_photo_1340973.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;</div>]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[Free TV licences of HKTVE, TVB and i-CABLE HOY renewed]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/13/P2026101300301.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/13/P2026101300301.htm</link><pubDate>Tue, 13 Oct 2026 16:30:00 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;The Government today (October 13) announced that the Chief Executive (CE) in Council has approved the applications for renewal of the domestic free television programme service (free TV) licences of HK Television Entertainment Company Limited (HKTVE), TVB Limited (TVB) and i-CABLE HOY Limited (i-CABLE HOY) for a period of 12 years, as well as various relaxation measures and enhancement requirements, with a view to fostering the sustainable development of the local broadcasting industry and providing more diversified and quality television programmes for the public.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;The CE in Council has accepted the recommendations of the Communications Authority (CA), which comprehensively considered licensees&#39; performance and proposals, public views as well as the latest developments in the broadcasting industry. The CE in Council agreed to renew the free TV licences of the three licensees for another 12 years, accepted the investment proposals for the first six years of the renewed licences, and supported the introduction of various relaxation measures (including allowing more flexibility for the licensees to broadcast non-designated language programmes for both integrated Chinese and English channels, and reducing the broadcast hours for positive programmes), as well as enhancement requirements (including increasing local production and broadcast of English programmes on telling good stories of Hong Kong and national education-related programmes, enhancing subtitling requirements, and adding a cap on simulcast content). The relevant regulatory updates, as set out in the Annex, will be implemented by way of licence conditions and/or directions by the CA for the renewed licences. Meanwhile, the CA has implemented a series of measures for simplifying administrative requirements and streamlining the complaint handling procedures to reduce the compliance costs of the licensees.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;A spokesman for the Commerce and Economic Development Bureau said, &quot;Hong Kong is an international metropolis as well as a broadcasting hub in the Asia-Pacific region. In line with the directions of the National 15th Five-Year Plan, the First Five-Year Plan for Economic and Social Development of the Hong Kong Special Administrative Region (2026-2030) has set out that the Government will strive to strengthen the global reach of the mainstream media. The CE in Council&#39;s decision of renewing the free TV licences, along with the timely regulatory updates, has demonstrated the Government&#39;s commitment to cultivating a more favourable business environment for our broadcasting industry to thrive in, capitalise on the strength of the &#39;Hong Kong brand&#39;, and reinforce Hong Kong&#39;s position as Asia&#39;s world city. On the whole, the CA&#39;s recommendations on the relaxation measures and enhancement requirements are well-balanced between addressing changes brought by the evolving broadcasting landscape and meeting public expectations.&quot;<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;The Chief Executive&#39;s 2026 Policy Address announced that the Government will remove barriers and ease restrictions for the broadcasting industry and ensure that the regulatory regime can keep pace with the times. With licensees&#39; suggestions and public support reflected in the opinion surveys to suitably relax advertising controls, the CA has commenced a review of the Generic Codes of Practice for Television (CoPs), and will soon conduct a public consultation on the proposed amendments to the CoPs before the end of this year. The target is to implement the amended CoPs in mid-2027. On the premise of safeguarding public interest, the direction of the review will be adopting a principle-based regulatory model, with the necessary removal of outdated and prescriptive requirements.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;Renewal of free TV licences is governed by section 11 of the Broadcasting Ordinance (Cap. 562). The CA shall, not later than 12 months before the expiry of the period of validity of the licence, submit recommendations to the CE in Council in relation to the renewal of the licence, and where appropriate, the conditions subject to which the licence may be renewed. The validity periods of the renewed licences of HKTVE, TVB and i-CABLE HOY are from April 1, 2027, to March 31, 2039; December 1, 2027, to November 30, 2039; and May 31, 2028, to May 30, 2040, respectively. The three licensees have respectively committed to investing $2,298 million for the six-year period from 2027 to 2033 (HKTVE), $6,230 million for the six-year period from 2028 to 2033 (TVB), and $950 million for the six-year period from 2028 to 2034 (i-CABLE HOY).]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[Effective Exchange Rate Index]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/13/P2026101300194.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/13/P2026101300194.htm</link><pubDate>Tue, 13 Oct 2026 16:00:00 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The effective exchange rate index for the Hong Kong dollar on Tuesday, October 13, 2026 is 100.4 (same as yesterday&#39;s index).]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[Inspection of aquatic products imported from Japan]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/13/P2026101300412.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/13/P2026101300412.htm</link><pubDate>Tue, 13 Oct 2026 15:43:00 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;In response to the Japanese Government&#39;s plan to discharge nuclear-contaminated water at the Fukushima Nuclear Power Station, the Director of Food and Environmental Hygiene issued a Food Safety Order which prohibits all aquatic products, sea salt and seaweeds originating from the 10 metropolis/prefectures, namely Tokyo, Fukushima, Ibaraki, Miyagi, Chiba, Gunma, Tochigi, Niigata, Nagano and Saitama, from being imported into and supplied in Hong Kong.<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;For other Japanese aquatic products, sea salt and seaweeds that are not prohibited from being imported into Hong Kong, the Centre for Food Safety (CFS) of the Food and Environmental Hygiene Department is conducting comprehensive radiological tests to verify that the radiation levels of these products do not exceed the guideline levels before they are allowed to be supplied in the market.<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;From noon on October 12 to noon today (October 13), the CFS conducted tests on the radiological levels of 115 food samples imported from Japan, which were of the &quot;aquatic and related products, seaweeds and sea salt&quot; category. No sample was found to have exceeded the safety limit. Details can be found on the CFS&#39;s thematic website titled &quot;Control Measures on Foods Imported from Japan&quot; (<a href="https://www.cfs.gov.hk/english/programme/programme_rafs/programme_rafs_fc_01_30_Nuclear_Event_and_Food_Safety.html" target="_blank">www.cfs.gov.hk/english/programme/programme_rafs/programme_rafs_fc_01_30_Nuclear_Event_and_Food_Safety.html</a>).<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;In parallel, the Agriculture, Fisheries and Conservation Department (AFCD) has also tested 50 samples of local catch for radiological levels. All the samples passed the tests. Details can be found on the AFCD&#39;s website (<a href="https://www.afcd.gov.hk/english/fisheries/Radiological_testing/Radiological_Test.html" target="_blank">www.afcd.gov.hk/english/fisheries/Radiological_testing/Radiological_Test.html</a>).<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;The Hong Kong Observatory (HKO) has also enhanced the environmental monitoring of the local waters. No anomaly has been detected so far. For details, please refer to the HKO&#39;s website (<a href="https://www.hko.gov.hk/en/radiation/monitoring/seawater.html" target="_blank">www.hko.gov.hk/en/radiation/monitoring/seawater.html</a>).<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;From August 24, 2023, to noon today, the CFS and the AFCD have conducted tests on the radiological levels of 267 271 samples of food imported from Japan (including 166 387 samples of aquatic and related products, seaweeds and sea salt) and 56 960 samples of local catch respectively. All the samples passed the tests.]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[Christmas air mail - latest dates of posting 2026]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/13/P2026101300396.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/13/P2026101300396.htm</link><pubDate>Tue, 13 Oct 2026 15:40:00 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Hongkong Post today (October 13) announced the latest air mail posting dates for Christmas this year. While the dates are provisional, they have been compiled with due consideration of the requirements of respective postal administrations, and are for reference only. These dates and services are subject to availability of flights, and may be altered at short notice. The public are advised to post earlier than the dates shown. Members of the public may visit the web page at (<a href="https://www.hongkongpost.hk/en/about_us/whats_new/index.html" target="_blank">www.hongkongpost.hk/en/about_us/whats_new/index.html</a>) on the service availability for various destinations before posting.<br />
