]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[Transport and Logistics Bureau highly concerned about Interim Statement issued by Air Accident Investigation Authority on Boeing 747-481 BDSF freighter accident]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/13/P2026101300254.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/13/P2026101300254.htm</link><pubDate>Tue, 13 Oct 2026 15:37:00 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The Air Accident Investigation Authority (AAIA) today (October 13) published Interim Statement ITR-2026-09 on an accident that occurred on October 20, 2025, involving a Boeing 747-481 BDSF freighter aircraft (registration mark TC-ACF) operated by ACT Airlines (on behalf of Emirates SkyCargo), which veered off the runway at Hong Kong International Airport, resulting in the deaths of two airport security personnel inside a ground airport security vehicle parked outside the runway area.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Since the accident, the Government has attached great importance to the follow-up work in relation to the case. The Interim Statement is an interim report on the progress of the investigation, issued for anniversary updates in accordance with the requirement of the International Civil Aviation Organization. Its purpose is to provide the aviation industry and the public with timely information on the progress of the investigation. The final investigation report will be published upon completion of the investigation, and will set out a comprehensive account of the accident, its causes and recommendations for enhancing aviation safety.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The technical analysis for this accident is complex, some of the work must be carried out overseas by the relevant authorities. The AAIA is also required to undertake extensive co-ordination with the accredited representatives and their technical advisors of parties including the National Transportation Safety Board of the United States, Boeing, and aircraft component manufacturers, and to verify and confirm the information concerned. These factors will affect the investigation timeline. The Transport and Logistics Bureau (TLB) has requested the AAIA to press ahead with the investigation on the premise of ensuring its rigour and professionalism, with a view to completing it as soon as possible under the target of concluding all investigation work by the end of 2026.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The AAIA will continue to conduct an independent and in-depth professional investigation in strict accordance with international standards, with the aim of establishing the truth of the accident to prevent the recurrence of similar incidents. Should any evidence of suspected unlawful acts come to light in the course of the investigation, it will be referred to the relevant law enforcement agencies for follow-up without delay.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;A spokesman for the TLB said, &quot;We once again extend our deepest condolences on the deaths of the two airport security personnel in this accident, and offer our sincere sympathies to their families. Since the accident, the Government has maintained close contact with the families of the deceased and, through cross-departmental co-ordination, has provided them with comprehensive and appropriate support, including housing and other related assistance.&quot;<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;In addition, as the freighter concerned is registered in T&uuml;rkiye, the TLB has maintained close communication with the Turkish Consulate General in Hong Kong, exchanging information on the follow-up matters to the accident and co-ordination of the investigation. The Turkish side has also been fully co-operative and supportive throughout. The TLB will continue to render full support to the investigation work, ensure the full implementation of the relevant safety recommendations, and further strengthen the flight and ground safety standards at Hong Kong International Airport.]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[Air Accident Investigation Authority publishes Interim Statement on Boeing 747-481 BDSF freighter accident investigation]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/13/P2026101300246.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/13/P2026101300246.htm</link><pubDate>Tue, 13 Oct 2026 15:35:00 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The Air Accident Investigation Authority (AAIA) today (October 13) published Interim Statement ITR-2026-09 on an accident that occurred on October 20, 2025, involving a Boeing 747-481 BDSF freighter aircraft (registration mark TC-ACF) operated by ACT Airlines (on behalf of Emirates SkyCargo), which veered off the runway at Hong Kong International Airport, resulting in the deaths of two airport security personnel inside a ground airport security vehicle parked outside the runway area.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The investigation team has completed key investigative actions, including wreckage recovery and the collection of relevant data and records such as from the airline, meteorological services, air traffic control and the airport. The relevant aircraft components (including the on-board recording systems) have been sent to the state of manufacture for examination. The AAIA has also arranged for its investigators to travel overseas to oversee the key examinations on-site. One of the key focuses of the investigation is the forward movement of the No. 4 engine thrust lever, which caused the aircraft to turn left during the landing roll and veer off the runway. To date, technical examinations of the relevant aircraft components have not revealed any mechanical failure related to the accident.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The investigation is still in progress. It includes a specialist sound spectrum analysis to determine whether the forward movement of the No. 4 engine thrust lever was driven mechanically or caused by external forces. The investigation team will also arrange flight simulation trials to reconstruct the circumstances of the runway excursion on the day of the accident, and will review the accident history of aircraft of the same type.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;A spokesperson for the AAIA said that the investigation involves complex technical workflows and requires verification of information with various professional parties. Immediately after the accident, the AAIA inspected and surveyed the accident site; interviewed the crew of the aircraft concerned; collected evidence including flight documents, flight data and cockpit voice recordings; and arranged for the early salvage of the aircraft wreckage and the recovery of the flight data recorder and other important aircraft components. The AAIA published the Preliminary Report and Public Notice PLR-2025-04 on November 18, 2025. The AAIA has proactively expedited the investigation, which is expected to be completed by the end of 2026. The final investigation report will be published after all investigation work and statutory review procedures have been completed. The Interim Statement is published to provide the aviation industry and the public with timely updates on the progress of the investigation. The Interim Statement can be downloaded from the AAIA website (<a href="https://www.tlb.gov.hk/aaia/eng/investigation_reports/index.html" target="_blank">www.tlb.gov.hk/aaia/eng/investigation_reports/index.html</a>).<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The AAIA is an independent investigation body established under the Transport and Logistics Bureau, responsible for investigating civil aviation accidents and incidents under the Hong Kong Civil Aviation (Investigation of Accidents) Regulations (Cap. 448B) and with reference to International Civil Aviation Organization standards. The purpose of an AAIA investigation is to identify the causes of an accident (including a review of the human factors involved) and to make safety recommendations to enhance the safety of aircraft passengers and prevent the recurrence of accidents.]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[Ombudsman probes effectiveness of Leisure and Cultural Services Department's management of indoor fitness rooms (with photos)]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/13/P2026101300364.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/13/P2026101300364.htm</link><pubDate>Tue, 13 Oct 2026 15:30:00 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[The following is issued on behalf of the Office of The Ombudsman:<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The Ombudsman, Mr Jack Chan, today (October 13) announced the launch of a direct investigation operation to examine the effectiveness of the Leisure and Cultural Services Department (LCSD)&#39;s management of indoor fitness rooms.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The National 15th Five-Year Plan explicitly proposes to actively develop mass sports, extensively promote fitness activities for all and strengthen participation in fitness activities among young people, with a target of raising the proportion of the population regularly participating in physical exercise to approximately 40 per cent. The country will enhance the national fitness institutional system, and step up the planning and construction of venues and facilities in local communities. Hong Kong&#39;s First Five-Year Plan also proposes to drive wider participation in sports, and promote sports in the community with the goal of enhancing the overall health of the public.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Currently, the LCSD operates a total of 87 indoor fitness rooms, generally located within sports centres across various districts. However, except for holders of relevant qualifications recognised by the Physical Fitness Association of Hong Kong, China, members of the public who wish to register as a user of LCSD fitness rooms and hire fitness equipment must first attend a three-hour &quot;Briefing on Proper Ways to Use Fitness Equipment&quot; (Briefing Session) organised by the LCSD and then pass a written test, or complete its 12-hour &quot;Fitness (Multi-gym) Training Course&quot; (Training Course) with an attendance rate of at least 80 per cent. Between 2023 and 2025, the number of registered users of LCSD fitness rooms increased by 56 per cent from around 151 000 to 236 000, while the total number of user visits to fitness rooms per year increased by 50 per cent from around 4.852 million to 7.262 million.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;A preliminary inquiry by the Office of The Ombudsman revealed a severe shortage of places for the Briefing Sessions and Training Courses organised by the LCSD. In 2025, the LCSD held around 1 000 Briefing Sessions throughout the year with a total of 25 000 places, but the total number of applications in the year was as many as 377 000. In the same year, the LCSD held around 2 500 Training Courses with a total of 58 500 places, but the total number of applications in the year reached 257 000. Unsuccessful applicants who cannot attend the Briefing Session or Training Course are not eligible to use the LCSD&#39;s fitness room facilities.&nbsp;<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Mr Chan said, &quot;Public recreation and sports facilities are of utmost importance to promoting participation in sports in the community. The high volume of applications for the LCSD&#39;s Briefing Sessions and Training Courses reflects keen public demand for use of fitness rooms. While the LCSD requires members of the public to either possess relevant qualifications or complete its fitness training before they can use its fitness rooms, no similar requirements are in place for other risky sports such as swimming. Furthermore, the supply of such training falls far short of demand. All these may be the biggest barrier for wider participation in the fitness sport and should be addressed and removed. The Office considers it necessary to review whether the qualification requirement for users still serves any practical need and whether a similar requirement is in place for public fitness rooms in the country and elsewhere.&quot;<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Meanwhile, there are two types of hire charges for LCSD fitness rooms, i.e. an hourly ticket and a monthly ticket, the latter entitling the holder to unlimited use. In view of the higher demand by monthly ticket holders, most fitness rooms allocate a larger share of the quota to them. Starting from November 1, 2024, monthly ticket holders have been required to book fitness room facilities via SmartPLAY. In response to their calls for greater flexibility, the LCSD currently allows monthly ticket holders to cancel the booking via SmartPLAY up to one hour before the start of the booked session. The Office has received views that other members of the public can hardly proceed to the fitness room at such short notice, leading to wastage of the vacated place.&nbsp;<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The Office also notes that the LCSD does not impose any penalties on monthly ticket holders for failing to take up a booked session. A monthly ticket holder who has made a booking but is absent need not take responsibility at all. Moreover, there is no cap on the number of sessions in fitness rooms that monthly ticket holders can book each day. Their actual use of the facilities is significantly less than the booked period. As a result, even though bookings are full, the actual number of users in the fitness rooms falls short of the number of hirers.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Mr Chan said, &quot;LCSD fitness rooms are publicly funded and affordable. The facilities are scarce public resources and should be reasonably used. An appropriate balance should be struck between convenient booking and efficient use of limited public resources.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&quot;With the promotion of fitness for all elevated to a national strategic level, the LCSD should proactively align with national development strategies by enhancing the mechanism for use of fitness rooms to foster broader participation in sports. In this light, I have decided to launch a direct investigation operation to examine the LCSD&rsquo;s management of fitness rooms, including the mandatory training requirement prior to use of fitness rooms, the booking mechanism, the utilisation rate of fitness rooms, the LCSD&rsquo;s monitoring measures and maintenance and repair of fitness equipment. Where necessary, pertinent recommendations will be made for improvement.&quot;<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The Ombudsman welcomes views from members of the public on this topic. Written submissions should reach the Office of The Ombudsman by November 13:<br />
<br />
Address: 30/F, China Merchants Tower, Shun Tak Centre<br />
168&ndash;200 Connaught Road Central, Hong Kong<br />
Fax: 2882 8149<br />
Email: di496@ombudsman.hk<div id="photos"><a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/13/P2026101300364_photo_1340969.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/13/P2026101300364_photo_1340969.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;<a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/13/P2026101300364_photo_1340970.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/13/P2026101300364_photo_1340970.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;</div>]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[Incoming passenger convicted and jailed for importing duty-not-paid cigarettes and alternative smoking products (with photo)]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/13/P2026101300395.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/13/P2026101300395.htm</link><pubDate>Tue, 13 Oct 2026 15:25:00 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;An incoming male passenger was sentenced to seven months&#39; imprisonment by the West Kowloon Magistrates&#39; Courts today (October 13) for importing duty-not-paid cigarettes and importing alternative smoking products, in contravention of the Dutiable Commodities Ordinance (DCO) and the Import and Export Ordinance (IEO).<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Customs officers intercepted a 39-year-old incoming Mainland male passenger arriving in Hong Kong from Tokyo, Japan, via Taipei, at Hong Kong International Airport on August 16 and seized about 71 600 duty-not-paid cigarettes and 13 600 alternative smoking products, with an estimated market value of about $363,000 and a duty potential of about $236,000, from his personal baggage. The passenger was subsequently arrested.<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Customs welcomes the sentence. The custodial sentence has imposed a considerable deterrent effect and reflects the seriousness of the offences.<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Customs reminds members of the public that under the DCO, cigarettes are dutiable goods to which the DCO applies. Any person who imports, deals with, possesses, sells or buys illicit cigarettes commits an offence. The maximum penalty upon conviction is a fine of $2 million and imprisonment for seven years.<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Under the IEO, any person who imports an alternative smoking product into Hong Kong commits an offence. The maximum penalty upon conviction is a fine of $2 million and imprisonment for seven years.<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Members of the public may report any suspected illicit cigarette activities to Customs&#39; 24-hour hotline 182 8080 or its dedicated crime-reporting email account&nbsp;(<a href="mailto:crimereport@customs.gov.hk" target="_blank">crimereport@customs.gov.hk</a>) or online form (<a href="https://eform.cefs.gov.hk/form/ced002/en/" target="_blank">eform.cefs.gov.hk/form/ced002</a>).<div id="photos"><a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/13/P2026101300395_photo_1340971.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/13/P2026101300395_photo_1340971.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;</div>]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[Auctions for Chinese New Year fair stalls in New Territories to start next Tuesday]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/13/P2026101300187.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/13/P2026101300187.htm</link><pubDate>Tue, 13 Oct 2026 15:00:00 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The Food and Environmental Hygiene Department (FEHD) today (October 13) reminded interested parties to join auctions that will start next Tuesday (October 20) for stalls at the 2027 Chinese New Year (CNY) fairs in the New Territories.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The eight fairs, with a total of 539 wet goods stalls, 232 dry goods stalls and eight fast food stalls, will be held at Sha Tsui Road Playground in Tsuen Wan, Plover Cove Road Mini-soccer Pitch in Tai Po, Tung Tau Industrial Area Playground in Yuen Long, Tin Hau Temple Plaza in Tuen Mun, Po Hong Park in Tseung Kwan O, Shek Wu Hui Playground in North District, Kwai Chung Sports Ground in Kwai Tsing and Yuen Wo Playground in Sha Tin.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The auctions for stalls of the CNY fairs in Tsuen Wan and Yuen Long will be held at the Assembly Hall, 2/F, Lai Chi Kok Government Offices, on October 20 and 22 respectively. The auction sessions are scheduled from 9am to 12.30pm (AM session) and 2pm until completion of the auction (PM session).<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The auctions for stalls of the CNY fairs in Tai Po, Tuen Mun, Tseung Kwan O, North District, Kwai Tsing and Sha Tin will be held at the above-mentioned venue on October 21, 23, 26, 27, 28 and 29 respectively. The auction session is scheduled from 9am until completion of the auction.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Details of the auctions are as follows:<br />
<br />
Sha Tsui Road Playground (Tsuen Wan)<br />
------------------------------------------------<br />
Auction date: October 20 (Tuesday)<br />
Number of stalls: 126<br />
AM session: Wet goods (Stall Nos. 1 to 64)<br />
PM session: Wet goods (Stall Nos. 65 to 96) and dry goods<br />
Opening prices:<br />
Wet goods: $1,770<br />
Dry goods (regular size): $8,480<br />
Dry goods (large size): $12,720<br />
&nbsp;<br />
Plover Cove Road Mini-soccer Pitch (Tai Po)<br />
-------------------------------------------------------<br />
Auction date: October 21 (Wednesday)<br />
Number of stalls: 75<br />
Stall types: Fast food, wet goods and dry goods<br />
Opening prices:<br />
Fast food: $5,480<br />
Wet goods: $2,270<br />
Dry goods (regular size): $1,450<br />
Dry goods (large size): $2,180<br />
&nbsp;<br />
Tung Tau Industrial Area Playground (Yuen Long)<br />
------------------------------------------------------------<br />
Auction date: October 22 (Thursday)<br />
Number of stalls: 158<br />
AM session: Fast food and wet goods (Stall Nos. 57 to 72 and 77 to 136)<br />
PM session: Wet goods (Stall Nos. 137 to 156) and dry goods<br />
Opening prices:<br />
Fast food: $5,480<br />
Wet goods: $700<br />
Dry goods (regular size): $1,350<br />
Dry goods (large size): $2,030<br />
&nbsp;<br />
Tin Hau Temple Plaza (Tuen Mun)<br />
------------------------------------------<br />
Auction date: October 23 (Friday)<br />
Number of stalls: 62<br />
Stall types: Fast food, wet goods and dry goods<br />
Opening prices:<br />
Fast food: $2,740<br />
Wet goods: $1,490<br />
Dry goods: $2,140<br />
&nbsp;<br />
Po Hong Park (Tseung Kwan O)<br />
---------------------------------------<br />
Auction date: October 26 (Monday)<br />
Number of stalls: 93<br />
Stall types: Fast food, wet goods and dry goods<br />
Opening prices:<br />
Fast food: $4,570<br />
Wet goods: $830<br />
Dry goods (regular size): $1,540<br />
Dry goods (large size): $2,310<br />
&nbsp;<br />
Shek Wu Hui Playground (North District)<br />
--------------------------------------------------<br />
Auction date: October 27 (Tuesday)<br />
Number of stalls: 92<br />
Stall types: Wet goods and dry goods<br />
Opening prices:<br />
Wet goods: $710<br />
Dry goods (regular size): $1,540<br />
Dry goods (large size): $2,310<br />
&nbsp;<br />
Kwai Chung Sports Ground (Kwai Tsing)<br />
--------------------------------------------------<br />
Auction date: October 28 (Wednesday)<br />
Number of stalls: 98<br />
Stall types: Wet goods and dry goods<br />
Opening prices:<br />
Wet goods: $490<br />
Dry goods (regular size): $2,250<br />
Dry goods (large size): $3,380<br />
&nbsp;<br />
Yuen Wo Playground (Sha Tin)<br />
--------------------------------------<br />
Auction date: October 29 (Thursday)<br />
Number of stalls: 75<br />
Stall types: Wet goods and dry goods<br />
Opening prices:<br />
Wet goods: $3,580<br />
Dry goods (regular size): $4,570<br />
Dry goods (large size): $6,860<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Bidders for CNY fair stalls must be at least 18 years old and ordinarily reside in Hong Kong.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Anyone can bid for more than one stall. A bidder must pay the bid price and register in person with his/her own name as the licensee of the stall immediately after successfully bidding for a stall. The bidder is also required to sign at once a licence agreement with the FEHD; otherwise he/she will forfeit the rights to operate the stall.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;All fair sites will be made available to the licensees three days in advance of the fairs (from January 28 to 30, 2027) for the setting up of stalls. In the event of any unforeseeable incident that will cause a reduction of the whole licence period (including the duration for setting up stalls and the business period of the fair), the Government has the right to postpone the commencement date and shorten the duration of the period. The bidding price (licence fee) paid will be refunded to the successful bidder on a pro-rata basis without interest.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Stall licensees must completely remove the stall structure and all paraphernalia, together with all refuse, debris and unsold commodities (whether damaged or otherwise) from the licensed area before 7am on February 6, 2027.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The FEHD reminds licensees that the stalls are solely for the purpose of selling and promoting the sale of the permitted commodities, and no other activities are allowed in the licensed area. If the FEHD considers that any activity conducted by the licensee to publicise, promote, display, show or sell any permitted commodities in the venue is unlawful, contrary to the interest of national security, immoral or incompatible with the object of the CNY fair, the FEHD is entitled to direct the licensee to stop conducting such activity and the licensee must immediately comply with such direction.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Stall licensees should not destroy, damage or abandon any unsold commodities at or in the vicinity of the stall. They may surrender unsold flowers and plants left behind at the stall to the FEHD at no charge, cost or compensation whatsoever, before 7am on February 6, 2027.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;According to the licence agreement, except inside designated stalls, licensees must not keep, store or use any compressed helium cylinder in the licensed area. Whereas licensees of the designated stalls may keep, store or use helium cylinders in the licenced area, the quantity of helium should be such that a licence is not required pursuant to the Dangerous Goods (Application and Exemption) Regulation 2012 (Cap. 295E), i.e. equivalent to 150 litres of helium. Sales of floating LED glowing balloons and aquarium fish by stall licensees are prohibited at the CNY fairs.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;In addition, as stated in the licence agreement, the height of dry goods stalls must not exceed 3 metres from ground level, while the height of wet goods stalls and fast food stalls must not exceed 4.5m from ground level. For wet goods stalls and fast food stalls with a height of more than 3m from ground level, the licensee must, at his/her own costs, provide the FEHD with the original certificate issued by an authorised person, a registered structural engineer, or a competent person under the Construction Sites (Safety) Regulations (Cap. 59I) to certify the structural safety of the structure in the licensed area before the fair is opened to the public. The licensee must also affix a copy of the aforesaid certificate on the structure of the stall. Also, each corner and anchor point of the marquee (if any) shall be securely fastened using appropriate ballast weights, such as sandbags or purpose-designed marquee water weights. During the period when the Strong Monsoon Signal is in force or is to be issued, the licensee shall immediately inspect the stall and adopt reinforcement measures as necessary.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Successful bidders shall comply with all the stipulations and provisions as set out in the licence agreement. Otherwise, the department is entitled to terminate the agreement and the licensee shall immediately vacate the stall.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Details of the 2027 CNY fairs such as the public notice, the locations and layouts of the fair venues, commodities allowed for sale at the fair stalls, open auction arrangements and related rules, and a sample of the licence agreement are available on the FEHD website (<a href="https://www.fehd.gov.hk" target="_blank">www.fehd.gov.hk</a>). For enquiries, please call the FEHD hotline 2868 0000.]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[Flat selection for Sale of Home Ownership Scheme Flats 2025 to commence from October 21]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/13/P2026101200540.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/13/P2026101200540.htm</link><pubDate>Tue, 13 Oct 2026 15:00:00 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[The following is issued on behalf of the Hong Kong Housing Authority:<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Flat selection for the Sale of Home Ownership Scheme (HOS) Flats 2025 (HOS 2025) of the Hong Kong Housing Authority (HA) will commence from October 21. The HA will invite eligible applicants in batches by post or email to select flats for sale under HOS 2025 at the designated time at the Housing Authority Customer Service Centre (HACSC) in Lok Fu, according to the priority under the respective application category.<br />
&nbsp;<br />
Flat selection for HOS 2025 to commence soon enhancing the housing ladder&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&quot;The HA has long been striving to enhance the housing ladder. In order to assist Public Rental Housing (PRH) tenants with better financial capabilities and other eligible applicants to achieve upward mobility by purchasing subsidised sales flats (SSFs), the HA launches various types of SSF schemes and continuously enhances the relevant arrangements. The HA jointly launched HOS 2025, the Sale of Green Form Subsidised Home Ownership Scheme (GSH) Flats 2025 (GSH 2025) and the White Form Secondary Market Scheme 2025 with applications invited in April. The ballots for the three schemes were separately drawn on July 10. Flat selection for GSH 2025 was completed in mid-September, and all GSH flats offered have been sold to Green Form applicants. With flat selection for HOS 2025 will commence on October 21, eligible applicants may take this opportunity to fulfil their home ownership aspirations early,&quot; a spokesman for the HA said today (October 13).<br />
&nbsp;<br />
Nearly 7 000 flats in five projects with diverse locations and flat types<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Flats for sale under HOS 2025 include about 7 000 flats in five new HOS developments located in Kai Tak, Kam Tin, Tseung Kwan O, Ping Shan and Tung Chung respectively, with saleable areas ranging from about 26.1 square metres to about 52.0 sq m (about 281 square feet to about 560 sq ft) (see Annex 1). In addition, about 400 resale HOS flats will be offered for sale under HOS 2025.<br />
&nbsp;<br />
Affordable pricing to facilitate home ownership<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The HA continues to determine the selling price of HOS flats at an affordable level, helping members of the public to step onto the housing ladder and realise the dream of homeownership. Based on the principle of setting selling prices at an affordable level, the discount rate for HOS 2025 was set at 30 per cent from the assessed market level (i.e. for sale at 70 per cent of the assessed market values).<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The selling prices of the flats in the new HOS developments under HOS 2025 range from about $1.5 million to about $4.8 million. Assuming a flat selling price of $2.8 million (saleable area around 36.2 sq m or 390 sq ft) with a 90 per cent of the flat price for a mortgage repayment period of 30 years at a prevailing mortgage interest rate of about 3.5 per cent, the monthly mortgage payment is about $11,300.<br />
&nbsp;<br />
Priority flat selection for families with newborns or elderly and extra support for single applicants<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The order of priority for flat selection by eligible applicants will be determined by the application category, quota allocation and ballot results. Affected tenants of the HA&#39;s announced PRH clearance projects (i.e. Wah On House, Wah Lok House, Wah Cheong House, Wah Tai House and Wah Kin House of Wah Fu Estate; Pik Hoi House, Kam Pik House and Tan Fung House of Choi Hung Estate; Geranium House and Narcissus House of Ma Tau Wai Estate and Sai Wan Estate) who would like to purchase an SSF in lieu of PRH will be accorded priority in flat selection over other applicants under HOS 2025.<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The HA has set a quota of 2 800 new HOS flats (i.e. about 40 per cent of the number of new flats for sale) under HOS 2025 for eligible family applicants applying under the Priority Scheme for Families with Elderly Members and Families with Newborns Flat Selection Priority Scheme for priority flat selection. Separately, to provide one-person applicants with a reasonable opportunity to purchase flats, the HA has set a quota of 700 new HOS flats under HOS 2025 for one-person applicants (i.e. about 10 per cent of the number of new flats for sale). The HOS 2025 priority sequence of different application categories is set out in Annex 2.<br />
&nbsp;<br />
Diversified information and exhibitions for easy understanding of the developments<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&quot;Sales brochures and price lists for the HOS developments will be made available for public collection at the HACSC in Lok Fu and for viewing on the HA/Housing Department&#39;s designated website (<a href="https://www.housingauthority.gov.hk/hos/2025/en" target="_blank">www.housingauthority.gov.hk/hos/2025/en</a>) from tomorrow (October 14) until the end of the flat selection period for HOS 2025,&quot; the spokesman said.<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Project models of the new developments (see Annex 3) and doll houses of HOS typical flats, virtual videos of samples of HOS flats, virtual reality tours of the interiors of samples of HOS flats, exhibition panels/electronic panels and other information on HOS developments will be available for public viewing at the HACSC in Lok Fu and on the designated website.<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Applicants may call the 24-hour HA Sales Hotline at 2712 8000 for enquiries.]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[Hong Kong Scholarship for Excellence Scheme opens for applications]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/13/P2026101300146.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/13/P2026101300146.htm</link><pubDate>Tue, 13 Oct 2026 14:30:00 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The Education Bureau (EDB) today (October 13) announced that the Hong Kong Scholarship for Excellence Scheme (HKSES) will, from today until December 31, be open for applications from eligible Hong Kong students who intend to pursue undergraduate or postgraduate studies at world-renowned universities outside Hong Kong starting from the 2027/28 academic year.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;A spokesman for the EDB said, &quot;As a merit-based scheme targeting the most outstanding Hong Kong students, the HKSES seeks to cultivate a cadre of brilliant young achievers with a sense of social responsibility and national identity, an affection for Hong Kong and an international perspective, who will return home after receiving a world-class education supported by the scholarship to enrich Hong Kong&#39;s talent pool and competitiveness in the long run. Selection will be based on candidates&#39; academic achievements and other attributes such as leadership qualities and potential as well as their contributions and commitment to society. Awardees are required to complete their specific study programmes and undertake to return to Hong Kong upon graduation to work for at least two years or a period equivalent to the duration of the scholarship received, whichever is longer.&quot;<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Awardees will receive a non-means-tested scholarship to cover their tuition fees, subject to a ceiling of $300,000 per annum. In addition, the HKSES will provide additional support to financially needy awardees during their studies. Awardees who have passed the means test will receive a bursary of up to $200,000 per student per annum to cover their living and study-related expenses. The value of the award will be correspondingly reduced if an awardee also receives other awards, both locally and abroad, or assistance to finance his or her same studies overseas.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Apart from the monetary support, the value of the HKSES also lies in its prestige and recognition as well as other non-monetary benefits. Awardees will receive a series of support and mentorship services to be rendered by the Government and other sectors of the community.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;More details on the HKSES are available on the HKSES website (<a href="https://hkses.edb.gov.hk/en/index.html" target="_blank">hkses.edb.gov.hk</a>). Eligible students who intend to pursue their first year of undergraduate or postgraduate studies at world-renowned universities outside Hong Kong in the 2027/28 academic year (i.e. during the period from August 2027 to July 2028) may submit their applications for the scholarship online via the application system on the HKSES website on or before December 31, 2026, by 6pm. Late applications will not be accepted.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Shortlisted applicants will be invited by the EDB to attend interviews for the scholarship from April 2027 onwards after receiving unconditional offers from their chosen programmes. They will be notified of the results in due course after the interviews.]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[LCSD setting up Asian Games Focal Site to broadcast Asian Para Games]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/13/P2026101300241.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/13/P2026101300241.htm</link><pubDate>Tue, 13 Oct 2026 12:00:00 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;With the 5th Asian Para Games to be held from October 18 to 24 this year, the Leisure and Cultural Services Department (LCSD) will continue to set up the &quot;Asian Games Focal Site&quot; at designated sports centres across the 18 districts to broadcast Asian Para Games competitions. Members of the public are welcome to watch the competitions at the focal sites and cheer for the athletes of the country and Hong Kong, China.&nbsp;<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The focal sites will open on October 18 from 5pm to 8pm and thereafter daily from 10am to 6pm up to October 24. Details are available on the dedicated website (<a href="https://www.lcsd.gov.hk/en/agfs/index.html" target="_blank">www.lcsd.gov.hk/en/agfs/index.html</a>). Any adjustments to the opening hours of the focal sites will be announced on the dedicated website.&nbsp;<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;In addition, a series of videos on Asian Para Games events has been uploaded to the dedicated website, in which athletes or coaches will explain and demonstrate sports skills to increase public understanding of para sports.&nbsp;&nbsp;]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[Keynote address by PSITI at HKTDC Hong Kong Electronics Fair (Autumn Edition) 2026 Future Industries Zone Opening Ceremony (English only) (with photo)]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/13/P2026101300257.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/13/P2026101300257.htm</link><pubDate>Tue, 13 Oct 2026 11:28:00 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Following is the keynote address by the Permanent Secretary for Innovation, Technology and Industry, Mr Kevin Choi, at the HKTDC (Hong Kong Trade Development Council) Hong Kong Electronics Fair (Autumn Edition) 2026 Future Industries Zone Opening Ceremony today (October 13):<br />
<br />
Distinguished guests, ladies and gentlemen,<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Good morning. It is a pleasure to join you all at the opening of the Future Industries Zone at the Hong Kong Electronics Fair (Autumn Edition) 2026.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Electronics remains central to Hong Kong&#39;s economy. Electronic products accounted for close to 80 per cent of Hong Kong&#39;s total exports in the first eight months of this year. Yet the significance of the industry goes beyond trade figures. Electronics is the foundation of artificial intelligence, advanced computing, smart manufacturing, healthcare technologies and many of the industries that will shape our future.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; That is why the launch of the Future Industries Zone is timely. A new generation of technologies is creating new industries and transforming established ones. The challenge is no longer confined to making a scientific discovery. We must also turn that discovery into a product, validate it in a real setting, establish a viable production process and bring it to the market.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; This is the essence of new industrialisation, and a practical way to develop new quality productive forces. It connects technological innovation with industrial innovation, so that research can create real economic value. For hardware companies in particular, transforming a prototype into a commercial product involves design for manufacturability, rigorous testing, certification and supply chain management, demanding clear validation before meeting operational needs.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; The Future Industries Zone responds directly to these needs. It brings together future microelectronics, future computing, future marine and quantum technology, future manufacturing and future wellness. More importantly, it places researchers and technology developers alongside manufacturers, users, investors and industry partners. This can help close the gap from research and development to design for manufacturing and mass production, while strengthening the wider electronics supply-chain ecosystem.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; The direction of the Zone is closely aligned with national and Hong Kong development priorities. This year marks the beginning of the National 15th Five-Year Plan. The Plan calls for deeper integration between technological and industrial innovation, the development of a modern industrial system and the fostering of new quality productive forces according to local conditions. It also supports Hong Kong in developing into an international innovation and technology centre.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Hong Kong&#39;s First Five-Year Plan and this year&#39;s Policy Address translate these national priorities into concrete action for Hong Kong. Building on our strengths in basic research, international connections and professional services, and the Greater Bay Area (Guangdong-Hong Kong-Macao Greater Bay Area)&#39;s strong manufacturing capabilities and complete supply chains, Hong Kong is developing a new industrialisation system with three major innovation and technology parks and five research and development institutions as its backbone.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; A central task is to strengthen the path from research and development to testing, pilot production, industrial application and market development. Pilot production is particularly important. It allows companies to verify whether a technology can be manufactured reliably and at the required quality, and to resolve technical problems before moving to full-scale production.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; In microelectronics, the Hong Kong Microelectronics Research and Development Institute is establishing pilot lines for third-generation semiconductors. They will support research, proof-of-concept fabrication, pilot production and low-volume production. The Institute will also take the lead in operating the first national manufacturing innovation centre to be established in Hong Kong, bringing together resources from Hong Kong, the Chinese Mainland and overseas, including local enterprises and start-ups participating in the Zone today, to strengthen our microelectronics ecosystem and accelerate commercialisation.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Technology needs to be tested in real operating environments. This is another area where the Future Industries Zone can make a practical contribution. Through scenario-based displays, enterprises and start-ups can show how their hardware and software solutions work, engage prospective users and obtain direct feedback. These are not merely product showcases. They give companies a valuable opportunity to validate and improve their technologies with reference to real needs.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; This year&#39;s Policy Address also supports the Hong Kong Science and Technology Parks Corporation in exploring the establishment of the Hong Kong Innovation and Technology Application Scenario Promotion Centre. By helping technology companies identify opportunities for validation and adoption in public services and different industries, the proposed centre will enable more promising solutions to move from demonstration to wider use.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; The Top 100 Future Industries Pioneer List adds another important dimension. By identifying promising enterprises in the frontier fields featured at the Zone, it gives them greater visibility and creates opportunities to connect with technology partners, manufacturers and potential users. This complements the Government&#39;s efforts to support enterprises through various policy initiatives such as the $10 billion New Industrialisation Acceleration Scheme in establishing smart production facilities in Hong Kong.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; The Zone also provides a link to the international technology community. The debut of CES Asia Unveiled Hong Kong, made possible through the HKEIA (Hong Kong Electronic Industries Association)&#39;s partnership with the Consumer Technology Association, brings an internationally recognised technology platform to the Fair. The pitch competition offers high-potential start-ups an opportunity to present their technologies, while the winning company will receive complimentary exhibition space at CES 2027 Eureka Park.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; This international exposure is valuable, but it rests on the same foundation: strong technology, effective validation and the ability to turn an idea into a product. By combining these elements in one place, the Future Industries Zone can help companies progress further along the path from research to industrial application, and support the formation of future industry clusters in Hong Kong.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; The Government will continue to work with industry, universities and research institutions to strengthen that path. The HKEIA&#39;s collaboration with the Government and the industry demonstrates how the wider innovation and technology ecosystem can work together.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; I thank the&nbsp;HKEIA, HKTDC and all partners for creating this new platform. I look forward to seeing the Future Industries Zone foster practical collaboration, accelerate the commercialisation of promising technologies and contribute to Hong Kong&#39;s new industrialisation and technology development.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; I wish the Future Industries Zone and the Hong Kong Electronics Fair every success, and all of you a productive and rewarding event. Thank you.<div id="photos"><a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/13/P2026101300257_photo_1340959.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/13/P2026101300257_photo_1340959.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;</div>]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[Red flags hoisted at Shek O Beach and Big Wave Bay Beach]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/13/P2026101300217.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/13/P2026101300217.htm</link><pubDate>Tue, 13 Oct 2026 10:15:00 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[Attention TV/radio announcers:<br />
<br />
Please broadcast the following as soon as possible:<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;Here is an item of interest to swimmers.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;The Leisure and Cultural Services Department announced today (October 13) that due to big waves, red flags have been hoisted at Shek O Beach and Big Wave Bay Beach in Southern District, Hong Kong Island. Beachgoers are advised not to swim at these beaches.]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[Brussels ETO presents Hong Kong's first film programme at KINO Rotterdam (with photos)]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/12/P2026101200689.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/12/P2026101200689.htm</link><pubDate>Mon, 12 Oct 2026 22:58:09 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The Hong Kong Economic and Trade Office in Brussels (Brussels ETO) is presenting Hong Kong&#39;s first thematic film programme at KINO Rotterdam in the Netherlands from October 9, 2026, to January 8, 2027 (Rotterdam time), as a special highlight. The programme is supported by the Cultural and Creative Industries Development Agency (CCIDA) and the Film Development Fund under the Culture, Sports and Tourism Bureau of the Hong Kong Special Administrative Region Government.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;Entitled &quot;Hong Kong Stories: Lives in Transition&quot;, the programme captures the multiple facets of Hong Kong&#39;s city life through the stories of people across different generations in the 15 classic films spanning from the 1980s to the late 2010s, namely &quot;A Moment of Romance&quot;, &quot;A Simple Life&quot;, &quot;As Tears Go By&quot;, &quot;Autumn Moon&quot;, &quot;Chungking Express&quot;, &quot;Dangerous Encounters of the First Kind&quot;, &quot;Infernal Affairs&quot;, &quot;July Rhapsody&quot;, &quot;Mad World&quot;, &quot;Made in Hong Kong&quot;, &quot;My Heart Is That Eternal Rose&quot;, &quot;Nomad&quot;, &quot;Rouge&quot;, &quot;Spacked Out&quot; and &quot;Still Human&quot;. The programme brings together a stellar line-up of internationally acclaimed Hong Kong film stars, including legendary figures such as Andy Lau, Anita Mui, Leslie Cheung, Maggie Cheung and Tony Leung, whose celebrated performances and enduring charm have won the hearts of audiences around the world for decades.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;Addressing guests at the opening reception on October 9, Deputy Representative of the Brussels ETO Ms Venus Tsoi thanked KINO Rotterdam for the thoughtful selection of films and its efforts in promoting the programme, which has received enthusiastic feedback across different channels, including an overwhelming response on social media. The strong interest demonstrates the widespread appeal of Hong Kong cinema in Europe and Brussels ETO is committed to creating more platforms for European audiences to discover the creativity and beauty of Hong Kong cinema.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;Citing the Hong Kong-Europe-Asian Film Collaboration Funding Scheme as an example, Ms Tsoi said, &quot;The Hong Kong Government has been rolling out various initiatives to promote international collaboration in the film industry. CCIDA will set up the Support Unit for Non-local Film Productions to provide one-stop services for overseas film crews filming in Hong Kong. I encourage you to leverage these resources and Hong Kong&#39;s excellent support to realise your film projects in Hong Kong, and I look forward to seeing the beautiful cityscape of Hong Kong through your cameras and your creative eyes.&quot;<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;Quoting Hong Kong&#39;s close to 50 million visitor arrivals in 2025, Ms Tsoi also said that the Hong Kong Government is dedicated to enhancing visitors&#39; travel experiences, and that &quot;Film + Tourism&quot; will be a key highlight in promoting Hong Kong&#39; s rich film culture. She concluded by inviting European visitors to visit Hong Kong for more engaging and immersive experiences inspired by Hong Kong&#39;s rich cinematic heritage.<div id="photos"><a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/12/P2026101200689_photo_1340953.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/12/P2026101200689_photo_1340953.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;<a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/12/P2026101200689_photo_1340954.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/12/P2026101200689_photo_1340954.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;</div>]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[TD seriously follows up on tram derailment incident]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/12/P2026101200687.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/12/P2026101200687.htm</link><pubDate>Mon, 12 Oct 2026 22:28:23 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;​The Transport Department (TD) is deeply concerned today (October 12) over a tram derailment incident on Shau Kei Wan Main Street East in the early hours, and has instructed the Hong Kong Tramways to conduct a thorough and in-depth investigation into the case.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;A spokesman for the TD said that, following the incident, the TD has immediately held an urgent meeting with the Electrical and Mechanical Services Department and the Tramways to follow up on the matter. While the Police are conducting an investigation into the traffic accident, the Tramways is also investigating the case. Initial findings revealed that human factors cannot be ruled out. The motorman concerned has been suspended from driving duties with immediate effect.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The spokesman added, &quot;We are deeply concerned about the recent incidents involving tram services. Tram safety is key to passenger safety and the overall stability of service performance. To ensure public and operational safety, the TD has instructed the Tramways to conduct a thorough investigation to ascertain the cause of incident, and submit an investigation report within one month, clearly setting out the cause of the accident and practical improvement recommendations to prevent recurrence of similar accidents.&quot;<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The TD will continue to closely monitor the operation of tram services.]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[CCIDA sponsors set up of Hong Kong Pavilion at Frankfurt Book Fair to showcase Hong Kong's creative excellence (with photos)]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/12/P2026101200495.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/12/P2026101200495.htm</link><pubDate>Mon, 12 Oct 2026 21:10:32 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;The Cultural and Creative Industries Development Agency (CCIDA) under the Culture, Sports and Tourism Bureau sponsored the publishing and printing industries to set up the <a href="https://www.bookfairhkpavilion.com/en" target="_blank">Hong Kong Pavilion</a> at the Frankfurt Book Fair (FBF), the world&#39;s largest trade fair for book rights and content, which was held from October 7 to 11 (Frankfurt time). During the five-day exhibition period, the Hong Kong Pavilion attracted over 90 000 visitors from around the world, showcasing Hong Kong&#39;s creative excellence to the international community and serving as a platform for the industries to expand into overseas markets.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;This year&#39;s Hong Kong Pavilion carried the theme &quot;Hong Kong: Our Narrative&quot; and the sub-theme &quot;Cosmic Chapters: Reading the Future&quot;, featuring more than 1 000 pieces of publications and printed works from over 70 Hong Kong publishers and printers.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;Highly popular among visitors was the exhibition staged at the &quot;Creative Corner&quot; thematic zone at which visitors were led to reflect on the meaning of space exploration through a curated selection of relevant publications and printed works.The inspiration came from Dr Lai Ka-ying, Hong Kong&#39;s first payload expert who participated in the successful Shenzhou-23 manned spaceflight mission. The exhibits at the Pavilion presented the key achievements in aerospace development and offered visitors a multifaceted exploration of the spirit of discovery through the lenses of publishing, technology, and the humanities.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;During the exhibition, the Hong Kong Pavilion also organised sharing sessions and networking activities, bringing together the Hong Kong and international industry representatives to exchange experience and insights. The events fostered connections and collaboration opportunities within the industries, while further promoting Hong Kong&#39;s creative content and cultural brands.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;The Hong Kong Pavilion at the FBF is one of the stops of the CCIDA-sponsored &quot;Soaring Creativity &ndash; Hong Kong Pavilion&quot; project. Since the project&#39;s inception in 2011, the project showcased a total of more than 54 000 outstanding Hong Kong exhibits to over seven million visitors from around the world to propel the local publishing and printing industries to go global.<div id="photos"><a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/12/P2026101200495_photo_1340926.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/12/P2026101200495_photo_1340926.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;<a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/12/P2026101200495_photo_1340927.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/12/P2026101200495_photo_1340927.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;</div>]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[CFS finds excessive Bacillus cereus in sample of preserved bean curd]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/12/P2026101200615.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/12/P2026101200615.htm</link><pubDate>Mon, 12 Oct 2026 20:17:00 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The Centre for Food Safety (CFS) of the Food and Environmental Hygiene Department today (October 12) announced that a sample of bottled preserved bean curd was found to contain an excessive amount of Bacillus cereus.&nbsp;<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Details of the batch of the product are as follows:&nbsp;<br />
<br />
Product name: 公和荳品廠 &mdash; 玫瑰腐乳 (No product name in English)<br />
Net weight: 340.5 grams&nbsp;<br />
Sales period of the affected batch: September 24 to October 9, 2026&nbsp;<br />
Place of origin: China<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;A spokesman for the CFS said, &quot;The CFS collected the above-mentioned sample from a retailer in Shum Shui Po for testing under its routine Food Surveillance Programme. The test result showed that the sample contained Bacillus cereus at a level of 140 000 per gram. According to the Microbiological Guidelines for Food, if ready-to-eat food contains Bacillus cereus at a level of more than 100 000 per gram, it is considered unsatisfactory. Members of the public should stop consume the affected batch of product.&quot;&nbsp;<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The spokesman said that the CFS has informed the retailer concerned of the issue, and instructed it to stop sale, remove from shelves and recall the affected batch of the product.&nbsp;<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;A preliminary investigation by the CFS has found that the affected batch of the product comprises only 48 bottles, 26 of which were sold at the retailer between September 24 and October 9. The retailer has voluntarily surrendered the remaining products from the same batch to the CFS for disposal. Members of the public may call its hotline at 4621 7383 for enquiries regarding the recall of the batch of the product concerned.&nbsp;<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&quot;Bacillus cereus is commonly found in the environment. Unhygienic conditions in food processing and storage may give rise to its growth. Consuming food contaminated with excessive Bacillus cereus or its heat-stable toxins may cause gastrointestinal upset such as vomiting and diarrhoea,&quot; the spokesman said.&nbsp;<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The CFS will continue to follow up the incident and take appropriate action. The investigation is ongoing.&nbsp;]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[217 more nomination forms for Election Committee Subsector Ordinary Elections received today]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/12/P2026101200614.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/12/P2026101200614.htm</link><pubDate>Mon, 12 Oct 2026 20:14:00 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The Returning Officers for the various subsectors of the 2026 Election Committee Subsector Ordinary Elections received a total of 206 nomination forms for candidates and 11 nomination forms from designated bodies today (October 12). This brings to 962 the total number of nomination forms for candidates and 23 the total number of nomination forms from designated bodies received since nominations opened on October 7. The nomination period will end on October 20.<br />
