&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Particulars of the nominations received today will be uploaded to the election website (<a href="https://www.elections.gov.hk/ecss2026/eng/index.html" target="_blank">www.elections.gov.hk</a>).]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[RTHK rallies public support for para athletes ahead of Asian Para Games (with photos)]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/12/P2026101200508.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/12/P2026101200508.htm</link><pubDate>Mon, 12 Oct 2026 20:08:00 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Radio Television Hong Kong (RTHK) today (October 12) held the RTHK Broadcast Asian Para Games 2026 com cheering event to announce its broadcast arrangements for the Games and to show support for the athletes who will soon be competing.<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; With the support of the Culture, Sports and Tourism Bureau (CSTB), RTHK will provide live coverage of the Games on RTHK TV31, promoting social inclusion and enabling the public to cheer on athletes from both the national team and the Hong Kong team, while witnessing their determination and outstanding performances on the international stage. The Under Secretary for Culture, Sports and Tourism, Mr Raistlin Lau; the Director of Broadcasting, Ms Angelina Kwan; the President of the Hong Kong Paralympic Committee (HKPC), Mrs Jenny Fung; the Vice President of HKPC and the Chef de Mission of the Hong Kong, China Delegation to the 5th Asian Para Games, Dr Jim Luk; and the Chairperson of the RTHK Board of Advisors, Ms Melissa Pang, were joined by the two athletes who will compete in the upcoming Asian Para Games: Leung Yuk-wing, the world&#39;s top-ranked athlete in the Boccia Men&#39;s Individual BC4 category, and para table tennis athlete Wong Yue-ching, to officiate at the event.<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; The officiating guests, together with So Wa-wai, former Hong Kong Paralympic athletics gold medalist; Lydia Sham, wushu athlete who won Hong Kong&#39;s first gold medal in Women&#39;s Changquan at the 20th Asian Games, and Chan Tik-lun, bronze medalist in Lightweight Men&#39;s Double Sculls, used sign language to convey their best wishes and encouragement to all participating athletes.<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Group-stage competitions in several sports will begin ahead of the official opening, starting on October 16. RTHK TV31 will broadcast &quot;Live coverage of 5th Asian Para Games&quot; from 3pm on October 16 and 17, allowing the public to keep abreast of the group-stage competitions closely. Live coverage of the Opening Ceremony will commence at 4.50pm on October 18. From October 19 to October 24, RTHK TV31 will provide live coverage of featured events daily from 10am to 6pm. Simultaneous sign language interpretation will be available throughout the broadcasts, enabling members of public to fully engage with the competitions and cheer on athletes from the national team and the Hong Kong team. The programmes will also be streamed on the &quot;RTHK On The Go&quot; mobile app and the RTHK YouTube channel. In addition, the public will be able to watch the events at the &quot;Asian Games Focal Sites&quot; located in designated sports centres across all 18 districts, as well as at various shopping malls.<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; RTHK is committed to supporting the development of parasports and promoting social inclusion through its programmes. In recent years, it has provided live coverage of major para sporting events, including the Tokyo Paralympic Games in 2021, the 4th Asian Para Games Hangzhou in 2023, the 2024 Paris Paralympic Games, and the 12th National Games for Persons with Disabilities and the 9th National Special Olympic Games in 2025.<div id="photos"><a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/12/P2026101200508_photo_1340928.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/12/P2026101200508_photo_1340928.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;<a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/12/P2026101200508_photo_1340931.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/12/P2026101200508_photo_1340931.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;<a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/12/P2026101200508_photo_1340929.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/12/P2026101200508_photo_1340929.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;<a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/12/P2026101200508_photo_1340930.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/12/P2026101200508_photo_1340930.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;</div>]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[Hong Kong comic art showcased at Brand Licensing Europe in London (with photos)]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/12/P2026101200555.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/12/P2026101200555.htm</link><pubDate>Mon, 12 Oct 2026 19:53:33 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The Hong Kong Economic and Trade Office in London (London ETO) supported the Hong Kong Arts Centre (HKAC) and the Hong Kong Trade Development Council (HKTDC) in presenting the Hong Kong Comix IPs Showcase at Brand Licensing Europe in London, the United Kingdom, from October 6 to 8 (London Time).<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The exhibition showcased 20 Hong Kong intellectual properties (IPs), highlighting the creativity and talent of Hong Kong artists and creators. Participating artists and IPs included Li Chi-tak, Chocolate Rain<a target="_blank">, </a>Isatisse, Chiya, Felix Ip, Be(Art) Limited, Chill Top Production, Elyk Creation, Going Ape Limited, Little Colour Little Art Limited, Jerry Cho x Jacky Cai, We Are The Pigs, Bonnie Pang, Miloza Ma, Pen So, LLAB Design Ltd, Peg Bar Limited, Penguin Nexus Ltd, TF Business Solutions and Tina Design. An industry reception was held on October 6, bringing together over 80 guests from the local business sector and creative industries.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Speaking at the reception, the Director-General of the London ETO, Miss Fiona Chau, said that the exhibition showcased both the imagination of Hong Kong&#39;s artists and the city&#39;s distinctive cultural spirit. She highlighted how Hong Kong&#39;s unique blend of Eastern and Western influences, tradition and modernity, and local culture and international ideas has contributed to the city&#39;s vibrant creative energy.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Miss Chau also highlighted Hong Kong&#39;s continued investment in culture and the arts, including the West Kowloon Cultural District and world-class institutions such as the Hong Kong Palace Museum and M+. She recognised the HKAC&#39;s contribution to artistic innovation and the nurturing of local talent, and invited guests to visit Hong Kong to experience first-hand the dynamism and diversity of the city&#39;s creative scene.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Established in 1977, the HKAC is a non-profit arts institution dedicated to promoting contemporary comix art, performing arts, visual arts, film, and video arts. Its participation in Brand Licensing Europe, in collaboration with the HKTDC, marks a milestone in showcasing Hong Kong&#39;s creative excellence and diverse artistic talent to international audiences.<div id="photos"><a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/12/P2026101200555_photo_1340943.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/12/P2026101200555_photo_1340943.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;<a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/12/P2026101200555_photo_1340944.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/12/P2026101200555_photo_1340944.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;<a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/12/P2026101200555_photo_1340945.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/12/P2026101200555_photo_1340945.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;<a href="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/12/P2026101200555_photo_1340946.jpg" target="_blank"><img src="https://gia.info.gov.hk/general/202610/12/P2026101200555_photo_1340946.jpg" style="max-width:320px;max-height:320px" alt="Photo" border="1" /></a>&nbsp;&nbsp;</div>]]></description></item><item><title><![CDATA[DH announces latest situation regarding Legionnaires' disease cases]]></title><guid>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/12/P2026101200569.htm</guid><link>https://www.info.gov.hk/gia/general/202610/12/P2026101200569.htm</link><pubDate>Mon, 12 Oct 2026 18:55:00 +0800</pubDate><description><![CDATA[&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The Centre for Health Protection (CHP) of the Department of Health (DH) today (October 12) reported the latest number of Legionnaires&#39; disease (LD) cases, and reminded the public of the importance of using and maintaining properly designed man-made water systems, adding that susceptible groups should strictly observe relevant precautions.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; From October 4 to October 10, the CHP recorded four LD cases, involving four males aged between 69 and 77. All of them had underlying illness. Details are as follows:<br />
&nbsp;<br />
Locally acquired cases<br />
---------------------------<br />
&nbsp;
<ul>
	<li>a 76-year-old male living in Sha Tin District;</li>
	<li>a&nbsp;76-year-old male living in Kwun Tong District; and</li>
	<li>a&nbsp;77-year-old male living in Kwai Tsing District.</li>
</ul>
<br />
&nbsp;Case with place of infection to be determined<br />
------------------------------------------------------<br />
&nbsp;
<ul>
	<li>a&nbsp;69-year-old male living in Kowloon City District. He travelled to the Chinese Mainland during the incubation period. Further investigation is needed to determine whether this is an imported or community-acquired case.</li>
</ul>
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;​The CHP is conducting epidemiological investigations to identify the potential sources of infection and high-risk exposure. An initial investigation revealed that the aforesaid cases are sporadic cases. No epidemiological linkages have been established between these cases and other confirmed cases previously recorded in Hong Kong.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; As of October 10, 157 LD cases had been recorded this year. In 2025 and 2024, there were 167 and 135 LD cases respectively.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Men, people aged over 50, smokers, alcoholics and persons with weakened immunity are more susceptible to LD. Some situations may also increase the risk of infection, including poor maintenance of water systems; living in areas with old water systems, cooling towers or fountains; using electric water heaters, whirlpool spas or hot water spring spas; and recent stays in hotels or ships.<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Legionellae are found in various environmental settings and grow well in warm water (20 to 45 degrees Celsius). They can be found in aqueous environments such as water tanks, hot and cold water systems, cooling towers, whirlpool spas, water fountains, humidifiers and home respiratory devices that support breathing. People may become infected when they breathe in contaminated droplets (aerosols) and mist generated by artificial water systems, or when handling garden soil, compost and potting mixes.<br />
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Immunocompromised persons should:<br />
&nbsp;
<ul>
	<li>Use sterile or boiled water for drinking, tooth brushing and mouth rinsing;</li>
	<li>Avoid using humidifiers, or other mist- or aerosol-generating devices; and</li>
	<li>If using humidifiers, or other mist- or aerosol-generating devices, fill the water tank with only sterile or cooled freshly boiled water, and not water directly from the tap. Also, clean and maintain humidifiers/devices regularly according to manufacturers&#39; instructions. Never leave stagnant water in a humidifier/device. Empty the water tank, wipe all surfaces dry, and change the water daily.</li>
</ul>
<br />
<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;​The public should observe the health advice below:<br />
&nbsp;
<ul>
	<li>Observe personal hygiene;</li>
	<li>Do not smoke and avoid alcohol consumption;</li>
	<li>Strainers in water taps and shower heads should be inspected, cleaned, descaled and disinfected regularly or at a frequency recommended by the manufacturer;</li>
	<li>If a fresh-water plumbing system is properly maintained, it is not necessary to install domestic water filters. Use of water filters is not encouraged as clogging occurs easily, which can promote growth of micro-organisms. In case water filters are used, the pore size should be 0.2 micrometres (&micro;m) or less and the filter needs to be changed periodically according to the manufacturer&#39;s recommendations;</li>
	<li>Drain and clean water tanks of buildings at least quarterly;</li>
	<li>Drain or purge for at least one minute infrequently used water outlets (e.g. water taps, shower heads and hot water outlets) and stagnant points of the pipework weekly or before use;</li>
	<li>Seek and follow doctors&#39; professional advice regarding the use and maintenance of home respiratory devices and use only sterile water (not distilled or tap water) to clean and fill the reservoir. Clean and maintain the device regularly according to the manufacturer&#39;s instructions. After cleaning/disinfection, rinse the device with sterile water, cooled freshly boiled water or water filtered with 0.2 &micro;m filters. Never leave stagnant water in the device. Empty the water tank, keep all surfaces dry, and change the water daily; and</li>
	<li>When handling garden soil, compost and potting mixes:</li>
</ul>

<ol>
	<li>Wear gloves and a face mask;</li>
	<li>Water gardens and compost gently using low pressure;</li>
	<li>Open composted potting mixes slowly and make sure the opening is directed away from the face;</li>
	<li>Wet the soil to reduce dust when potting plants; and</li>
	<li>Avoid working in poorly ventilated places such as enclosed greenhouses.</li>
</ol>
&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The public may visit the CHP&#39;s&nbsp;<a href="https://www.chp.gov.hk/en/view_content/24307.html" target="_blank">LD page</a>, the&nbsp;<a href="https://www.emsd.gov.hk/en/supporting_government_initiatives/legionnaires_disease/publications/codes_of_practice/index.html" target="_blank">Code of Practice for Prevention of LD</a>&nbsp;and the&nbsp;<a href="https://www.emsd.gov.hk/en/supporting_government_initiatives/legionnaires_disease/publications/general/index.html" target="_blank">Housekeeping Guidelines for Cold and Hot Water Systems for Building Management</a>&nbsp;of the Prevention of LD Committee, and the CHP&#39;s&nbsp;<a href="https://www.chp.gov.hk/en/view_content/42729.html" target="_blank">risk-based strategy</a>&nbsp;for prevention and control of LD.<br />
<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;Additionally, as smokers and alcoholics are more susceptible to LD, the Government urges members of the public to quit smoking and avoid drinking. Members of the public may call the DH Integrated Smoking Cessation Hotline at 1833 183, which is answered by registered nurses who provide professional counselling and referrals to a wide range of free smoking cessation services. The public may also visit the smoking cessation thematic website (<a href="https://www.livetobaccofree.hk/en/index.html" target="_blank">www.livetobaccofree.hk</a>) for more information on smoking cessation. The DH has also developed and promulgated the Alcohol Screening and Brief Intervention tool to primary healthcare service providers to facilitate the early identification and management of at-risk drinkers. For information on alcohol-related harm, please visit the DH&#39;s&nbsp;<a href="https://www.change4health.gov.hk/en/alcohol_aware/index.html" target="_blank">Change for Health website</a>.]]></description></item></channel></rss>